    {
      "id": "web_of_science_1662",
      "title": "Predicting the Fundamental Frequency of Steel-CLT Floor Systems",
      "authors": "Forster, Justice and Phillips, Adam R. and Fischer, Erica and Sarlo, Rodrigo",
      "affiliations": "Virginia Polytechnic Institute \\& State University; Oregon State University",
      "countries": [],
      "journal": "STRUCTURES CONGRESS 2025",
      "year": 2025,
      "score": 35,
      "abstract": "Hybrid construction allows for engineers to take advantage of the benefits of different materials to develop more sustainable and robust structures. One example of hybrid construction is steel-cross laminated timber (CLT) floors, which are becoming a popular option due to their lightweight and favorable environmental sustainability. However, the steel -CLT floor system can be susceptible to floor vibrations that exceed serviceability limit states from walking footfalls. The goal of this study was to determine if AISC's Design Guide 11 could be modified to predict the fundamental frequency of steel-CLT floor systems and to validate the method utilizing heel-drop acceleration test results from a case-study building. The frequencies calculated from the proposed analytical method were compared to the fundamental frequency obtained from the heel-drop tests and were shown to match within approximately 2\\% to -4\\% error. While this validation was for only one case-study building, it demonstrates that the developed analytical method has potential.",
      "keywords": [],
      "studyType": "Proceedings Paper",
      "quality": null,
      "doi": null,
