    {
      "id": "web_of_science_1671",
      "title": "Comparative study of connection performance for cross-laminated timber-glulam composite floor systems",
      "authors": "Zhang, Xiaoyue and Zhang, Sigong and Niu, Binjie and Yang, Huifeng",
      "affiliations": "Chongqing University; Newcastle University - UK; Nanjing Tech University",
      "countries": [],
      "journal": "STRUCTURES",
      "year": 2025,
      "score": 36,
      "abstract": "Long-span timber floor systems can be effectively achieved by integrating mass timber panels with linear timber elements to form composite action. Their flexural performance is largely governed by the mechanical behaviour of the connectors. This study investigated the structural performance of various connection systems used in crosslaminated timber (CLT)-glulam composite floors. A series of push-out tests were conducted on seven groups of specimens, each featuring different shear connector types, including inclined and vertical self-tapping screws, steel tubes, and perforated steel plates. Key performance parameters-shear strength, initial stiffness, and ductility-were experimentally evaluated and compared across all configurations. In addition, the test results were benchmarked against analytical predictions to assess the accuracy and applicability of simplified design models. Among the systems tested, the combination of steel tubes and screws significantly improved both stiffness and load-carrying capacity, while also demonstrating favourable post-peak behaviour (i.e., enhanced ductility). This hybrid connector system offers a cost-effective solution for composite floor systems. Furthermore, it was found that the ductility factors defined in EN 12512 do not adequately capture the ductile behaviour of different connection systems. In contrast, the use of absolute ductility-calculated as the difference between the ultimate slip and the slip at yield-provided a more accurate representation of system behaviour.",
      "keywords": [
        "Cross-laminated timber",
        "Glulam",
        "Self-tapping screws",
        "Push-out tests",
        "Ductility factors"
      ],
      "studyType": "Article",
      "quality": null,
      "doi": "10.1016/j.istruc.2025.110083",
