    {
      "id": "web_of_science_1846",
      "title": "Experimental and numerical studies on seismic performance of multi-story glulam frame-CLT core wall structure with buckling-restrained braces",
      "authors": "Zhang, Baozhuang and Song, Xiaobin and Luo, Lie and Chen, Yu",
      "affiliations": "Tongji University; Tongji University",
      "countries": [],
      "journal": "JOURNAL OF BUILDING ENGINEERING",
      "year": 2025,
      "score": 38,
      "abstract": "This paper presents the experimental testing and numerical modelling of the seismic performance of a novel multi-story glulam frame-cross laminated timber (CLT) core wall structure with the buckling-restrained braces (BRBs). A 1/2 scaled five-story glulam frame-CLT core wall structure with the BRBs was designed and fabricated. The shaking table tests were conducted on the new hybrid structural model under six different seismic hazard levels. The failure modes, seismic responses, collaborative working mechanism, working condition and contribution of the BRBs, and inter-story shear force of the structural model were analyzed. The test results showed that the primary failure modes of the structural model exhibited the cracking of the CLT core wall, the uplift and expansion at the vertical joints, buckling and fracture of anti-pull components, and the partial pull-out of the self-tapping screws of the BRBs, while no visible damage occurred to the glulam frame. The damping ratio of the structural model can reach up to 12.5 \\%, indicating good energy dissipation capacity. The acceleration amplification factor of each floor ranged from 1.0 to 5.0, and the acceleration amplification factor of the first floor was significantly higher than that of upper floors. The BRBs significantly contributed to the lateral stiffness of the existing glulam frame-CLT core wall structures under major earthquakes. The new hybrid structure performed well after severe earthquake excitation with PGA of 1.24 g, with the maximum inter-story drift ratio not exceeding the code-specified limit of 1/50 rad for the elasto-plastic inter-story drift ratio, meeting the seismic fortification goal of preventing the collapse in major earthquake. In addition, a non-linear numerical model of the new hybrid structure was established and verified. Parametric analyses were carried out. The influence of the number of structural floors and the crosssectional sizes of the braces on the maximum displacement and inter-story drift ratio of the top story was revealed. It is found that the predictions of the numerical model were in good agreement with the shaking table test results, satisfying the requirements of engineering accuracy. This research can offer valuable insights for the design and engineering applications of multi-story and high-rise modern timber structures.",
      "keywords": [
        "Multi-story timber structure",
        "Glulam frame",
        "CLT core wall",
        "Buckling-restrained brace",
        "Seismic performance",
        "Shaking table test",
        "Numerical simulation"
      ],
      "studyType": "Article",
      "quality": null,
      "doi": "10.1016/j.jobe.2025.114362",
      "language": "English",
      "source": "Web of Science",
