    {
      "id": "web_of_science_1863",
      "title": "Environmental benefits of using an all-wood floor system in midrise office construction: an LCA based comparative case study in the Southeastern United States",
      "authors": "Ijeoma, Muzan Williams and Ou, Sovanroth and Nabati, Amin and Chen, Hao and Stoner, Michael and Layton, Patricia and Albright, Dustin and Ross, Brandon and Pang, Weichiang and Carbajales-Dale, Michael",
      "affiliations": "Clemson University; Clemson University; Clemson University",
      "countries": [],
      "journal": "ENVIRONMENTAL RESEARCH COMMUNICATIONS",
      "year": 2025,
      "score": 39,
      "abstract": "This study presents a cradle-to-grave whole-building life cycle assessment (WBLCA) of two functionally equivalent mid-rise commercial office buildings in Atlanta, Georgia: one constructed using mass timber with an all-wood composite floor system (CLT and GLT), and the other using conventional steel and reinforced concrete. Using Tally (R), a Revit-integrated LCA tool, environmental impacts were evaluated across eight categories: global warming, acidification, eutrophication, smog formation, ozone depletion, total primary energy, renewable and non-renewable energy use, and normalized to 1 m2 of floor area over a 50-year service life. Results showed that the mass timber building required 25\\% less material mass and achieved a 4.5\\% reduction in global warming potential (GWP) (2801.12 kg CO2-eq/m2 versus 2975.85 kg CO2-eq/m2 for the steel-concrete system) and 27.9\\% more renewable energy use. However, timber's carbon advantage was diminished by conservative end-of-life modeling, assuming 63.5\\% wood landfilling and 98\\% steel recycling, which significantly influenced net GWP through Module D credits. Additionally, the mass timber building stored 857,551 tCO2-eq in wood components (long-term biogenic carbon storage). The timber structure also exhibited higher eutrophication and acidification impacts, primarily due to emissions from adhesives and chemical treatments. Contribution analysis showed that finishes and non-structural components contributed disproportionately to overall impacts in both systems, illustrating the limitations of focusing solely on structural materials in building LCA. The findings emphasize the need for integrated material strategies, circular end-of-life planning, and regionally grounded modeling to fully realize the environmental potential of wood-based construction.",
      "keywords": [
        "cross laminated timber",
        "whole building life cycle assessment",
        "US Southeast",
        "wood composite floor system",
        "biogenic carbon",
        "mid-rise office buildings"
      ],
      "studyType": "Article",
      "quality": null,
      "doi": "10.1088/2515-7620/ae1f6a",
