    {
      "id": "scopus_346",
      "title": "3D CHARACTERIZATION OF A 10 STORY BUILDING SPECIMEN USING TERRESTRIAL LIDAR/UAV-BASED PHOTOGRAMMETRY",
      "authors": "Lo, Eric K. and Driscoll, John W. and Lozano Bravo, H. and Norton, Tanner J. and Sorosh, Shokrullah and Hutchinson, Tara C. and Pei, Shiling and Ryan, Keri L. and Driscoll, Neal William and Kuester, Falko",
      "affiliations": "University of California, San Diego, La Jolla, CA, United States; Colorado School of Mines, Golden, CO, United States; University of Nevada, Reno, Reno, NV, United States",
      "countries": [
        "United States"
      ],
      "journal": "World Conference on Earthquake Engineering proceedings",
      "year": 2024,
      "score": 3,
      "abstract": "Earthquake motions can cause residual changes in the 3D geometry of structures. In shake table experiments, simulated earthquake motions provide an opportunity to demonstrate and evaluate strategies for acquiring and measuring these changes. Prior approaches include the use of terrestrial lidar scanning and photogrammetry, each of which has been applied to capture pre/post event 3D states and enable change detection. In this paper, a methodology for applying UAV-based photogrammetry and terrestrial lidar scanning to create a comprehensive 3D digital twin with subcentimeter resolution to detect structural and nonstructural changes induced by earthquake motions is presented. These methods were applied in the landmark NHERI TallWood full-scale (10-story) shake table test specimen, where the building height presented unique challenges for comprehensive documentation. Notably, this unique cross laminated timber building was captured in its baseline as-built condition before testing and following a suite of more than 100 simulated earthquakes. The terrestrial lidar scanning provided high resolution 3D data of the interior as well as the lower floors of the exterior, while UAV-based photogrammetry allowed for high resolution 3D data collection of the exterior of the structure, which was otherwise inaccessible due to building height. A comparison of the 3D data from each technique is presented and the datasets are used to evaluate global and local changes in the specimen, as well as provide precise measurements for over 700 pieces of instrumentation. These preliminary results show the utility of the proposed 3D data collection methodology, which can be leveraged for more complex analyses in future work. © 2024, International Association for Earthquake Engineering. All rights reserved.",
      "keywords": [
        "Earthquake effects",
        "Tall buildings",
        "Wooden buildings",
        "Wooden construction",
        "3D characterization",
        "3D data",
        "3D geometry",
        "Building height",
        "Data collection",
        "Earthquake motion",
        "High resolution",
        "Lidar scanning",
        "Shake table experiments",
        "Terrestrial lidars",
        "Earthquake engineering"
      ],
