    {
      "id": "scopus_1294",
      "title": "Sound insulation in lightweight constructions - Austrian experiences with solid wood panel buildings",
      "authors": "Ferk, Heinz J.",
      "affiliations": "Technische Universitat Graz, Laboratory for Building Physics, Graz, Styria, Austria",
      "countries": [
        "Austria"
      ],
      "journal": "Institute of Noise Control Engineering of the USA - 35th International Congress and Exposition on Noise Control Engineering, INTER-NOISE 2006",
      "year": 2006,
      "score": 3,
      "abstract": "The using of cross laminated solid wood panels in timber structures as load bearing plates and shear panels also for multi-storey wooden buildings is increasing, especially in Austria and some other European countries. The advantage of this lightweight material is sustainability, a good possibility of prefabrication of building elements and parts, good stability for static and also for earthquake loading and a good behavior concerning construction physics loadings, but it is necessary to take care to sound insulation. Cross laminated timber panels have orthotropic material characteristic values, dependent on thickness and number of layers. Cause of it is relative light weight and relatively high stiffness; conventional constructions with this material show a high flanking transmission, as well as a low airborne and impact sound insulation. In this paper the development results for some suitable constructions are described. Some realized projects, their constructions and results of airborne and impact sound measurements are presented. The results show, that it is possible to build also multi-storey apartment buildings with exacting constructions, for example continuous slabs and prefabricated single leaf partitions with high sound insulation, as well as for example prefabricated cellular modules for hotels and office buildings.",
