    {
      "id": "scopus_1138",
      "title": "Design and construction of a 53-meter-tall timber building at the University of British Columbia",
      "authors": "Poirier, Erik A. and Moudgil, Manu and Fallahi, Azadeh and Staub-French, Sheryl and Tannert, Thomas",
      "affiliations": "The University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC, Canada",
      "countries": [
        "Canada"
      ],
      "journal": "WCTE 2016 - World Conference on Timber Engineering",
      "year": 2016,
      "score": 6,
      "abstract": "The paper discusses the challenges during the design of a tall timber building: The University of British Columbia Brock Commons Phase I project, known as UBC Tall Wood Building (TWB). Once completed, TWB will be the tallest mass-timber building in the world standing at 18 stories with a height of 53 m. The discussion includes the technical, financial, supply chain and regulatory challenges, as well as the social, economic and environmental benefits that result from the building. The TWB will house 404 student beds laid out in single and quad occupancy rooms. The structural system is viewed as being very simple by the project stakeholders, simplicity and ease of erection being one of the key drivers for this project. The structural system is designed as a hybrid configuration: the foundations, ground floor and first level, as well as the building cores, are cast-in-place concrete. Levels 2 to 18 are mass-timber columns (standard Glulam with some parallel strand lumber columns on the lower levels for increased compression strength) and floors made of cross-laminated timber panels and a non-structural concrete topping.",
      "keywords": [
        "Building monitoring",
        "Hybrid structure",
        "Site specific regulations",
        "Tall wood construction"
      ],
      "studyType": "Conference paper",
