    {
      "id": "scopus_348",
      "title": "UNDERSTANDING THE DISSIPATION PROVIDED BY TIMBER-TO-TIMBER SCREW CONNECTIONS USING X-RAY CT",
      "authors": "De Santis, Yuri and Pasca, Dag Pasquale and Aloisio, Angelo and Fragiacomo, Massimo",
      "affiliations": "Università degli Studi dell'Aquila, Department of Civil, L'Aquila, AQ, Italy; Treteknisk, Oslo, Oslo, Norway",
      "countries": [
        "Italy",
        "Norway"
      ],
      "journal": "World Conference on Earthquake Engineering proceedings",
      "year": 2024,
      "score": 9,
      "abstract": "Inclined screws represent a typical connection solution for newly built timber structures and for the retrofitting of existing buildings. Inclined screws connections are well suited for use in composite floors and beams providing high capacity and stiffness due to the combined axial-shear resisting mechanisms. Frequently, inclined screws are used to connect cross-laminated timber plate elements to existing floor joist leaving the existing planking as an intermediate layer having negligible structural role. This paper investigates the effect of layer orientation, friction, and interlayers on the slip modulus, capacity and energy dissipation of timber-to-timber connections with inclined screws. First, the authors carried out push-out tests considering six structural configurations. Then, through X-ray computed tomography (CT), they assessed the deformed configuration of the screws to isolate the screw and timber contributions to the total dissipated energy. The deformed shape of the connectors provides an estimate of the connection bending deformation assuming a rigid-plastic stress-strain constitutive for steel. By subtraction, the authors evaluated the role of timber embedment and steel deformation in the overall dissipative capacity in all the tested configurations. The experimental results revealed that most dissipated energy comes from the screw without interlayers, while the remaining can be attributed to timber plasticization. Conversely, the presence of the interlayer significantly unbalances such contribution, considerably reducing the role of timber plasticization. © 2024, International Association for Earthquake Engineering. All rights reserved.",
      "keywords": [
        "Connectors (structural)",
        "Earthquakes",
        "Mortar",
        "Wooden buildings",
        "Wooden floors",
        "Axial shear",
        "Composite beam",
        "Composite floors",
        "Dissipated energy",
        "High capacity",
        "High stiffness",
        "High-capacity",
        "Screw connections",
        "Timber structures",
        "X-ray computed tomography",
        "Earthquake engineering"
      ],
      "studyType": "Book chapter",
      "quality": null,
      "doi": null,
      "language": "English",
