    {
      "id": "scopus_1274",
      "title": "Timber upgrade of structures on seismically active areas",
      "authors": "Sustersić, Iztok and Dujič, Bruno and Gostič, Samo",
      "affiliations": "CBD d.o.o., Celje, Slovenia; GIZRMK Do.o., Ljubljana, Slovenia",
      "countries": [
        "Slovenia"
      ],
      "journal": "11th World Conference on Timber Engineering 2010, WCTE 2010",
      "year": 2010,
      "score": 12,
      "abstract": "The paper presents an example of seismic design approach, when a heavy and stiff existing structure from masonry bricks with reinforced concrete parts is planned to be upgraded with a light and flexible timber structure. The first part of the paper deals with the general principles used when upgrading existing structures. There is an emphasis on the shear analysis of masonry structures. There is also a short word on the analysis of existing foundations. The second part of the paper deals with an upgrade of a 30 year old hotel with a ground floor and 3 upper floors. 3 additional floors were added to the existing structure built from silicate masonry with RC horizontal and vertical ties and RC slabs. The upgraded floors are made from cross laminated timber (Xlam) and the floor finalisation is made from wood based materials to keep down the weight. The hotel stands in Slovenia in a very seismically active zone, where peak ground acceleration can reach 0,225 g for earthquakes with a return period of 475 years.",
      "keywords": [
        "Seismic analysis",
        "Timber construction",
        "Upgrade"
      ],
      "studyType": "Conference paper",
