    {
      "id": "scopus_943",
      "title": "Timber t-section beams: From pre-tensioning to self-tensioning",
      "authors": "Estévez-Cimadevila, Javier and Otero-Chans, Dolores and Martín-Gutiérrez, Emilio and Suárez-Riestra, Félix and Vázquez-Rodríguez, José Antonio",
      "affiliations": "Universidade da Coruña, Group of Architectonic Structures, A Coruna, A Coruña, Spain",
      "countries": [
        "Spain"
      ],
      "journal": "Structures and Architecture: Bridging the Gap and Crossing Borders - Proceedings of the 4th International Conference on Structures and Architecture, ICSA 2019",
      "year": 2019,
      "score": 16,
      "abstract": "A comparative bending behaviour analysis of timber T-section beams, in unten-sioned, pre-tensioned and self-tensioned conditions was carried out. Their T-section was composed of a glulam Picea abies or Oak web and an upper CLT flange. Twenty-four 9 m span specimens were tested until failure. Self-tensioning used a patented system (SsS©) based on a device composed of articulated rods that is anchored to the supports of the beams and connected to a non-adherent tendon. Although pre-tensioning the T-sections significantly increases their load-bearing capacity, this is not significant because the dimensions of simply supported beams are not usually determined by their ultimate limit state but by their serviceability limit state. Results show that pre-tensioning slightly varied stiffness because of the increased compression area. The results of self-tensioning specimens show a significant improvement in their behavior at the serviceability limit state. Consequently the proposed system is considered to be especially suitable for use with long-span wooden structural floors. © 2019 Taylor & Francis Group, London.",
      "keywords": [
        "Timber",
        "Wooden floors",
        "Bending behaviour",
        "Load-bearing capacity",
        "Long span",
        "Picea abies",
        "Serviceability limit state",
        "Simply supported beams",
        "T sections",
        "Ultimate limit state",
        "Architecture"
      ],
