    {
      "id": "scopus_1226",
      "title": "Is cross-laminated timber suitab le for building structures to thirty levels?",
      "authors": "Chapman, John B.",
      "affiliations": "University of Auckland, School of Architecture and Planning, Auckland, AUK, New Zealand",
      "countries": [
        "New Zealand"
      ],
      "journal": "Structures and Architecture: New concepts, applications and challenges",
      "year": 2013,
      "score": 19,
      "abstract": "This paper c onsiders if cross-lam inated timber construction, whic h is comm only referred to as CLT, is suitable to support commer cial buildings to thirty levels. It proposes a building system with CLT panels for a central tube core, colu mns and floor beams. The panels are arranged with the aims of achieving structural efficiency and minimal material wastage. The main structural element is a 96m high by 15.2m diameter central tube core. The core is made up of ninety-six full size panels that are interconnect ed to form one integrated element. The shear forces between the panels in the central core are transferred by 'shear teeth' which are hopefully simple to construct and unlikely to lead to significant joint slip. An analy sis of the structure is reported and the paper concludes that the proposed sy stem with CLT elements may be suitable for buildings to thirty levels. © 2013 by Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.",
      "keywords": [],
      "studyType": "Book chapter",
      "quality": null,
      "doi": null,
      "language": "English",
      "source": "Scopus",
      "numString": 2,
      "isDuplicate": false,
