    {
      "id": "scopus_2022",
      "title": "Predictive Design of a Multilayered Laminate Shell Based on AI and 1st-Order Classical Laminate Theory",
      "authors": "Helaili, S. and Rezgui, Taysir and Najar, Fehmi and Chafra, Moez",
      "affiliations": "University of Carthage, Ecole Polytechnique de Tunisie, La Marsa, Tunis, Tunisia; Institut Supérieur des Technologies de l’Environnement de l’Urbanisme et du Bâtiment, Tunis, Tunis, Tunisia; University of Carthage, Ecole Nationale d'Ingénieurs de Bizerte, Bizerte, Bizerte, Tunisia; Prince Sattam Bin Abdulaziz University, Department of Mechanical Engineering, Al Kharj, Riyad, Saudi Arabia; Université de Tunis El Manar, Tunis, Tunis, Tunisia; Université de Tunis El Manar, Ecole Nationale d'Ingénieurs de Tunis, Matériaux, Tunis, Tunis, Tunisia",
      "countries": [
        "Tunisia",
        "Saudi Arabia"
      ],
      "journal": "Lecture Notes in Mechanical Engineering",
      "year": 2024,
      "score": 19,
      "abstract": "The design of multilayered laminate shells and plates requires a very specific approach. This is a multistage approach that begins with a pre-design work to select the number of layers, the layer thickness, and the layer reinforcement type depending on the application and based on previous works. After that, the predesign is subject to verification, and the structural element made of multilayered laminates is checked. The loads, the load combinations, and the structural calculation make it possible to determine the loads to which the structural element would be subjected: the bending moments, the shear forces, and the concentrated forces. The shells are structural elements mainly subject to bending moments, but their dimensioning also depends on the nature of the supports: fixed, articulated, or a combination of them. In this paper, a pre-design approach using artificial intelligence has been developed. A database of laminated shells was generated based on input parameters. Criteria related to resistance and deformation made it possible to calculate an objective function to determine the optimal design. This same base was used to teach an artificial intelligence algorithm to predict the optimal design. The algorithm has been tested, and the results are predictive to some degree of accuracy. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2024.",
      "keywords": [
        "AI",
        "CLT theory",
        "Multilayered laminate"
      ],
      "studyType": "Conference paper",
      "quality": null,
      "doi": "10.1007/978-3-031-70428-4_16",
      "language": "English",
      "source": "Scopus",
      "numString": 5,
      "isDuplicate": false,
      "duplicateOf": null,
