    {
      "id": "scopus_1167",
      "title": "Predicting the impact and airborne sound insulation performance of monolithic wood and concrete structures using laboratory data",
      "authors": "Byrick, Wilson R. and Golden, Matthew V.",
      "affiliations": "Pliteq Inc., Toronto, ON, Canada",
      "countries": [
        "Canada"
      ],
      "journal": "ICSV 2016 - 23rd International Congress on Sound and Vibration: From Ancient to Modern Acoustics",
      "year": 2016,
      "score": 21,
      "abstract": "When attempting to design floor-ceiling assemblies that achieve international building code (IBC) minimums of STC 50 and IIC 50 based on laboratory testing to ASTM E90 and ASTM E492 respectively, it is important to be able to predict one-third octave band deficiencies based on the type of structure. This paper examines four types of floor-ceiling structure, Cross-laminated timber (CLT), Heavy Timber, concrete slab and hollow-core concrete slab. By comparing one-third octave band data from the four types of structure we can select different floor or ceiling side isolation techniques in order to meet IBC minimum code. Comparative plots from different labs are presented to determine the reproducibility of data from lab to lab. Multiple identical assemblies are compared in the same lab to assess the repeatability with the listed structures. By predicting one-third octave band deficiencies and resonant frequency trends in the four different structures, we can apply various resilient toppings and mounted ceilings to meet the IBC minimum codes and improve overall tenant satisfaction.",
      "keywords": [
        "Building codes",
        "Ceilings",
