      "id": "scopus_907",
      "title": "Mechanical analysis of photovoltaic panels with various boundary condition",
      "authors": "Li, Yongxue and Xie, Lingzhi and Zhang, Tengyuan and Wu, Yupeng and Sun, Yanyi and Ni, Zhichun and Zhang, Jingquan and He, Bo",
      "affiliations": "Sichuan University, College of Architecture and Environment, Chengdu, Sichuan, China; Sichuan University, Institute of New Energy and Low-Carbon Technology, Chengdu, Sichuan, China; University of Nottingham, Department of Architecture and Built Environment, Nottingham, Nottinghamshire, United Kingdom; Suzhou Talesun Solar Technologies Co., Ltd., Changshu, China; Sichuan University, College of Materials Science and Engineering, Chengdu, Sichuan, China",
      "countries": [
        "China",
        "United Kingdom"
      ],
      "journal": "IOP Conference Series: Materials Science and Engineering",
      "year": 2019,
      "score": 22,
      "abstract": "The photovoltaic (PV) panels currently existed on market are a kind of laminated plate structure, which is composed of two stiff glass skins and a soft interlayer. Some of those panels are installed on the buildings and integrated as the components of the structures, such as wall and roof. In different locations, the installations of the panels are different and the boundary conditions are not simply supported any more. The generating electricity requirement of the panels needs the mechanic behaviour of them is stable with any boundary condition. In this paper the bending behaviour of PV panels with various boundary conditions is analysed and the influence of boundary condition is studied carefully. The Kirchhoff theory which is one of the classical lamination theory (CLT) is adopted to build governing equations of photovoltaic panels under static force. A Rayleigh-Rita method is modified to solve the governing equations and calculate the static deformation and stress. Different boundary condition requires different assumptions of the deflection function and a modified general function is developed to solve that problem. A theoretical solution is derived out and used to do the numerical calculation. A bending experiment of PV panel with two opposite edges simply supported and the other two free is used to verify the correctness and accuracy of the proposed solution. Finally, the influence of different boundary condition is stated by comparing the numerical results and some guides for the PV panel installation are proposed. © Published under licence by IOP Publishing Ltd.",
      "keywords": [
        "bending experiment",
        "classical lamination theory",
        "modified deflection function",
        "photovoltaic panel",
        "Rayleigh-Rita method",
        "various boundary condition"
      ],
      "studyType": "Conference paper",
      "quality": null,
      "doi": "10.1088/1757-899X/556/1/012048",
