    {
      "id": "scopus_1022",
      "title": "Diaphragmatic behaviour of hybrid cross-laminated timber steel floors",
      "authors": "Loss, Cristiano and Gobbi, Filippo and Tannert, Thomas",
      "affiliations": "University of Northern British Columbia, Wood Engineering, Prince George, BC, Canada; Università di Trento, Trento, TN, Italy",
      "countries": [
        "Canada",
        "Italy"
      ],
      "journal": "WCTE 2018 - World Conference on Timber Engineering",
      "year": 2018,
      "score": 22,
      "abstract": "The diaphragmatic behaviour of floors represents one important requirement for earthquake resistant buildings since diaphragms connect the lateral load resisting systems at each floor level and transfer the seismic forces to them as a function of their in-plane stiffness. This paper presents an innovative hybrid timber-steel solution for floor diaphragms developed by coupling cross-laminated timber panels with cold-formed custom-shaped steel beams. The floor consists of prefabricated repeatable units which are fastened on-site using pre-loaded bolts and self-tapping screws, thus ensuring a fast and efficient installation. An experimentally validated numerical model is used to evaluate the influence of the; i) in-plane floor stiffness; ii) aspect ratio and shape of the building plan; and iii) relative stiffness and disposition of the shear walls, on the load distribution to the shear walls. The load transfer into walls and lateral deformation of the construction system primarily depend on the adopted layouts of shear walls, and for most cases an in-plane stiffness of floors two times larger than that of walls is recommended. © WCTE 2018 Committee.",
      "keywords": [
        "Hybrid floors",
        "Load path",
        "Modular members",
        "Numerical analyses",
        "Prefabrication",
        "Sensitivity analysis"
      ],
      "studyType": "Conference paper",
      "quality": null,
      "doi": null,
