    {
      "id": "scopus_2027",
      "title": "Free Vibration Analysis of Fiber Reinforced Composite Beam for Fixed-Fixed Boundary Condition",
      "authors": "Ammineni, Syam Prasad and Madhavi, Matlapudi and Nandini, Ch",
      "affiliations": "Maturi Venkata Subba Rao Engineering College, Department of Mechanical Engineering, Hyderabad, TS, India",
      "countries": [
        "India"
      ],
      "journal": "AIP Conference Proceedings",
      "year": 2023,
      "score": 26,
      "abstract": "The free vibrations of laminated composite beam with varied section modulus have been examined with a new proposed theoretical model developed using lamination theory of composite layers and classical beam theories. As numerical techniques approach using ANSYS has limitation in solving composite layered beams due to constraint in the existing BEAM (non-layered element) and SHELL (Over constrained for beam) elements. Efforts are made to develop a theoretical model in a blend mode using the characteristics of BEAM and layered SHELL structures. The proposed theories named as Layered Beam Element-Classical Lamination Theory (LBE-CLT) and Layered Beam Element-First Order Shear Deformation Theory (LBE-FSDT) is applicable to the practically feasible fiber reinforced composite layered beam structures. The governing equations are solved to predict natural frequencies and related mode shapes of a composite beam with varied section modulus viz., rectangle, hollow rectangle, and hollow circle under fixed-fixed elastic conditions. MATLAB code is developed to obtain theoretical results and later compared to Numerical Techniques. The results indicate that composite rectangular beams have high flexural strength and stiffness than steel beams of similar cross-sections. It has been observed that angle of plies has significant impact on dynamic parameters of the beam. The proposed theories are accurate and also efficient in predicting the natural frequencies and mode shapes and thus can be utilized for aerospace and automobile applications. © 2023 American Institute of Physics Inc.. All rights reserved.",
      "keywords": [
        "CLT",
        "FSDT",
        "LBE",
        "MATLAB",
        "NaturalFrequencies"
      ],
      "studyType": "Conference paper",
      "quality": null,
      "doi": "10.1063/5.0158545",
