    {
      "id": "scopus_1030",
      "title": "Disproportionate collapse investigation for mid-rise timber buildings",
      "authors": "Mpidi Bita, Hercend and Tannert, Thomas",
      "affiliations": "The University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC, Canada; University of Northern British Columbia, Wood Engineering, Prince George, BC, Canada",
      "countries": [
        "Canada"
      ],
      "journal": "WCTE 2018 - World Conference on Timber Engineering",
      "year": 2018,
      "score": 27,
      "abstract": "This paper employs the sudden column loss idealisation to study the probability of disproportionate collapse of a nine-storey flat-plate building, with Cross-Laminated Timber (CLT) floor and wall panels and Glue-Laminated timber columns. The approach, characterised by quickly deleting the vertical key elements from their structural topology, triggers dynamic effects to mimic extreme loading events on a building designed for gravity and lateral loads. To capture the new load-path developed from the possible collapse-resistance mechanisms, the method uses finite element models with both geometric and material nonlinearities. Different removal scenarios are considered which include corner and internal ground floor columns. The maximum allowable deformations of the CLT panels and the connections between them define the thresholds for disproportionate collapse. The outcomes of this investigation show that, for the considered case study building, it is not possible to redistribute the loads from the damaged to the undamaged parts of the building, hence highlighting a high probability of disproportionate collapse. From the results of a sensitivity analysis, the most important connections are: i) column-to-column axial stiffness, ii) floor-to-floor axial and shear stiffnesses, and iii) the floor-to-column/wall rotational stiffness. In addition, a post-and-beam system at the top floor is required to ensure hanging and catenary/membrane actions for disproportionate collapse prevention. © WCTE 2018 Committee.",
      "keywords": [
        "Alternative load-path analysis",
        "Extreme loadings",
        "Progressive collapse",
        "Robustness",
        "Structural integrity"
      ],
      "studyType": "Conference paper",
      "quality": null,
      "doi": null,
      "language": "English",
      "source": "Scopus",
      "numString": 2,
      "isDuplicate": false,
