    {
      "id": "scopus_294",
      "title": "STRUCTURAL DYNAMIC CALCULATIONS OF TRAFFIC INDUCED VIBRATIONS IN MODERN CROSS LAMINATED TIMBER CONSTRUCTIONS",
      "authors": "Paraire, Emmanuel and Appel, Silke",
      "affiliations": "GuD Geotechnik und Dynamik Consult GmbH, Berlin, Germany",
      "countries": [
        "Germany"
      ],
      "journal": "Proceedings of the International Congress on Sound and Vibration",
      "year": 2024,
      "score": 28,
      "abstract": "Timber buildings are becoming increasingly popular due to their sustainability and ease of construction. The development of modern construction methods with cross-laminated timber (CLT) now allows for the erection of high-rise timber buildings. The increase of timber building projects in cities with dense traffic networks means that constructions often take place in locations with a high potential for vibration problems. The forecast of traffic induced vibrations using finite element (FE) simulations is a well-proven design method for reinforced concrete structures. However, the structural dynamic behaviour of multi-storey timber buildings is still largely unexplored. While various research projects are attempting to address this topic, practical and reliable solutions are currently needed for the design of ongoing construction projects. The floor vibration response to road and rail traffic has been investigated for CLT building projects using FE models. The computational approaches and the modelling of the CLT structure are based on measurements and results from a research group. The models include both timber and hybrid timber-concrete floor constructions, as well as their coupling with floating screeds. © 2024 Proceedings of the International Congress on Sound and Vibration. All rights reserved.",
      "keywords": [
        "finite element methods",
        "timber constructions",
        "traffic vibrations"
      ],
      "studyType": "Conference paper",
      "quality": null,
      "doi": null,
      "language": "English",
      "source": "Scopus",
      "numString": 2,
      "isDuplicate": false,
      "duplicateOf": null,
