    {
      "id": "scopus_816",
      "title": "Key parameters for impact and airborne noise control in wood buildings",
      "authors": "Hu, Linjuan and Dagenais, Christian and Qian, Cheng and Gagnon, Sylvain",
      "affiliations": "FPInnovations, Canada, Pointe-Claire, QC, Canada",
      "countries": [
        "Canada"
      ],
      "journal": "Proceedings of 2020 International Congress on Noise Control Engineering, INTER-NOISE 2020",
      "year": 2020,
      "score": 28,
      "abstract": "Lack of understanding of wood building noise control fundamentals motivated this study. The well-known mass law and the theory of sound transmission through isotropic and uniform plate are too simple too be effectively applied for wood construction, especially for lightweight wood-frame construction due to its complexity. Wood is not a uniform and homogeneous material, and its properties vary with time and space. This paper presents some preliminary results from a multi-year study on the effects of key parameters on wood building noise control, other than mass. The parameters currently studied in laboratory included dynamic stiffness of resilient layers and its effect on cross-laminated timber (CLT) floor impact noise control, stud torsional rigidity and static airflow resistivity of insulation materials in cavity on wood stud wall airborne noise control. It was found that these three material properties are very significant for noise-controlled wood wall and floor design. The lower the dynamic stiffness, the better the floor impact sound insulation; the higher the airflow resistivity and the lower the torsional rigidity, the better the wall airborne sound insulation. Further study is being conducted to establish databases of these parameters and to derive guidance for noise-controlled wood wall and floor design, modelling and product development. © Proceedings of 2020 International Congress on Noise Control Engineering, INTER-NOISE 2020. All rights reserved.",
