    {
      "id": "scopus_1291",
      "title": "Case study of 8-story buildings constructed in Sweden, using massive timber systems",
      "authors": "Gagnon, Sylvain and Hu, Linjuan and Desjardins, Richard",
      "affiliations": "Forintek Canada Corporation, Ottawa, ON, Canada",
      "countries": [
        "Canada"
      ],
      "journal": "Proceedings, Annual Conference - Canadian Society for Civil Engineering",
      "year": 2008,
      "score": 28,
      "abstract": "In order to increase the use of structural wood in large residential and commercial constructions, it is necessary to develop new wood-based systems and assemblies that can either be used alone or in combination with concrete and steel (i.e., hybrid systems). Recently, the European wood industry has developed innovative floor and roof systems that are well suited for such applications. During the last 10 years, several European countries, including Germany, Austria, Sweden and Norway, have invested great efforts in research and development on wood-based products and systems. One of the innovative wood-based products developed in these countries is the cross-laminated timber panel (X-laminated timber or CLT panel), which can be used in floor, wall and roof systems. This system was developed about 10 years ago and consists of panels varying from 5 to 30 cm in thickness, although even thicker panels are used in some cases. The panels are made of crosswise glued or nailed layers of boards having an average thickness of 2 cm. Openings for windows, doors, staircases and for other utilities are pre-cut using CNC machines. Buildings are generally assembled on site, but panels are prefabricated and brought to the site, where they are connected by means of mechanical fastening systems (e.g., bolts, lag bolts, self-tapping screws, ringed annual shank nails, etc.). A new impressive construction project, launched in 2006 in the city of Växjö, Sweden, consists of four 8-story buildings. This article describes the details of this project, with focus on major technical issues and challenges that scientists, engineers and architects have encountered, such as fire and structural safety, vibration, acoustic performance, etc, and the solutions developed to resolve these issues.",
      "keywords": [
        "Acoustic performance",
