    {
      "id": "scopus_768",
      "title": "Cross-laminated timber buildings equipped with ductile multiple shear slotted-in steel plates connections: Proof of concept",
      "authors": "Roncari, Andrea and Loss, Cristiano",
      "affiliations": "Università di Trento, Department of Civil, Trento, TN, Italy; The University of British Columbia, Department of Wood Science, Vancouver, BC, Canada",
      "countries": [
        "Italy",
        "Canada"
      ],
      "journal": "World Conference on Timber Engineering 2021, WCTE 2021",
      "year": 2021,
      "score": 29,
      "abstract": "Globally, meanwhile assisting at the densification of cities, through which demand for affordable housing is growing fast, jurisdictions are setting embodied carbon restrictions policies for buildings and infrastructure development. In the effort of seeking carbon-neutral alternatives for buildings, along with reshaping the built environment, developers and construction companies are currently interested in expanding the use of wood beyond the traditional residential market. Cross-laminated timber (CLT) is by far the most used massive timber panel-type product, with the panel's dimensions mostly driven by design or logistic and transportation needs. In this work, a new CLT construction method is proposed, and two robust timber-to-steel connection solutions are discussed. Connections are designed for strength and serviceability compliance with the National Building Code of Canada. Numerical simulation of both connections shows ductile failure mode is predictable and confirms high elastic stiffness is attainable. Connections allow for anchoring to the ground CLT walls of a reference 15-story building. Solution adopted in forming the lateral force-resisting system also avoids classic limitations of the platform-frame construction, with capacity lowered by compression stress perpendicular to the grain of wood. © WCTE 2021. All rights reserved.",
      "keywords": [
        "CLT",
        "Composite floors",
        "High-rise buildings",
        "Hybrid structure",
        "LFRS",
        "Mass timber construction"
      ],
