    {
      "id": "scopus_338",
      "title": "SEISMIC PERFORMANCE ASSESSMENT OF LOW-RISE CLT BUILDING UNDER NEAR AND FAR-FIELD GROUND MOTIONS",
      "authors": "Javidi, Saeid and Gavric, I. and Fourlaris, G. and Salami, Mohammad Reza",
      "affiliations": "Birmingham City University, College of Built Environment, Birmingham, West Midlands, United Kingdom; Element5 Limited Partnership, Toronto, ON, Canada",
      "countries": [
        "United Kingdom",
        "Canada"
      ],
      "journal": "World Conference on Earthquake Engineering proceedings",
      "year": 2024,
      "score": 31,
      "abstract": "In this paper, the seismic performance of a low-rise Cross-laminated timber (CLT) building is evaluated using Incremental Dynamic Analysis (IDA). The building is subjected to Far-Field and Near-Field ground motion record sets, as summarised in FEMA P-695. Despite their rising popularity, there is still limited understanding of the seismic performance of CLT buildings, particularly in areas with high seismic intensity. This highlights the need for further research to clarify the behaviour of CLT buildings under seismic loads. A wide range of factors influences the seismic performance of CLT buildings, including the design criteria, connector types and layout, panels’ rigidity, opening effect of walls and floors stiffness and characteristics of the ground motions to which they are exposed, such as amplitude, duration, and dominant frequencies. For this purpose, a finite element model has been developed in OpenSees to simulate the behaviour of the three-storey CLT building of the SOFIE project. After validating the numerical model against shaking-table test results, the dynamic response of the three-storey CLT building has been examined under various ground motions with distinct characteristics. Consequently, the structure's response to various seismic intensity levels has been utilised to derive performance curves for different ground motions, enabling the assessment of the structure's potential for collapse and behaviour factors. The calculated behaviour factor and IDA results can be applied to improve the seismic design guidelines and enhance the safety and reliability of CLT buildings. © 2024, International Association for Earthquake Engineering. All rights reserved.",
      "keywords": [
        "Architectural design",
        "Dynamic response",
        "Earthquake effects",
        "Model buildings",
        "Rigid structures",
        "Safety factor",
        "Seismic design",
        "Seismic response",
        "Structural dynamics",
        "Tall buildings",
        "Behaviour factors",
        "Cross laminated",
        "Far-field ground motion",
        "Ground-motion",
        "Incremental dynamic analysis",
        "Laminated timber",
        "Near field ground motion",
        "Seismic Performance",
        "Seismic performance assessment",
        "Timber buildings"
      ],
