    {
      "id": "scopus_1292",
      "title": "Cross laminated timber as innovative building material",
      "authors": "Bejtka, I. and Lam, Frank C.F.",
      "affiliations": "Karlsruher Institut für Technologie, Campus Süd, Karlsruhe, Baden-Wurttemberg, Germany; The University of British Columbia, Department of Wood Science, Vancouver, BC, Canada",
      "countries": [
        "Germany",
        "Canada"
      ],
      "journal": "Proceedings, Annual Conference - Canadian Society for Civil Engineering",
      "year": 2008,
      "score": 31,
      "abstract": "Cross laminated timber (CLT) are multi-layer plate elements made by gluing laths, planks or boards together with the layers orientated perpendicular to each other (crosswise orientation). In Europe, three to nine layer CLT elements are made with a maximum total thickness of 500 mm and maximum plate dimensions of 30 m × 4.8 m. These CLT elements can be used in the massive wood building concept as floors and walls loaded in the out-of-plane and in-plane directions, respectively. This paper provides technical information to support opportunities for using CLT elements in massive wood buildings in residential and commercial markets. It shows how CLT elements can be used for floors loaded perpendicular to the CLT element surface and for walls loaded in-plane direction. For these applications simplified calculation models for the load-carrying behaviour are presented. Furthermore, the paper discusses the use of dowel type fasteners to transfer loads between the CLT elements by presenting an overview on the connections showing how to estimate their load-carrying capacity based on the well known European Yield Model.",
      "keywords": [
        "Commercial markets",
        "In-plane directions",
