    {
      "id": "scopus_901",
      "title": "Butt-joint bonding of timber as a key technology for point-supported, biaxial load bearing flat slabs made of cross-laminated timber",
      "authors": "Zöllig, Stefan and Muster, Marcel and Themessl, Adam",
      "affiliations": "Timber Structures 3.0 AG, Thun, Switzerland; ETH Zürich, Institute of Structural Engineering - Timber Structures, Zurich, ZH, Switzerland; Berner Fachhochschule, Wood and Civil Engineering, Bern, BE, Switzerland",
      "countries": [
        "Switzerland"
      ],
      "journal": "IOP Conference Series: Earth and Environmental Science",
      "year": 2019,
      "score": 32,
      "abstract": "An efficient butt-joint bonding technology allows to build new types of timber structures. Under the name Timber Structures 3.0 a connection has been developed which connects timber elements with an end-grain to end-grain butt joint bonding. Therefore, it is now possible to build continuous, point supported flat slabs in cross laminated timber (CLT). Multiple CLT slabs are connected rigidly together and are only supported by columns. Some major challenges had to be solved in terms of bending strength of the glued connection and shear resistance of the part of the slab above the column. The research in both topics is successful and more projects were built in the last two years using this technology. Starting point was a real scale structure at ETH Zurich, followed by a working platform for a timber construction company and finally four three storey residential buildings. The research team is continuing to optimize the different elements of this innovative technology and will soon provide engineers with guidelines to design their own biaxial, point supported timber flat slabs. © 2019 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights reserved.",
      "keywords": [
        "Bending strength",
        "Butt welding",
        "Construction industry",
        "Laminating",
        "Sustainable development",
        "Bonding technology",
        "Innovative technology",
        "Key technologies",
        "Residential building",
        "Scale structures",
        "Shear resistances",
        "Timber construction",
        "Timber structures",
        "Timber"
      ],
