    {
      "id": "scopus_736",
      "title": "Indirect method for detecting char fall off events in full-scale unprotected CLT walls and floors assemblies during ASTM E119 fire test",
      "authors": "Hong, Seung-hyun and Muszyñski, Lech and Gupta, Rakesh",
      "affiliations": "Oregon State University, Department of Wood Science and Engineering, Corvallis, OR, United States",
      "countries": [
        "United States"
      ],
      "journal": "World Conference on Timber Engineering 2021, WCTE 2021",
      "year": 2021,
      "score": 33,
      "abstract": "The most desired behavior for CLT panels in fire events is a gradual transformation of flaming combustion into smouldering combustion until the natural self-extinguishment. Such behavior is observed when the charred wood on the surface provides layer of insulation for the core material. However, a large proportion of CLT products is bonded with PUR adhesive system that at some point of fire event lets the char layer fall off creating additional source of heat in the interior and, exposing unburned surface of the next layer to fire. The severity of the effect of such event on the fire dynamic may depend on the precise timing compared to the progress of the char front through the panel section. The hypothesis of this study is that the char fall-off events may be detected with reasonable accuracy based on the analysis of the micro-disturbances of the furnace temperature signal. This paper presents the theoretical background and a proof of concept for a practical application of such method. © WCTE 2021. All rights reserved.",
      "keywords": [
        "CLT",
        "Cross-Laminated Timber",
        "DF-L",
        "Douglas fir-larch",
        "Fire resistance",
        "Floors",
        "Melamine urea formaldehyde",
        "MUF",
        "Polyurethane",
        "PUR",
        "SPF",
        "Spruce-pine-fir",
        "Walls"
      ],
