    {
      "id": "scopus_760",
      "title": "Mechanical performance of CLT-concrete notched connector by shear tests",
      "authors": "Thai, Minh Van and Ménard, Sylvain and Elachachi, Sidi Mohammed and Galimard, Philippe",
      "affiliations": "Université du Québec à Chicoutimi, Chicoutimi-Jonquiere, QC, Canada; Université de Bordeaux, Bordeaux, Nouvelle-Aquitaine, France",
      "countries": [
        "Canada",
        "France"
      ],
      "journal": "World Conference on Timber Engineering 2021, WCTE 2021",
      "year": 2021,
      "score": 33,
      "abstract": "The mechanical performance of a timber-concrete notched connector was investigated through the influential analysis of four variables: length of the loaded edge of the timber, notch depth, screw length, and the thickness of the concrete layer. This type of connector is particularly suitable for the CLT-concrete floor structure because of its performance in terms of stiffness, strength, and easy installation on site. The performance of the notched connector included the load-bearing capacity, slip modulus, and failure behavior. Both two geometric variables influence the slip modulus, while the maximum load was heavily dependent on the depth of the notch. The length of the screw and thickness of the concrete layer had no significant effect on the mechanical performance of the connector. Different failure types were also studied. © WCTE 2021. All rights reserved.",
      "keywords": [
        "CLT-concrete composite",
        "Experimental testing",
        "Geometry variable",
        "Notched connector",
        "Timber-concrete floor systems"
      ],
