    {
      "id": "scopus_774",
      "title": "Connectors for hybrid cross-laminated timber prefabricated buildings",
      "authors": "Tolotti, Silvia and Tannert, Thomas and Loss, Cristiano",
      "affiliations": "Università di Trento, Department of Civil, Trento, TN, Italy; University of Northern British Columbia, Prince George, BC, Canada; The University of British Columbia, Department of Wood Science, Vancouver, BC, Canada",
      "countries": [
        "Italy",
        "Canada"
      ],
      "journal": "World Conference on Timber Engineering 2021, WCTE 2021",
      "year": 2021,
      "score": 38,
      "abstract": "Common mass-timber buildings use prefabricated wood-based elements for wall, floor and roof applications. For tall and large construction, hybrid solutions in which mass-timber elements are combined with concrete, steel or other structural materials create cost-effective sustainable systems and reduce on-site construction time. As part of wood-based building construction advancements, new methods of assembly are being developed with particular attention to prefabrication and modular components, such as volumetric modules or macro-panel walls and floors. This paper presents and discusses a new connection method that can be incorporated into composite steel-cross-laminated timber (CLT) floors or CLT-infill hybrid frames. Shear connectors consist of threaded rods inserted into CLT panels and surrounded by a thick layer of epoxy-based grout. Connectors can be easily designed to provide high stiffness and shear capacity and responds to quick assembly requirements on-site. Results obtained from an experimental campaign considering quasi-static loads acting along the main axis of CLT elements are reported and discussed. This connection delivers a solution to architects' and designers' needs for flexibility at production and construction sites. © WCTE 2021. All rights reserved.",
      "keywords": [
        "Capacity",
        "CLT connectors",
        "Glued rods",
        "Shear connectors",
        "Stiffness",
        "Timber joints"
      ],
