    {
      "id": "scopus_344",
      "title": "INFLUENCE OF CONSTRUCTION METHODS ON THE LATERAL BEHAVIOUR OF CLT-GLULAM HYBRID STRUCTURAL SYSTEMS",
      "authors": "Wang, Xijun and He, Minjuan and Li, Zheng",
      "affiliations": "Tongji University, Shanghai, China",
      "countries": [
        "China"
      ],
      "journal": "World Conference on Earthquake Engineering proceedings",
      "year": 2024,
      "score": 40,
      "abstract": "Bracing glulam frames with cross-laminated timber (CLT) shear walls, the CLT-glulam hybrid structural system presents a promising solution for multi-story timber construction. In this paper, the influence of construction methods on the lateral behaviour of such hybrid structural systems was investigated. A total of six prototype CLT-glulam hybrid structural systems were designed, considering both platform and balloon construction methods for CLT shear walls. The seismic performance of the prototype structural systems was evaluated in terms of maximum inter-story drift ratios (MaxISDRs), horizontal peak floor accelerations (PFAs), and connection damage indices. The results show that the hybrid structural systems with balloon-type CLT shear walls had smaller MaxISDRs but larger FPAs, compared to those with platform-type CLT shear walls. The connection in balloon-type CLT shear walls experienced more severe damage than those in platform-type CLT shear walls. For the connection damage of balloon-type CLT shear walls, the probability of nonexceedance (PNE) values for the collapse prevention (CP) performance level were lower than 50%, falling below the expected PNE value for major earthquakes. The connection damage of glulam frames remained low during major earthquakes. © 2024, International Association for Earthquake Engineering. All rights reserved.",
      "keywords": [
        "Earthquake effects",
        "Earthquake engineering",
        "Mortar",
        "Structural frames",
        "Wooden construction",
        "Balloon type",
