    {
      "id": "scopus_1205",
      "title": "Performance of two-storey CLT house subjected to lateral loads",
      "authors": "Popovski, Marjan and Gavric, Igor and Schneider, Johannes",
      "affiliations": "FPInnovations, Canada, Pointe-Claire, QC, Canada; The University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC, Canada",
      "countries": [
        "Canada"
      ],
      "journal": "WCTE 2014 - World Conference on Timber Engineering, Proceedings",
      "year": 2014,
      "score": 41,
      "abstract": "The work presented in this paper is a continuation of the FPInnovations' research project on determining the performance of the CLT as a structural system under lateral loads [1]. In the first part of the project a series of quasi-static tests on different connections and various configurations of CLT wall panels were conducted [2]. Wall configurations included single panel walls with three different aspect ratios, multi-panel walls with step joints and different types of screws to connect them, as well as two-storey wall assemblies. In the analytical part of the project, analytical models were developed and verified against the test results and a series of non-linear dynamic analyses was conducted on a mid-rise CLT building [4]. The results from these analyses were used to propose appropriate values for the R-factors for CLT structures in the National Building Code of Canada (NBCC). In this paper the research work of the next step of this program is presented. A two storey full-scale model of a CLT house was tested under quasi-static monotonic and cyclic lateral loading in two directions, one direction at a time. The CLT house used in the testing program was 6.0 m long, 4.8 m wide and had a total height of 4.79 m (Fig. 1). All wall and floor/roof panels were 3-ply panels with a thickness of 94 mm. The design of the house was performed according to 2010 NBCC for locality such as Vancouver, BC with force modification factors R<inf>d</inf> = 2.0 and R<inf>0</inf> = 1.5.",
      "keywords": [
        "Cross-laminated timber",
        "Cyclic tests",
        "Full-scale tests",
        "Seismic performance"
      ],
