    {
      "id": "scopus_352",
      "title": "DEVELOPING AN INNOVATIVE CONNECTION FOR EXISTING RC BUILDINGS EXTERNALLY RENOVATED BY CLT PANELS",
      "authors": "Mehdipour, Zabih and Poletti, Elisa and Fontes, André Cerejeira and Branco, Jorge Manuel",
      "affiliations": "Institute for Sustainability and Innovation in Structural Engineering, Coimbra, Coimbra, Portugal; Universidade do Minho, Lab2PT, Braga, Braga, Portugal",
      "countries": [
        "Portugal"
      ],
      "journal": "World Conference on Earthquake Engineering proceedings",
      "year": 2024,
      "score": 42,
      "abstract": "Prefabricated cross-laminated timber (CLT) shear walls, accompanied by ductile connections, have recently emerged as a promising and competitive solution among other exoskeletons for enhancing the structural performance of existing buildings, mainly those infilled with masonry walls. The high strength-to-weight ratio of the panels enables them to remain in the elastic range under seismic loads, while the connections are designed to act as fuses for the structure. This means that they concentrate the main seismic forces and prevent their transfer to the building elements and masonry infill, which are vulnerable to brittle failures due to being designed based on out-dated seismic and design codes. To achieve high ductility and efficient dissipation of seismic energy, the geometry of the connection, such as the size and arrangement of fasteners, as well as the mechanical properties of the connectors, such as the use of fully threaded screws and high-grade bolts, must be considered in designing an effective Beam-CLT connection. In this study, an experimental campaign was carried out to evaluate the cyclic behaviour of an innovative connection for reinforced concrete buildings externally strengthened with CLT panels. The results demonstrate that this connector significantly enhances the strength and stiffness of the building while efficiently dissipating seismic energy, thereby ultimately enhancing the building's ductility. The promising findings of the study provide owners/designers with more choices to select as the connector, in addition to those already available on the market, depending on their retrofitting purposes and budgets. © 2024, International Association for Earthquake Engineering. All rights reserved.",
      "keywords": [
        "CLT shear walls",
        "ductile design",
        "existing buildings",
        "experimental campaign",
        "RC-CLT connection"
      ],
      "studyType": "Book chapter",
      "quality": null,
      "doi": null,
      "language": "English",
      "source": "Scopus",
