    {
      "id": "scopus_1278",
      "title": "Experimental approach on sound transmission loss of Cross Laminated Timber floors for building",
      "authors": "Kouyoumji, Jean Luc and Gagnon, Sylvain",
      "affiliations": "FCBA, Division of Research, Bordeaux, France; Forintek Canada Corporation, Ottawa, ON, Canada",
      "countries": [
        "France",
        "Canada"
      ],
      "journal": "39th International Congress on Noise Control Engineering 2010, INTER-NOISE 2010",
      "year": 2010,
      "score": 45,
      "abstract": "Adequate levels of noise control in multi-family buildings are mandatory requirements of building codes in Canada, the United States, Europe, and most developed Asian countries. In many jurisdictions, these requirements are as strictly enforced as those for structural sufficiency and fire safety. Much effort has been spent on evaluation of sound transmission class (STC) and impact sound insulation class (IIC) of floor and wall assemblies and on studies of flanking transmission in multi-family dwellings in Canada. However, no work has been done until now in Canada on the acoustic performance of CLT systems in construction. This document focuses mainly on the development of CLT floor assemblies made of crosslaminated timber elements capable to perform well acoustically in residential, multiresidential and non residential buildings in Canada and in US. Existing generic floor assemblies used in Europe are presented in the document as well as examples of floor assemblies that could be used in the North American market.",
      "keywords": [
        "Cross-Laminated Timber",
        "Sound Transmission Loss",
        "Wood solid floor"
      ],
      "studyType": "Conference paper",
      "quality": null,
