    {
      "id": "scopus_2052",
      "title": "Experimental investigations of reinforced cross-laminated timber-concrete composite systems",
      "authors": "Shahnewaz, Md and Jackson, Robert and Fast, Tobias and Tannert, Thomas",
      "affiliations": "Fast + Epp, Vancouver, BC, Canada; University of Northern British Columbia, School of Engineering, Prince George, BC, Canada",
      "countries": [
        "Canada"
      ],
      "journal": "World Conference on Timber Engineering 2021, WCTE 2021",
      "year": 2021,
      "score": 45,
      "abstract": "This paper presents experimental investigations on timber concrete composite (TCC) systems as a part of the design of “The Arbour”, a mass timber building for George Brown College, located in Toronto, Canada. The objective was to determine the stiffness, ultimate load-carrying capacity, and failure modes of TCC systems using various shear connectors. The TCC system was comprised of 245mm thick, 7-ply cross-laminated timber (CLT) panels with 150 mm concrete topping, joined with shear connectors. A total of 18-half scale and 12-full scale 2.4 m wide TCC floor segments with a span of 4.6 m and 9.2 m, respectively, were tested to failure under symmetric four-point bending as well as torsional bending. The efficiency of three types of shear connectors: self-tapping screws, steel kerf plates, and proprietary glued-in Holz-Beton-Verbund (HBV) plates were evaluated. Since no test results were available for steel kerf plate shear connectors in CLT, small scale tests were on this connector were conducted utilizing three different embedment depths in the CLT, varying from 35mm to 90 mm. To simulate a torsional load case for edge panels, one test series was completed by applying eccentric loading near the edge - inducing torsional behaviour. The small-scale test provided data on the required embedment depth of steel kerf plates. The full-scale tests showed that the steel kerf plates exhibited the highest capacity and stiffness, and were the most economical solution. The results from this research have been utilized to make project-specific design decisions for The Arbour and are intended to further the study of TCC components for future large-span applications. © WCTE 2021. All rights reserved.",
      "keywords": [
        "Connections",
        "Cross-laminated timber",
        "Shear capacity",
        "Timber concrete composite",
        "Vibration"
      ],
      "studyType": "Conference paper",
      "quality": null,
      "doi": null,
      "language": "English",
      "source": "Scopus",
      "numString": 5,
      "isDuplicate": true,
      "duplicateOf": "scopus_731",
