    {
      "id": "scopus_106",
      "title": "CLT-steel composite flooring a sustainable solution for South Africa?",
      "authors": "Skorpen, Sarah A. and Cattaert, G. and Bouchaïr, Abdelhamid",
      "affiliations": "University of Pretoria, Department of Civil Engineering, Pretoria, Gauteng, South Africa; Clermont Auvergne INP, Institut Pascal, Aubiere, Auvergne-Rhone-Alpes, France",
      "countries": [
        "South Africa",
        "France"
      ],
      "journal": "Engineering Materials, Structures, Systems and Methods for a More Sustainable Future",
      "year": 2025,
      "score": 51,
      "abstract": "Timber-steel composite flooring systems are being used in many countries around the world and can have numerous advantages for buildings, including reduced structural mass and reduced embodied carbon. Most slab systems in South Africa have traditionally been concrete, but as the CLT (cross laminate timber) starts to become more readily available, it is becoming a viable slab system option. The aim of this paper is to discuss the viability of using CLT-steel composite slab systems for the South African market, using CLT constructed with fast-growing varieties of timber such as South African Pine and Eucalyptus. To this end the properties of CLT constructed from South African Pine and Eucalyptus were tested, as well as the stiffness of composite connectors. The effect of varying the orien-tation of the CLT panel was considered by testing samples both parallel and perpendicular to the grain. © 2025 selection and editorial matter, Alphose Zingoni; individual chapters, the contributors. All rights reserved.",
