    {
      "id": "scopus_524",
      "title": "LABORATORY MEASUREMENTS OF LIGHTWEIGHT FLOATING FLOOR SYSTEMS ON CROSS-LAMINATED TIMBER (CLT) SLABS",
      "authors": "Rodrigues, Marina and Motal, Tomáš and Horváth, Zoltán",
      "affiliations": "CDM Stravitec, Brussels, Belgium; CDM Stravitec, Prague, Czech Republic; CDM Stravitec, Budapest, Hungary",
      "countries": [
        "Belgium",
        "Czechia",
        "Hungary"
      ],
      "journal": "Proceedings of the International Congress on Sound and Vibration",
      "year": 2023,
      "score": 58,
      "abstract": "Mass timber solutions, including cross-laminated timber (CLT), are an excellent substitute to the more traditional fossil fuel-intensive construction materials. The use of these mass timber products allows for the reduction of the carbon footprint of the built environment. Nevertheless, the use of mass timber poses some rather unique challenges in terms of acoustics. Moreover, one of the most desired aspects of mass timber construction is the ability to leave a building's structure exposed as finish, which creates the need for asymmetric assemblies. Consequently, when talking about floor/ceiling applications, it is preferable that any acoustic component be installed on top of the assembly. One of the main advantages of mass timber is the strength-to-weight ratio, which allows for lighter setups than would be possible with other slab-type building materials. This reduces the overall mass of the building, which in turn offers many other benefits, such as reduced seismic forces and smaller building foundation requirements. To maintain these benefits and reduce the carbon footprint of the building, adding the mass of a concrete slab to improve the acoustic performance is not the best solution, leaving only the option of adding decouplers. To measure airborne sound reduction and impact noise isolation of lightweight floating floor systems and confirm that CLT-assemblies can provide satisfactory sound insulation, CDM Stravitec conducted a test campaign at the Belgian Building Research Institute. During this campaign, different types of decouplers were tested, including discrete bearings with various thicknesses and/or different void depths (filled entirely or partially with insulation material) and resilient mats and strips placed between the CLT slab and the different timber, cement and gypsum-based boards and this with and without the use of the well-known constrained layer damping technique. This paper presents the findings of that test campaign. © 2023 Proceedings of the International Congress on Sound and Vibration. All rights reserved.",
      "keywords": [
        "building acoustics",
        "cross-laminated timber",
        "floating floors",
        "timber construction"
      ],
      "studyType": "Conference paper",
      "quality": null,
      "doi": null,
      "language": "English",
