    {
      "id": "scopus_1228",
      "title": "Mitigation of content damage for tall cross laminated timber (CLT) buildings during earthquake events",
      "authors": "Pei, Shiling and van de Lindt, John W. and Liu, Hongyan",
      "affiliations": "Southwest Jiaotong University, Key Laboratory of Seismic Engineering of Sichuan Province, Chengdu, Sichuan, China; Walter Scott, Jr. College of Engineering, Fort Collins, CO, United States; University of Minnesota Duluth, Department of Civil & Environmental Engineering, Duluth, MN, United States",
      "countries": [
        "China",
        "United States"
      ],
      "journal": "ISEC 2013 - 7th International Structural Engineering and Construction Conference: New Developments in Structural Engineering and Construction",
      "year": 2013,
      "score": 58,
      "abstract": "With very high strength-to-weight ratio, timber has historically been a suitable choice for earthquake resistant housing. Cross laminated timber (CLT) construction has been proven to be a viable option for tall wood buildings potentially over 10 stories. However, current building style for CLT tends to produce a very stiff design and cause amplification of seismic accelerations associated with the near-elastic structural responses and result in occupants' injury and severe contents damage. In this study, a conceptual performance-based seismic design approach to include selected stories with relatively low stiffness and high ductility in a tall CLT building to reduce the amplification effect was introduced. CLT is particularly suitable for the implementation of the method because CLT shear walls are robust in resisting gravity load and can be configured to gain desired level of ductility by altering connection details. The proposed design concept was applied to an example 12-story CLT building to be built in high seismic region. The numerical simulation showed that the design method can effectively limit floor acceleration and reduce content damage during a MCE level event. Given a number of ductile stories, the behavior of the as-designed system will be similar to a base-isolated structure. © © 2013 by Research Publishing Services.",
      "keywords": [
        "Cross laminated timber",
        "Ductility",
        "Floor acceleration",
        "Performance-based seismic design"
      ],
      "studyType": "Conference paper",
      "quality": null,
      "doi": "10.3850/978-981-07-5354-2-St-162-485",
      "language": "English",
