    {
      "id": "scopus_68",
      "title": "Vibration Performance Evaluation of CLT-Concrete Composite Floor for Applying Wooden Apartment",
      "authors": "An, Jin-hyun and Kim, Sung-yong and Oh, Keunyeong",
      "affiliations": "Changwon National University, Dept. of Architectural Engineering, Changwon, Gyeongsangnam-do, South Korea; Construction Engineer Policy Institute of Korea, Dept. of Building Research, Seoul, South Korea",
      "countries": [
        "South Korea"
      ],
      "journal": "Journal of the Architectural Institute of Korea",
      "year": 2025,
      "score": 59,
      "abstract": "This study compares the vibration performance of timber-concrete composite (TCC) floors, formed by combining cross-laminated timber (CLT) and a 60 mm concrete topping, with that of single CLT floors. Two CLT specimens (T-150 and T-210) and one TCC specimen (TCC-210) underwent impact hammer and walking tests to measure their dynamic properties, including fundamental frequency, damping ratio, and dynamic stiffness. All floors exhibited fundamental frequencies above 10 Hz, indicating no risk of resonance under walking loads. TCC-210 achieved the highest damping ratio (1.80%) and dynamic stiffness, demonstrating enhanced vibration control through the structural coupling of timber and concrete. Notably, TCC-210 satisfied the recommended response factor for office environments proposed by the Steel Construction Institute (SCI). Finite element analysis was then conducted to evaluate floor vibration performance in a wooden apartment setting, revealing that TCC floors demonstrated the most favorable results. Both the experiments and the analyses were performed under conservative conditions, suggesting that real-world performance might be underestimated. In particular, considering boundary conditions and non-structural elements could further improve vibration behavior. These findings highlight the potential of TCC technology for timber buildings, while underscoring the need for continued research, refined design guidelines, and domestic standards to fully realize its capabilities. © 2025 Architectural Institute of Korea.",
      "keywords": [
        "Cross-Laminated timber (CLT)",
        "Floor Vibration",
        "Numerical Analysis",
        "Serviceability",
        "Timber-Concrete Composite (TCC)"
      ],
      "studyType": "Article",
      "quality": null,
      "doi": "10.5659/JAIK.2025.41.6.247",
