    {
      "id": "scopus_790",
      "title": "DAMAGE ASSESSMENT OF A CROSS LAMINATED TIMBER WALL BY APPLYING VIBRATION-BASED MODEL UPDATING TECHNIQUES",
      "authors": "Opazo-Vega, Alexander and Benedetti-Leonelli, F. and Jara-Cisterna, Alan and Oyarzo-Vera, Claudio A.",
      "affiliations": "Universidad del Bio Bio, Department of Civil & Environmental Engineering, Concepcion, BI, Chile; Universidad Catolica de la Santisima Concepcion, Department of Civil Engineering, Concepcion, BI, Chile",
      "countries": [
        "Chile"
      ],
      "journal": "World Conference on Earthquake Engineering proceedings",
      "year": 2021,
      "score": 61,
      "abstract": "In Chile, cross-laminated timber (CLT) panels are emerging as a suitable construction system to overcome the challenges associated with increasing the number of sustainable buildings in a country with high seismicity. The lateral stiffness of this construction system mainly depends on the quantity and distribution of the connectors between the different CLT panels. During a seismic event, these joints could suffer a degradation of their stiffness, due to the phenomena of connectors' yielding and timber's crushing. These damages are usually hidden by non-structural sheathing elements installed in walls and slabs, so it is difficult to identify both the geometric location and severity of the damage after a seismic event. The purpose of this study is to identify the location and severity of joint damage in a two-story, I-section, CLT wall by applying vibration-based model updating techniques. One 2.6-m-long, 1.2-m-wide, and 4.2-m-high CLT wall was subjected to incremental non-reversed cyclic lateral load tests. The CLT wall consisted of 100mm-thick, three-ply CLT wall and slab panels with Radiata Pine lamina. The CLT wall was built with the platform system, from the assembly of eight wall panels (2.0-m-high, 1.2-m-wide) and two slab panels (2.6-m-long, 1.2-m-wide). Wall panels were connected to the steel foundation and CLT slab using commercial Simpson Strong-Tie angle brackets and hold-downs. All other joints between wall panels were made through self-drilling screws. The main difference between the two CLT modules was that the first used eighteen hold-downs and six angle brackets, while the second twelve hold-downs and twelve angle brackets. The CLT wall was instrumented with both displacement transducers and accelerometers, and low-energy modal impact tests were applied between each increment of the quasi-static lateral load cycle. Modal properties (frequencies and mode shapes) of the CLT wall were estimated through Enhanced Frequency Domain Decomposition (EFDD) and Stochastic Subspace Identification (SSI) methods. A simplified finite element model of the CLT wall was successively updating through global sensitivity analysis techniques (Elementary Effect Test (EET) and Regional Sensitivity Analysis (RSA)) until the differences between experimental and numerically simulated frequencies and modal shapes were minimal. In the CLT wall, the damages were concentrated in the edge hold-downs and the vertical wall-to-wall screwed connections. The results suggest that it is possible to detect, with the proposed methodology, both the location and the severity of damage at the CLT wall joints. The excellent correlation between the observed, measured, and simulated damages support the idea that this approach can be extended to more complicated structural configurations. © 2024, International Association for Earthquake Engineering. All rights reserved.",
      "keywords": [
        "global sensitivity analysis",
        "joint damage detection",
        "massive timber walls",
        "operational modal analysis"
      ],
      "studyType": "Book chapter",
      "quality": null,
      "doi": null,
      "language": "English",
      "source": "Scopus",
      "numString": 2,
      "isDuplicate": false,
      "duplicateOf": null,
      "dataProcessingStatus": "included",
