    {
      "id": "scopus_755",
      "title": "Mass timber panel-concrete composite floor behaviour with inclined self-tapping screws and insulation layer",
      "authors": "Mirdad, Md Abdul Hamid and Chui, Yinghei",
      "affiliations": "University of Alberta, Edmonton, AB, Canada",
      "countries": [
        "Canada"
      ],
      "journal": "World Conference on Timber Engineering 2021, WCTE 2021",
      "year": 2021,
      "score": 64,
      "abstract": "Mass Timber Panels (MTP) are a new generation of engineered wood panels that are available in large plane dimensions to facilitate fast floor construction with the obvious environmental benefit of being a renewable material. In floor construction, concrete slab or topping is often applied over the MTP panels to improve various performance attributes, including structural, acoustic and vibration serviceability. Mass Timber Panel-Concrete (MTPC) composite floor system often consists of a Mass Timber Panel (MTP) connected to the concrete layer with mechanical fasteners and a sound insulation layer in between. Behaviour of the MTPC composite with an insulation layer, which may have an adverse impact on the composite properties is not well investigated. To address this gap, composite floor panels in one-way action has been tested in four-point bending with different configurations to investigate the influence of different parameters. This bending test will provide test data to assist in the development of an analytical model to predict the capacity, effective bending stiffness, and failure modes of MTPC composite floor system. © WCTE 2021. All rights reserved.",
      "keywords": [
        "Cross laminated timber",
        "Glue laminated timber",
        "Mass timber panel",
        "One-way action",
        "Self-tapping screw",
        "Third-point bending",
        "Timber-concrete composite floor"
      ],
