    {
      "id": "scopus_1173",
      "title": "Evaluation of bending tests on composite glulam-CLT beams connected with double-sided punched metal plates and inclined screws",
      "authors": "Jacquier, Nicolas and Girhammar, U. A.",
      "affiliations": "Luleå University of Technology, Div. of Structural Engineering - Timber Structures, Lulea, Norrbotten, Sweden",
      "countries": [
        "Sweden"
      ],
      "journal": "Construction and Building Materials",
      "year": 2015,
      "score": 68,
      "abstract": "Composite beam elements made of glulam beams and cross laminated timber (CLT) panels, and connected with a new shear connection system were tested under four point bending. The beams tested represent a section of a cassette floor element to be used in a multi-storey CLT construction system. The shear connection is primarily made of double-sided punched metal plate fasteners, connecting the CLT and glulam members to form a T-cross-section. Due to the uncertainty about the capacity of the double-sided nail plates to resist possible separation forces between the timber members, the shear connection may be secured with screws. Bending tests were performed with three shear connection configurations: double-sided punched metal plate fasteners only, inclined screws only, and double-sided punched metal plate fasteners combined with inclined screws. An additional test with a screw-glued connection was made for comparison. The results show that a shear connection with double-sided nail plates can be designed to provide a sufficiently high level of composite action and load-carrying capacity and represents an alternative shear connection system for glulam-CLT composite floors. © 2015 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.",
      "keywords": [
        "Bending tests",
        "CLT",
        "Composite beam",
        "Cross laminated timber",
        "Double-sided punched metal plate fasteners",
        "Glulam",
        "Inclined screws"
      ],
