    {
      "id": "scopus_793",
      "title": "APPLICABILITY OF MS ELEMENT FOR CLT CONSTRUCTIONS",
      "authors": "Miyake, Tatsuya and Azumi, Yoshiharu and Kawai, Naohito and Isoda, Hiroshi and Araki, Yasuhiro",
      "affiliations": "Nihon System Sekkei Architects & Engineers, Japan; Kogakuin University, Tokyo, Japan; Kyoto University, Kyoto, Kyoto, Japan; Japan National Institute for Land and Infrastructure Management, Tsukuba, Ibaraki, Japan",
      "countries": [
        "Japan"
      ],
      "journal": "World Conference on Earthquake Engineering proceedings",
      "year": 2021,
      "score": 71,
      "abstract": "It is well known that bending strength of reinforced concrete (“RC” in the followings) column is affected by axial force. Multiple Spring element (“MS element” in the followings) had been developed to deal with this problem, where concrete and steel bars in cross section of RC column were converted into discrete spring elements between rigid planes placed parallel in small distance. Lateral stiffness and strength of CLT panel shear wall are similarly affected by vertical load. When CLT panel shear wall is subjected to lateral force, the edge of CLT panel touching rigid plane such as concrete surface of foundation deforms from compressive stress as concrete of RC column, and tensile connection deforms as steel bars in RC column. Therefore, MS element is expected useful also to examine force-deformation relation of the edge of CLT panel shear wall, including the edge touching soft plane such as CLT floor panel where the edge embeds into the panel. Based on this inference, lateral loading tests of CLT panel shear wall under static vertical load were conducted to confirm the usefulness of MS element. As a result, the relations between bending moment and rotation from the tests well agreed with analytical results using MS element for various value of vertical load, proving the applicability of MS element and Bernoulli-Euler hypothesis even for the edge of CLT panel connected to the other member. In addition, the relation between bending strength and axial strength that is so called M-N interaction where “M” means bending force and “N” means axial force, was obtained using MS element. Two of other M-N interactions were selected for comparison. One was led from the approximate method based on the result of study on RC structure, another was led analytically using the standard structural model shown in the manual on structural design of CLT construction in Japan. In both of them, comparing with the M-N interaction from MS element, it was confirmed that the bending strength in high axial force was underestimated especially in the latter, and that the expansion of compressive region from the axial force wasn’t appropriately estimated in these methods. Lastly, the load incremental analysis using the structural model with MS elements for 3-story CLT construction used in past shake table test was conducted. As a result, it was recognized that analytical relations between story shear and story drift well agreed with the result of shake table test, while analytical result using the standard structural model above mentioned underestimated the stiffness and the strength. The composition of MS element, that is composed with discrete spring elements corresponding to the edge of CLT panel and the tensile connection, isn’t simple. However, it is set between 2 nodes, and the force-deformation relations of each spring elements are automatically converted into the force-deformation relation of these nodes. Therefore, MS element can be dealt as the unitary element in the structural model for analysis, enabling simplification of the model. Besides, M-N interaction is appropriately estimated even in high axial force by MS element, therefore it is adequate also for structural design of high-rise building. MS element is planned to be added as one of the standard elements for structural model of CLT constructions in next revision of the manual above mentioned. © 2024, International Association for Earthquake Engineering. All rights reserved.",
      "keywords": [
        "CLT construction",
        "M-N interaction",
        "structural design",
        "structural model"
      ],
      "studyType": "Book chapter",
      "quality": null,
      "doi": null,
      "language": "English",
      "source": "Scopus",
      "numString": 2,
      "isDuplicate": false,
      "duplicateOf": null,
