    {
      "id": "scopus_236",
      "title": "Status of CLT building construction from 2004 to 2023",
      "authors": "Jeong, Gi-young",
      "affiliations": "Chonnam National University, Department of Wood Science and Engineering, Gwangju, South Korea",
      "countries": [
        "South Korea"
      ],
      "journal": "Construction and Building Materials",
      "year": 2024,
      "score": 75,
      "abstract": "The goal of this study was to review cross-laminated timber (CLT) construction from 2004 to 2023 by focusing on structural systems and the material selection for structural elements of CLT buildings with different heights and purposes. Data on 358 three-story or higher CLT buildings were collected. The CLT building data were classified according to the purpose, geographical location, story level, structural system, core, main structural materials, and floor design of the building. Although numerous high-rise apartments and mixed-use CLT buildings were constructed from 2004 to 2023, the most dominant story level was mid-rise CLT buildings (5[sbnd]10 stories), which represented 73 % of all CLT buildings. High-rise (11 stories and higher) and low-rise (1[sbnd]4 stories) CLT buildings were 15.3 % and 11.5 %, respectively. Across the period, the most common structural system used in CLT buildings was the column-and-beam system, representing 46.2 %, followed by the wall system, indicating 38.4 %. Notably, residential buildings used more CLT cores than concrete or steel cores, whereas non-residential buildings used more concrete cores than CLT cores. The cluster analysis indicated the following order of importance for the grouping factors of CLT buildings: height, structural system, floor material, purpose of building, location, and core material. The design processes differed according to the height of the CLT buildings. © 2024 Elsevier Ltd",
      "keywords": [
        "CLT",
        "Core",
        "Floor",
        "Purpose of building",
        "Structural system"
      ],
      "studyType": "Article",
