    {
      "id": "scopus_183",
      "title": "NUMERICAL INVESTIGATION OF CLT COMPOSITE DOUBLE T-BEAMS",
      "authors": "Masoudnia, Reza and Masoudnia, Saber and Hashemi, Ashkan and Quenneville, Pierre J.H.",
      "affiliations": "University of Waikato, Hamilton, WKO, New Zealand; Red Stag, Hamilton, New Zealand; Red Stag; University of Auckland, Department of Civil & Environmental Engineering, Auckland, AUK, New Zealand; University of Auckland, Department of Civil & Environmental Engineering, Auckland, AUK, New Zealand",
      "countries": [
        "New Zealand"
      ],
      "journal": "Proceedings from the 14th World Conference on Timber Engineering: Advancing Timber for the Future Built Environment, WCTE 2025",
      "year": 2025,
      "score": 88,
      "abstract": "A cross-laminated timber (CLT) composite double T-beam is a section consisting of two CLT composite T-beams joined side by side using a single solid CLT flange. An adequate number of screws is used to create an exceptional connection that functions as a structural shear key in the CLT composite section. This combination makes it a cost-efficient structural system for roof and floor applications, capable of carrying high loads over relatively long spans. CLT and LVL are two well-known high-performance engineered wood products composed of layered lumber boards. In CLT, the boards are stacked and glued together crosswise at 90-degree angles, whereas in LVL they are arranged parallel to one another. An experimentally verified numerical model of the CLT composite T-beam has been used to study the behavior of CLT composite double T-beams. The detailed parametric study showed that the effective flange width increases as the ratio of the transverse layer depth to the longitudinal layer depth of the CLT flange rises. Additionally, using a CLT flange with a higher modulus of elasticity slightly improves the effective flange width. This wider effective flange in the optimized CLT composite T-beam allows designers to increase both the beam span and the spacing between the webs, resulting in a more cost-efficient design. A comprehensive numerical parametric study demonstrates that the CLT composite double T-beam has the potential for widespread use in long-span roof and floor applications in mass timber constructions for both residential and commercial projects. © (2025) WORLD CONFERENCE ON TIMBER ENGINEERING 2025 (WCTE 2025) All rights reserved.",
      "keywords": [
        "CLT composite double T-beam",
        "CLT composite T-beam",
        "Cross Laminated Timber",
        "Effective flange width",
        "Engineered wood product"
      ],
      "studyType": "Conference paper",
      "quality": null,
      "doi": "10.52202/080513-0345",
