    {
      "id": "scopus_947",
      "title": "Evaluation of delamination and bending performance of composite clt reinforced with CFRP",
      "authors": "Song, Yojin and Lee, In-hwan and Song, Dabin and Hong, Soonil",
      "affiliations": "Kangwon National University, Division of Forest Material Science and Engineering, Chuncheon, Gangwon-do, South Korea",
      "countries": [
        "South Korea"
      ],
      "journal": "Wood and Fiber Science",
      "year": 2019,
      "score": 170,
      "abstract": "To develop a high-performance, lightweight, cross-laminated timber (CLT) floor, this study tested the delamination performance between carbon fiber-reinforced plastic (CFRP) and CLT and the bending performance of a CFRP composite CLT that was differently reinforced according to the shape of the CFRP. The test results showed that the soaking and boiling delamination between CLT and CFRP of the CFRP composite CLT produced by spreading a polyurethane adhesive at 300 g/m<sup>2</sup> were both less than 5%, satisfying the Korean standard. Furthermore, the composite CLT (3 ply) of which the entire outer surface of the tension laminae was reinforced with a CFRP plate (thickness: 1.2 mm) showed a mean MOE and a mean MOR higher by 27% and 48%, respectively, than those of the unreinforced CLT (3ply). Furthermore, even though the weight of this CFRP composite CLT was smaller than that of 5ply CLT by approximately 40%, its bending moment was measured higher by 14% than that of the 5 ply CLT (thickness: 175 mm) fabricated by limited state design as specified in PRG-320. © 2019 by the Society of Wood Science and Technology.",
      "keywords": [
        "Bending strength",
        "Carbon fiber-reinforced plastic (CFRP)",
        "Composite CLT floor",
        "Cross-laminated timber (CLT)",
        "Delamination test",
        "Japanese larch"
      ],
      "studyType": "Article",
      "quality": null,
