        {
          "id": "scopus_20",
          "title": "Shear performance of timber-bamboo composite joints in hybrid cross-laminated timber (HCLT) ribbed floor system",
          "authors": "Yin, Tianxiao and Wang, Zhiqiang and Reynolds, Thomas P.S.",
          "affiliations": "Nanjing Forestry University, College of Materials Science and Engineering, Nanjing, Jiangsu, China; The University of Edinburgh, Institute for Infrastructure and Environment, Edinburgh, Scotland, United Kingdom",
          "countries": [
            "China",
            "United Kingdom"
          ],
          "journal": "Journal of Building Engineering",
          "year": 2026,
          "score": 17,
          "abstract": "Conventional cross-laminated timber (CLT) may encounter difficulties when achieving spans of more than 6 m, resulting in a significant increase in cost. The development of new types of hybrid CLT (HCLT) and timber-bamboo composite can provide reliable potential solutions for this situation. In this paper, connections for a new timber-bamboo composite floor system, made of HCLT and bamboo scrimber were assessed. Different connection configurations were compared, including use of adhesive on interface, the insertion methods and parameters of fully threaded self-tapping screws (STS). Load-carrying capacity, slip modulus and ductility ratio of joints were evaluated through experimental push-out tests. The prediction models in Eurocode 5 and Canadian standard CSA-O86 were employed to predict the load-carrying capacity of joints for comparison with experimental values. The results showed that the load-carrying capacity and slip modulus of joints using one-component polyurethane (1C-PUR) was significantly higher than those using screws, but the ductility ratio was relatively lower. Joints with inclined screws showed higher load-carrying capacity and slip modulus than those with horizontal screws, while the ductility ratio of joints with horizontally inserted screws was higher. The current models showed underestimation of load-carrying capacity of joints connected by screws. The results of this study provide a reference for the subsequent modification of theoretical models and the future development of HCLT-bamboo composite floor systems. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd",
          "keywords": [
            "Bamboo scrimber",
            "Hybrid cross-laminated timber",
            "Mechanical properties",
            "Self-tapping screws",
            "Theoretical model"
          ],
          "studyType": "Article",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.1016/j.jobe.2025.114834",
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Scopus",
