        },
        {
          "id": "scopus_99",
          "title": "The effect of the floor-wall interaction on the post-elastic rocking behaviour of segmented CLT shear walls",
          "authors": "D'Arenzo, Giuseppe and Seim, Werner",
          "affiliations": "Aarhus Universitet, Department of Civil and Architectural Engineering, Aarhus, Midtjylland, Denmark; Universität Kassel, Timber Structures and Building Rehabilitation, Kassel, Hessen, Germany",
          "countries": [
            "Denmark",
            "Germany"
          ],
          "journal": "Engineering Structures",
          "year": 2025,
          "score": 28,
          "abstract": "This paper investigates the impact of floor-wall interaction on the lateral rocking behaviour of two-panel segmented Cross-Laminated Timber (CLT) shear walls through experimental testing and numerical analyses. The study extends a simplified equivalent spring modelling approach to the post-elastic domain to accurately simulate the effects of floor-wall interaction. Experimental withdrawal tests on four types of floor-to-wall connections, utilizing Fully Threaded Screws (FTSs) and Partially Threaded Screws (PTSs) at varying inclinations, revealed that FTSs exhibited higher stiffness and load-carrying capacity but lower deformation capacity compared to PTSs. The maximum force of FTS connections was about twice that of PTS connections, while PTS connections achieved ultimate displacements up to seven times greater. Numerical analyses using a parametric framework showed that floor-wall interaction increases the stiffness and lateral load-carrying capacity of segmented shear walls, with a significant reduction in deformation capacity. These analyses also demonstrated that the equivalent spring can effectively replicate the influence of floor-wall interaction on shear wall lateral response. Expressions for calculating the strength and ductility of this equivalent spring were developed, considering a limited set of geometric and mechanical parameters. These expressions provide a simplified yet accurate method for estimating the effects of floor-wall interaction. The findings suggest that understanding the interaction between floor and wall elements is crucial for optimizing the performance of segmented CLT shear walls in seismic and lateral load scenarios. © 2024 The Authors",
          "keywords": [
            "Cross-laminated timber",
            "Diaphragm interaction",
            "Experimental tests",
            "Floor-to-wall connection",
            "Multi-panel shear-walls",
            "Rocking behaviour",
            "Seismic design"
          ],
          "studyType": "Article",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.1016/j.engstruct.2024.119212",
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Scopus",
          "numString": 2,
          "isDuplicate": false,
          "duplicateOf": null,
          "dataProcessingStatus": "pending",
          "filter1Status": "pending",
          "filter2Status": "pending",
          "filter3Status": "pending",
          "exclusionReason": null,
          "inclusionCriterion": null,
          "exclusionCriterion": null,
          "extractionCriteria": null,
          "searchString": "(( \"cross laminated timber\" OR \"cross-laminated timber\" OR “x-lam” OR CLT) AND ( slab OR floor OR roof ))",
          "importDate": "2026-02-23T22:36:57.999Z",
          "lastModified": "2026-02-23T22:36:57.999Z"
        },
        {
          "id": "scopus_100",
          "title": "Bending performance of domestic larch cross-laminated timber reinforced with fiber-reinforced polymer",
          "authors": "Baek, Seungyoup and Song, Yojin and Hong, Soonil",
          "affiliations": "Kangwon National University, Department of Forest Biomaterials Engineering, Chuncheon, Gangwon-do, South Korea",
          "countries": [
            "South Korea"
          ],
          "journal": "Wood Material Science and Engineering",
          "year": 2025,
          "score": 78,
          "abstract": "The added fiber-reinforced polymer (FRP) to cross-laminated timber (CLT) panels is expected to enable cost down and light-weight of the floor slab and improve bending performance. In this study, FRP was reinforced in Larix kaempferi 5-layer CLT, which was two types FRP and three types of reinforcement locations. The FRPs used Glass Fiber Cloth (GFC) and Carbon Fiber Reinforced Plastic sheet (CFRP). The reinforcement locations were the outermost tensile part, between the outermost tensile layer and the Minor strength direction layer, and between all layers. The bending performance of FRP-reinforced CLTs was evaluated and compared with unreinforced CLTs (control). The specimens reinforced with FRP between the outermost tensile layer and the minor strength direction layer had delamination, decreasing the modulus of rupture (MOR). The specimen with reinforced GFC between all layers improved bending performance but had delamination. The specimen reinforced with GFC in the outermost tensile component of the CLT improved bending performance but had tensile failure. However, CFRP has suppressed the tensile failure of CLT and improved bending performance by introducing consistent rolling shear failure. The reinforcement of CFRP on the outermost tensile section of CLT resulted in a 57% improvement in MOR compared to the control. © 2024 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.",
          "keywords": [
            "carbon fiber reinforced plastic (CFRP) sheet",
            "cross-laminated timber (CLT)",
            "glass fiber cloth (GFC)",
            "Larix kaempferi carr",
            "MOR"
          ],
          "studyType": "Review",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.1080/17480272.2024.2374380",
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Scopus",
          "numString": 2,
          "isDuplicate": false,
          "duplicateOf": null,
          "dataProcessingStatus": "pending",
          "filter1Status": "pending",
          "filter2Status": "pending",
          "filter3Status": "pending",
          "exclusionReason": null,
          "inclusionCriterion": null,
          "exclusionCriterion": null,
          "extractionCriteria": null,
          "searchString": "(( \"cross laminated timber\" OR \"cross-laminated timber\" OR “x-lam” OR CLT) AND ( slab OR floor OR roof ))",
          "importDate": "2026-02-23T22:36:57.999Z",
          "lastModified": "2026-02-23T22:36:57.999Z"
        },
        {
          "id": "scopus_101",
          "title": "ANCHORING OF CROSS-LAMINATED TIMBER SHEAR WALLS",
          "authors": "Velebil, Lukáš and Matějka, Šimon and Kuklík, Petr and Pošta, Jan and Hataj, Martin and Jochman, Jan",
          "affiliations": "Czech Technical University in Prague, Prague, Czech Republic",
          "countries": [
            "Czechia"
          ],
          "journal": "Wood Research",
          "year": 2025,
          "score": 18,
          "abstract": "This paper presents a validated finite element model of a cross-laminated timber (CLT) wall-to-floor connection and compares its predictions with results from a full-scale shear wall test. The CLT wall and floor panels are modelled as orthotropic shell elements, while the discrete connectors (tension anchors, angle brackets and the screw connection between floor panels) are represented by line hinges with nonlinear load-slip relationships.These relationships are derived from dedicated component tests on tension anchors, shear brackets and screw head embedment. The model is analysed in nonlinear static steps corresponding to the experimental loading sequence. Vertical slip along the wall-to-floor interfaces and the global lateral load-displacement response of the wall are extracted and compared with measurements. The results show that the simplified line-hinge representation reproduces both the global stiffness and the local deformation pattern in the joints with satisfactory accuracy for design-oriented analyses. © 2025 Pulp and Paper Research Institute. All rights reserved.",
          "keywords": [
