        {
          "id": "scopus_107",
          "title": "Performance of CLT-concrete composite slabs made with South African Timber",
          "authors": "Teixeira, Damiano Luis and der Merwe, J. E. and Roth, Chris P.",
          "affiliations": "University of Pretoria, Department of Civil Engineering, Pretoria, Gauteng, South Africa",
          "countries": [
            "South Africa"
          ],
          "journal": "Engineering Materials, Structures, Systems and Methods for a More Sustainable Future",
          "year": 2025,
          "score": 19,
          "abstract": "The use of timber-concrete composite (TCC) floors constructed with cross-laminated timber (CLT) is increasing worldwide. Such systems rely on the integrity of the horizontal shear con-nection along the interface between the two materials to ensure composite action. Although several stu-dies have investigated these structural composites, none report on the behaviour of such systems using faster-growing timber species that are typical in continental Africa. This study addresses this gap by considering South African Pine (softwood) and Eucalyptus (hardwood) CLT configurations. Results highlight the benefit of considering hardwood as part of the CLT configuration. With the high cost associated with engineered screws, which are typical for such floor structures, its adoption might be hindered locally. This paper reports experimental research results that consider a five-layer CLT-concrete composite (CCC) floors, focusing on the quasi-static flexural response of a notch connection with and without metallic fasteners. © 2025 selection and editorial matter, Alphose Zingoni; individual chapters, the contributors. All rights reserved.",
          "keywords": [
            "Bending stiffness",
            "Cross-laminated timber",
            "Horizontal shear",
            "Softwood-Hardwood",
            "Timber-concrete composites"
          ],
