        },
        {
          "id": "scopus_141",
          "title": "Vibration Assessment and Numerical Modelling of Mass Timber Composite Floors: Insights From an Experimental-Numerical Campaign",
          "authors": "Malek, Sardar and Cheraghi-Shirazi, Najmeh and Setiawan, Fendy and Creagh, Ariel and Khoshkbari, Parham",
          "affiliations": "University of Victoria, Department of Civil Engineering, Victoria, BC, Canada; Degenkolb Engineers, San Francisco, CA, United States; Degenkolb Engineers, San Francisco, CA, United States; Google LLC, Mountain View, CA, United States",
          "countries": [
            "Canada",
            "United States"
          ],
          "journal": "Proceedings from the 14th World Conference on Timber Engineering: Advancing Timber for the Future Built Environment, WCTE 2025",
          "year": 2025,
          "score": 4,
          "abstract": "Mass timber construction is increasingly favored by developers and architects due to sustainability concerns when compared to conventional concrete and steel systems. Several design guides worldwide assist practitioners in utilizing mass timber products in buildings effectively. However, the methodologies for assessing vibration in mass timber floors proposed by these guidelines have not been thoroughly validated in real-world scenarios. Existing methodologies often rely on laboratory tests, which may not be directly applicable to the vibration design of actual buildings. Recently, an experimental-numerical campaign has been conducted on real buildings to investigate these methodologies. The test data from a range of vibration tests (hammer, heel-drop, and human walking tests) in real mass timber buildings have been compared to modeling predictions and results are briefly described here. The influence of parameters and assumptions outlined in the U.S. mass timber design guide on the dynamic response of the tested floors was carefully investigated and a summary of key findings from parametric studies is also presented. The effects of timber-to-timber and timber-to-concrete connections, as well as different boundary conditions such as beam-to-beam, beam-to-column, and adjacent bays, along with considerations in flexural stiffness calculations, are quantified and discussed. © (2025) WORLD CONFERENCE ON TIMBER ENGINEERING 2025 (WCTE 2025) All rights reserved.",
          "keywords": [
            "Cross-Laminated Timber (CLT)",
            "Finite element analysis (FEA)",
            "Mass timber",
            "Standards",
            "Vibration"
          ],
          "studyType": "Conference paper",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.52202/080513-0314",
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Scopus",
          "numString": 2,
          "isDuplicate": false,
          "duplicateOf": null,
          "dataProcessingStatus": "pending",
          "filter1Status": "pending",
          "filter2Status": "pending",
          "filter3Status": "pending",
          "exclusionReason": null,
          "inclusionCriterion": null,
          "exclusionCriterion": null,
          "extractionCriteria": null,
          "searchString": "(( \"cross laminated timber\" OR \"cross-laminated timber\" OR “x-lam” OR CLT) AND ( slab OR floor OR roof ))",
          "importDate": "2026-02-23T22:36:58.005Z",
          "lastModified": "2026-02-23T22:36:58.005Z"
        },
        {
          "id": "scopus_142",
          "title": "Vibration Tests on Long-Span CLT-GLT Composite Floors",
          "authors": "Nabati, Amin and Ou, Sovanroth and Ijeoma, Muzan Williams and Pang, Weichiang and Stoner, Michael W. and Ross, Brandon E. and Albright, Dustin and Layton, Patricia A. and Carbajales-Dale, Michael A.",
          "affiliations": "College of Engineering, Computing and Applied Sciences, Clemson, SC, United States; College of Engineering, Computing and Applied Sciences, Clemson, SC, United States; School of Architecture, Clemson, SC, United States; Clemson University, Department of Forestry and Environmental Conservation, Clemson, SC, United States",
          "countries": [
            "United States"
          ],
          "journal": "Proceedings from the 14th World Conference on Timber Engineering: Advancing Timber for the Future Built Environment, WCTE 2025",
          "year": 2025,
          "score": 17,
          "abstract": "In this study, the vibrational behaviour of two full-scale composite floor systems, each 12.2 m (40 ft) long, made of Cross-Laminated Timber (CLT) and Glued-Laminated Timber (GLT) with office use under heel drop excitation has been investigated. The primary aims include studying composite structures' natural frequencies, mode shapes, and damping. Additionally, the effects of openings in GLT and CLT on vibrational behaviour will be discussed. Experimental vibration tests are conducted for two composite floor systems with different types of connections, including screws with glue and screw with sharp metal before and after making the opening. The results show that openings in CLT and GLT have negligible effects on the vibration behaviour of the composite system. © (2025) WORLD CONFERENCE ON TIMBER ENGINEERING 2025 (WCTE 2025) All rights reserved.",
          "keywords": [
            "CLT",
            "Composite floor",
            "GLT",
            "heel-drop",
            "Timber",
            "Vibration"
          ],
          "studyType": "Conference paper",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.52202/080513-0054",
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Scopus",
          "numString": 2,
          "isDuplicate": false,
