        {
          "id": "scopus_169",
          "title": "STEEL PLATE CONNECTIONS FOR TWO-WAY SPANNING CLT-CONCRETE COMPOSITE FLOORS",
          "authors": "Chaboki, Hamid Reza and Zhou, Jianhui and Tannert, Thomas",
          "affiliations": "University of Northern British Columbia, School of Engineering, Prince George, BC, Canada",
          "countries": [
            "Canada"
          ],
          "journal": "Proceedings from the 14th World Conference on Timber Engineering: Advancing Timber for the Future Built Environment, WCTE 2025",
          "year": 2025,
          "score": 28,
          "abstract": "In this study, steel plate connections for two-way spanning cross-laminated timber (CLT)-concrete composite floors (CLTCC) were investigated. Initially, the impact of shear connections between the CLT panel and the concrete layer in both strength directions, as well as the moment connection between CLT panels in the minor strength direction, on the structural performance of point-supported CLTCC floors were studied numerically. Subsequently, shear tests were conducted for the connection properties of the proposed steel plate connections in both major and minor directions. Additionally, four-point bending tests were performed on both CLT and CLTCC beams in the minor strength direction, featuring a moment joint at mid-span. The numerical results indicated that the two-way action of the CLTCC floors was predominantly influenced by the connection shear and rotational stiffness along the minor strength axis. Experimental results showed that the T-bar steel plate with self-tapping screws provided biaxial shear transfer for two-way spanning CLTCC. The bending performance of CLT beams in the minor strength direction with a bottom laminated veneer lumber spline and a top steel T-bar plate approached that of continuous CLT. CLTCC beams with the steel T-bar plate serving as both the shear and moment connections reached approximately three times the bending moment capacity and nine times the bending stiffness compared to bare CLT. © (2025) WORLD CONFERENCE ON TIMBER ENGINEERING 2025 (WCTE 2025) All rights reserved.",
          "keywords": [
            "Cross-laminated timber",
            "Steel kerf plates",
            "Timber-concrete composite"
          ],
          "studyType": "Conference paper",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.52202/080513-0393",
          "language": "English",
