        {
          "id": "scopus_340",
          "title": "LARGE-SCALE MULTIDIRECTIONAL CYCLIC TESTING OF A RESILIENT SC-CLT WALL SUB-ASSEMBLY",
          "authors": "Amer, Alia and Sause, Richard S. and Ricles, James M.",
          "affiliations": "Lehigh University, Bethlehem, PA, United States",
          "countries": [
            "United States"
          ],
          "journal": "World Conference on Earthquake Engineering proceedings",
          "year": 2024,
          "score": 18,
          "abstract": "A post-tensioned self-centering (SC) controlled-rocking CLT shear wall (SC-CLT wall) has potential to be a low-damage lateral-load resisting system for seismically resilient buildings. Previous research focused primarily on the lateral-load response of isolated self-centering timber walls under unidirectional loading. However, to achieve seismically resilient buildings, improved knowledge of the lateral-load response and damage of the SC-CLT walls under multidirectional loading, and the capability of floor diaphragm and collector beam connections to accommodate the multidirectional building seismic response including the controlled-rocking response of the SC-CLT walls, is needed. This paper presents an experimental study on the multidirectional cyclic lateral-load response and damage of SC-CLT walls and the associated response of the floor diaphragm and collector beam connections within a 0.625-scale three-dimensional timber sub-assembly. The paper presents the multidirectional displacement control scheme and associated control algorithm to overcome the flexibilities in the test sub-assembly and achieve effective multidirectional displacement control of large-scale three-dimensional lateral-load test sub-assemblies. © 2024, International Association for Earthquake Engineering. All rights reserved.",
