        {
          "id": "scopus_345",
          "title": "EFFECT OF SEISMIC LOAD INTENSITY ON DESIGN OF CONNECTIONS IN CLT BUILDINGS-PARAMETRIC STUDY",
          "authors": "Lukić, Iva and Azinović, Boris and Naumovski, Luka and Premrov, Miroslav and Žegarac Leskovar, Vesna",
          "affiliations": "Technische Universitat Graz, Graz, Styria, Austria; Slovenian National Building and Civil Engineering Institute, Ljubljana, Slovenia; Univerza v Mariboru, Transportation Engineering and Architecture, Maribor, Slovenia",
          "countries": [
            "Austria",
            "Slovenia"
          ],
          "journal": "World Conference on Earthquake Engineering proceedings",
          "year": 2024,
          "score": 17,
          "abstract": "Cross-laminated timber (CLT) has become an increasingly important material for load-bearing structures of mid to high-rise buildings and is considered a sustainable alternative to traditional building materials, even in earthquake-prone regions. Because the connections between CLT panels are the main source of energy dissipation in the platform type CLT structures, it is common for similar structures in earthquake-prone regions to require a greater number of connections (i.e., connectors and fasteners) than in non-earthquake-prone regions. This is also because seismic action is usually a part of the decisive lateral load combination in the design of CLT structures in earthquake-prone regions. In this study, we investigate CLT structures with the same floor plan, but different number of stories (up to 8) subjected to different seismic actions (ag up to 0.25 g). Each structural model was designed according to Eurocode 8, considering medium ductility class. In addition, a modal response spectrum analysis was performed for each model. A total of 160 models were designed and the corresponding types and quantities of fasteners and connectors were accurately determined. It was found that the connections contribute up to 2.5% of the total weight of the structure in the case of an 8-story building designed on ag=0.25g (soil type E), while the same structure on areas with ag=0.1g (soil type A) requires significantly fewer connections (i.e., 0.5% of the total weight of the structure). The results of the structural analyses in terms of the amount of connection material can be further used in the life cycle assessment and life cycle cost estimation. © 2024, International Association for Earthquake Engineering. All rights reserved.",
