        },
        {
          "id": "scopus_349",
          "title": "A COMPARATIVE DESIGN AND LCA STUDY ON THE FEASIBILITY OF EARTHQUAKE-RESISTANT MASS-TIMBER BUILDINGS",
          "authors": "Sener, Kadir C. and Mutlu, Erkan Okay and Aníl, Özgür",
          "affiliations": "Auburn University, Auburn, AL, United States; Gazi Üniversitesi, Ankara, Ankara, Turkey",
          "countries": [
            "United States",
            "Turkey"
          ],
          "journal": "World Conference on Earthquake Engineering proceedings",
          "year": 2024,
          "score": 13,
          "abstract": "The building and construction industry drives the largest portion of global energy use and emissions. With climate change mitigation being a forefront topic of concern, the building and construction industry has a responsibility to alleviate its environmental impact, particularly in developing countries with a need for sustainable structural systems that explicitly consider environmental impact. Mass timber has recently emerged as a viable alternative to conventional construction materials and gained significant popularity in many developed nations. Recent design code advancements on mass timber elements and systems have paved the way for their use as multi-story building systems in seismic-prone regions. The devastating earthquakes that took place on February 3, 2023, in Turkiye have showcased the poor performance of the reinforced concrete building inventory. As the earthquakes affected a populated province where a vast majority of buildings sustained significant damage, hundreds of thousands of new buildings are expected to be constructed in the near term. Considering 93% of Turkiye’s population resides in considerably high earthquake-hazard regions, this rebuilding process gives a rare opportunity for constructing buildings with low-carbon structural solutions that utilize sustainable materials as well as modularity and prefabrication practices, therefore enabling the speedy constructability needed for the region. To evaluate the feasibility and potential benefits of seismic-resistant multi-story mass timber structures in Turkiye, this study conducts a comparative study between functionally equivalent reinforced concrete and mass timber buildings. The buildings use generic floor plans obtained from governmental agencies in Turkiye that mainly utilize shear wall systems for both gravity and lateral load resistance. The objective of the study was to investigate the design, lateral load performance, and economic aspects of the systems with a particular emphasis on sustainability through conducting a comparative life cycle assessment of mass timber relative to conventional reinforced concrete systems using realistic building layouts. The designed timber structure showcased adequate performance under seismic loads and was found to be considerably more environmentally advantageous compared to the companion reinforced concrete building. The mass-timber building resulted being more expensive based on material cost, but the difference is expected to shrink with more widespread CLT manufacturing in future. © 2024, International Association for Earthquake Engineering. All rights reserved.",
          "keywords": [
            "Concrete buildings",
            "Concrete construction",
            "Earthquake effects",
            "Mortar",
            "Seismic design",
            "Structural dynamics",
            "Wooden buildings",
            "Building and construction",
            "Climate change mitigation",
            "Earthquake resistant",
            "Energy emissions",
            "Energy use",
            "Global energy",
            "Lateral loads",
            "Reinforced concrete buildings",
            "Timber buildings",
            "Timber structures",
            "Shear walls"
          ],
