        {
          "id": "scopus_358",
          "title": "A dynamic stiffness method to estimate the sound insulation of arbitrarily installed thick cross laminated timber (CLT)",
          "authors": "Churchill, Claire and Meggitt, Joshua W.R. and Wong-McSweeney, Daniel B.C. and Cox, Trevor J. and Filippoupolitis, Marios and Woodcock, James S.",
          "affiliations": "University of Salford, Acoustics Research Centre, Salford, Greater Manchester, United Kingdom; Arup Group Limited, London, United Kingdom",
          "countries": [
            "United Kingdom"
          ],
          "journal": "53rd International Congress and Exposition on Noise Control Engineering, Internoise 2024",
          "year": 2024,
          "score": 18,
          "abstract": "There is no generally accepted standard methodology to determine the dynamic constants of whole cross laminated timber (CLT) plates and CLT walls or floors are installed in situ under very different conditions to laboratory measurement conditions. Therefore, a methodology is sought to determine the sound insulation of CLT plates installed under arbitrary conditions without accurate information concerning the dynamic constants (and boundary conditions). The methodology may have applications where a panel can be tested in situ e.g. in pre-completion testing. In the laboratory measurement a CLT plate was mounted on the laboratory floor on two wooden joists placed at arbitrary positions to support the weight of the plate to represent a possible installation in situ. © 2024 by Societe Francaise d’Acoustique. All Rights Reserved.",
