        },
        {
          "id": "scopus_524",
          "title": "LABORATORY MEASUREMENTS OF LIGHTWEIGHT FLOATING FLOOR SYSTEMS ON CROSS-LAMINATED TIMBER (CLT) SLABS",
          "authors": "Rodrigues, Marina and Motal, Tomáš and Horváth, Zoltán",
          "affiliations": "CDM Stravitec, Brussels, Belgium; CDM Stravitec, Prague, Czech Republic; CDM Stravitec, Budapest, Hungary",
          "countries": [
            "Belgium",
            "Czechia",
            "Hungary"
          ],
          "journal": "Proceedings of the International Congress on Sound and Vibration",
          "year": 2023,
          "score": 28,
          "abstract": "Mass timber solutions, including cross-laminated timber (CLT), are an excellent substitute to the more traditional fossil fuel-intensive construction materials. The use of these mass timber products allows for the reduction of the carbon footprint of the built environment. Nevertheless, the use of mass timber poses some rather unique challenges in terms of acoustics. Moreover, one of the most desired aspects of mass timber construction is the ability to leave a building's structure exposed as finish, which creates the need for asymmetric assemblies. Consequently, when talking about floor/ceiling applications, it is preferable that any acoustic component be installed on top of the assembly. One of the main advantages of mass timber is the strength-to-weight ratio, which allows for lighter setups than would be possible with other slab-type building materials. This reduces the overall mass of the building, which in turn offers many other benefits, such as reduced seismic forces and smaller building foundation requirements. To maintain these benefits and reduce the carbon footprint of the building, adding the mass of a concrete slab to improve the acoustic performance is not the best solution, leaving only the option of adding decouplers. To measure airborne sound reduction and impact noise isolation of lightweight floating floor systems and confirm that CLT-assemblies can provide satisfactory sound insulation, CDM Stravitec conducted a test campaign at the Belgian Building Research Institute. During this campaign, different types of decouplers were tested, including discrete bearings with various thicknesses and/or different void depths (filled entirely or partially with insulation material) and resilient mats and strips placed between the CLT slab and the different timber, cement and gypsum-based boards and this with and without the use of the well-known constrained layer damping technique. This paper presents the findings of that test campaign. © 2023 Proceedings of the International Congress on Sound and Vibration. All rights reserved.",
          "keywords": [
            "building acoustics",
            "cross-laminated timber",
            "floating floors",
            "timber construction"
          ],
          "studyType": "Conference paper",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": null,
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Scopus",
          "numString": 2,
          "isDuplicate": false,
          "duplicateOf": null,
          "dataProcessingStatus": "pending",
          "filter1Status": "pending",
          "filter2Status": "pending",
          "filter3Status": "pending",
          "exclusionReason": null,
          "inclusionCriterion": null,
          "exclusionCriterion": null,
          "extractionCriteria": null,
          "searchString": "(( \"cross laminated timber\" OR \"cross-laminated timber\" OR “x-lam” OR CLT) AND ( slab OR floor OR roof ))",
          "importDate": "2026-02-23T22:36:58.060Z",
          "lastModified": "2026-02-23T22:36:58.060Z"
        },
        {
          "id": "scopus_525",
          "title": "IMPACT NOISE REDUCTION OF CROSS LAMINATED TIMBER FLOOR",
          "authors": "Caniato, Marco and Bettarello, Federica and Granzotto, Nicola and Marzi, Arianna and Gasparella, Andrea",
          "affiliations": "Free University of Bozen-Bolzano, Faculty of Engineering, Bolzano, BZ, Italy",
          "countries": [
            "Italy"
          ],
          "journal": "Proceedings of the International Congress on Sound and Vibration",
          "year": 2023,
          "score": 20,
          "abstract": "The demand of sustainable building materials has recently gained popularity. Particularly in some regions, multi-storey timber constructions are becoming increasingly popular. Further studies and new acoustic characterisations of these components are then of paramount importance, particularly referred to the acoustic behaviour caused by diverse impact sources on the same timber floor, as demonstrated by relevant literature. Thus, it is crucial to enhance research on horizontal timber elements using different impact sources such as the tapping machine and the rubber impact ball. This study provides a thorough acoustic characterization of a 200 mm cross-laminated timber floor, in two different conditions: bare floor and bare floor coupled with diverse floating floors. The aim is to understand the influence of both impact sources, but also to investigate if a possible correlation exists relating the tapping machine excitation to the rubber ball one. In light of this, two distinct correlations are offered, one related to (i) the bare floor and to (ii) the floating floor configuration. The importance of valid relationships between the impact noise levels produced by the two diverse sources is discussed. © 2023 Proceedings of the International Congress on Sound and Vibration. All rights reserved.",
          "keywords": [
            "no more than five words (e.g. building acoustics, finite element methods)"
          ],
          "studyType": "Conference paper",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": null,
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Scopus",
          "numString": 2,
          "isDuplicate": false,
          "duplicateOf": null,
          "dataProcessingStatus": "pending",
          "filter1Status": "pending",
          "filter2Status": "pending",
          "filter3Status": "pending",
          "exclusionReason": null,
          "inclusionCriterion": null,
          "exclusionCriterion": null,
          "extractionCriteria": null,
          "searchString": "(( \"cross laminated timber\" OR \"cross-laminated timber\" OR “x-lam” OR CLT) AND ( slab OR floor OR roof ))",
          "importDate": "2026-02-23T22:36:58.060Z",
          "lastModified": "2026-02-23T22:36:58.060Z"
        },
        {
          "id": "scopus_526",
          "title": "INFLUENCE OF DRY-TYPE DOUBLE FLOOR STRUCTURE ON FLOOR IMPACT SOUND INSULATION PERFORMANCE",
          "authors": "Hiramitsu, Atsuo and Hirakawa, Susumu",
          "affiliations": "Building Research Institute, Department of Environmental Engineering, Tsukuba, Ibaraki, Japan; Japan National Institute for Land and Infrastructure Management, Tsukuba, Ibaraki, Japan",
          "countries": [
            "Japan"
          ],
          "journal": "Proceedings of the International Congress on Sound and Vibration",
          "year": 2023,
          "score": 62,
          "abstract": "The dry-type double floor structure is often constructed on floor slabs as the floor finishing material for concrete apartment houses in Japan. This floor structure consists of a support footing with anti-vibration rubber, particle board, asphalt-based sound insulation sheets, flooring, and other top surface materials. In the case of the concrete building, the floor finishing material is effective for the light-weight floor impact sound insulation performance, however, it is not effective for the heavy-weight floor impact sound insulation performance. This is believed to the due to the mass-spring resonance of the air layer and the top surface. For the Cross Laminated Timber (CLT) building, the floor finishing material is effective for both the light-weight and the heavy-weight floor impact sound insulation performance hence the floor finishing material works differently with the different buildings. But no data was obtained from the same product installed on the different floor structures. The reduction of the transmitted floor impact sound level due to the same dry-type double floor structure was investigated with different constructions. The laboratory experiments were conducted in the CLT experimental building, a 6-storey wood-frame construction experimental building and vertical transmission suite in BRI Japan. Those are the 210 mm CLT bare floor, 210 mm CLT floor with floating floor and composite 210 mm CLT and 100 mm concrete floor and 200 mm concrete bare floor. As a result, the reduction of transmitted floor impact sound level changes depending on the driving-point impedance. The higher the driving-point impedance, the smaller the reduction of transmitted floor impact sound level was obtained. © 2023 Proceedings of the International Congress on Sound and Vibration. All rights reserved.",
          "keywords": [
            "Cross Laminated Timber",
            "driving-point impedance",
            "dry-type double floor structure",
            "floor impact sound insulation",
            "reduction of transmitted impact sound by floor coverings"
          ],
          "studyType": "Conference paper",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": null,
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Scopus",
          "numString": 2,
          "isDuplicate": false,
          "duplicateOf": null,
          "dataProcessingStatus": "pending",
          "filter1Status": "pending",
          "filter2Status": "pending",
