        {
          "id": "scopus_546",
          "title": "Experimental Investigations of Full-Scale Cross-Laminated Timber Concrete Composite Floors",
          "authors": "Shahnewaz, Md and Jackson, Robert and Tannert, Thomas",
          "affiliations": "Fast + Epp, Vancouver, BC, Canada; University of Northern British Columbia, Prince George, BC, Canada",
          "countries": [
            "Canada"
          ],
          "journal": "Lecture Notes in Civil Engineering",
          "year": 2023,
          "score": 27,
          "abstract": "Experimental investigations on full-scale Timber Concrete Composite (TCC) floor systems with various composite connectors are presented in this paper. The stiffness, strength, and failure modes of were evaluated. The 9.2 m long and 2.4 m wide TCC floor segments were comprised of 245 mm thick, 7-ply Cross-laminated Timber (CLT) panels with 150 mm concrete topping connected with three types of shear connectors: (i) self-tapping screws, (ii) steel kerf plates, and (iii) glued-in Holz-Beton-Verbund (HBV) plates. Six TCC floor segments were tested to failure under symmetric four-point bending and three TCC floor segments were tested under torsional bending by applying eccentric loading near the edge. The floor deformations at nine locations and connector slips at CLT-concrete interfaces at eight locations along the length of the floor were measured. The full-scale tests showed that the steel kerf plates—for the selected connector configurations- exhibited the highest capacity and stiffness. © 2023, Canadian Society for Civil Engineering.",
          "keywords": [
            "Concretes",
            "Laminated composites",
