        {
          "id": "scopus_581",
          "title": "Larch and living: Contemporary wooden house in Leipzig-Lindenau; Lärche und Lebensart: Zeitgenössischer Holzbau in Leipzig-Lindenau",
          "authors": "Heuer, Antje and Stenzel, Dirk",
          "affiliations": "Afiliação não informada",
          "countries": "País não informado",
          "journal": "Zeitschrift fur Herz-, Thorax- und Gefasschirurgie",
          "year": 2022,
          "score": 7,
          "abstract": "With a 5-storey residential and commercial building, architect Dirk Stenzel from the Atelier for Strategic and Sustainable Architecture (ASUNA) impressively shows the possibilities of contemporary timber construction. The new building was built on a site that had previously been undeveloped for a long time. At a prominent location in the urban space, it marks an acute-angled corner of the district in Leipzig’s Lindenau district, four resident parties and a large specialist shop find space to live and work here. The architect brings a raw material into focus in a new way. The material wood is light and highly resilient, grows back in large quantities, is easy to process and convert with little energy consumption. Prefabricated elements enable optimized construction processes regardless of the weather. Above the foundation slab the house was built in just 5 weeks. Wood creates a good room climate and an attitude to life. In addition, it can even still be used if the house is demolished one day. The house can be seen as an ecological model because 1m<sup>3</sup> of wood binds 1 ton of carbon dioxide. A total of 520m<sup>3</sup> are built into the new building. The outer walls, ceilings and roof are made of cross-laminated timber elements, while columns and transoms are made of glued laminated timber. The house has received multiple awards, including the Saxony State Prize for Building Culture and the Saxony Timber Construction Prize in 2019. It acts as a statement not a dogma. It shows an architectural solution, an attitude and a way of living. The work not only demonstrates the suitability of the material for the future it above all testifies to a free spirit and a willingness to experiment. It advocates independent thinking, about the contemporary use of a material, about building, about life. © 2022, The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Medizin Verlag GmbH, ein Teil von Springer Nature.",
          "keywords": [
            "ASUNA",
            "Cross laminated timber",
            "Dirk Stenzel",
            "Saxon State Prize for Building Culture 2019",
            "Wooden houses"
          ],
          "studyType": "Note",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.1007/s00398-021-00486-3",
          "language": "German",
          "source": "Scopus",
          "numString": 2,
