        },
        {
          "id": "scopus_677",
          "title": "Lightweight and slender timber-concrete composite floors made of CLT-HPC and CLT-UHPC with ductile notch connectors",
          "authors": "Lamothe, Serge and Sorelli, Luca G. and Blanchet, Pierre and Galimard, Philippe",
          "affiliations": "Université Laval, Department of Civil Engineering, Quebec, QC, Canada; Université Laval, Department of Wood and Forest Sciences, Quebec, QC, Canada; Université de Bordeaux, Department of Construction Timber Engineering, Bordeaux, Nouvelle-Aquitaine, France",
          "countries": [
            "Canada",
            "France"
          ],
          "journal": "Engineering Structures",
          "year": 2021,
          "score": 42,
          "abstract": "Cross-Laminated Timber (CLT) structures have been emerging worldwide for residential floors in multi-storey buildings thanks to their lightness, fast construction and low ecological footprint. This work aims at fostering this application, which is often limited by vibrational and deflection limits, by investigating composite slab floors made of CLT and High-Performance Concrete (HPC) slab as well as CLT and Ultra High-Performance Fiber Reinforced Concrete (UHPFRC). Firstly, the composite floors CLT-HPC and CLT-UHPFRC with a span of 8 m were designed by considering a multicriteria analysis. To assure a certain structural ductility, previously developed ductile notch connectors were employed. As an economic choice, no shear reinforcement in the concrete slab was employed. Then, full-scale composite beams were fabricated in order to verify the predicted flexural behaviour and natural frequency. A numerical analysis was carried out to verify the connectors could effectively yield before the timber collapse. The comparison between the numerical simulation and the slip measurements confirmed that about 50% of the notch connections fully yielded and underwent inelastic deformation which favors the structural ductility. In the case of the CLT-HPC floor, a reduction of the notch contact surface due to the use of plastic sheet waterproofing as well as shear cracks developing in the concrete close to the notch corner both reduced the expected structural stiffness. Finally, the CLT-UHPFRC floor is endowed with outstanding values of slenderness ratio (∼35) and lightness (∼2 kPa), while eliminating the use of shear reinforcement and sheet waterproofing. © 2021 Elsevier Ltd",
          "keywords": [
            "Cross Laminated Timber (CLT)",
            "Ductile Notch Connections",
            "High Performance Concrete (HPC)",
            "Timber-concrete composite structures",
            "Ultra-High Fiber Performance Concrete (UHPFRC)"
          ],
          "studyType": "Article",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.1016/j.engstruct.2021.112409",
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Scopus",
          "numString": 2,
          "isDuplicate": false,
          "duplicateOf": null,
          "dataProcessingStatus": "pending",
          "filter1Status": "pending",
          "filter2Status": "pending",
          "filter3Status": "pending",
          "exclusionReason": null,
          "inclusionCriterion": null,
          "exclusionCriterion": null,
          "extractionCriteria": null,
          "searchString": "(( \"cross laminated timber\" OR \"cross-laminated timber\" OR “x-lam” OR CLT) AND ( slab OR floor OR roof ))",
          "importDate": "2026-02-23T22:36:58.085Z",
          "lastModified": "2026-02-23T22:36:58.085Z"
        },
        {
          "id": "scopus_678",
          "title": "Experimental study on the system effect of bending cross-laminated timber fabricated with Karamatsu larch",
          "authors": "He, Minjuan and Sun, Xiaofeng and Ren, Haiqing and Li, Zheng and Feng, Wei",
          "affiliations": "Tongji University, Department of Structural Engineering, Shanghai, China; Chinese Academy of Forestry, Beijing, China",
          "countries": [
            "China"
          ],
          "journal": "Construction and Building Materials",
          "year": 2021,
          "score": 58,
          "abstract": "Cross-laminated timber (CLT) is recognized as a code-compliant construction material, which is commonly used as bending members (e.g., floor diaphragm and roofs) of mid- and high-rise timber structures. For one CLT bending panel that performs as an orthotropic plate, its transverse laminas can be regarded as a continuous load-distribution system, which connects the equally spaced longitudinal laminas. Therefore, for one CLT panel with more longitudinal laminas in its outermost layers, a higher bending strength might be resulted in due to the system effect, which is denoted by the system strength factor. In this study, for investigating the system effect of CLT panels, bending tests were conducted on 3-layer non-edge-glued CLT panels, which consisted of 3 longitudinal laminas, 5 longitudinal laminas, and 7 longitudinal laminas in their outermost layers, respectively. All the CLT were fabricated with the Karamatsu larch grown in China, which can be served as a potential wood species for CLT manufacturing. Then, their bending resisting properties and failure modes were analyzed. The bending strength or the flexural modulus of the Karamatsu larch CLT was evaluated. Finally, the system effect of the CLT bending strength was analyzed; the system strength factor for the CLT with 3 laminas, 5 laminas, and 7 laminas in its outermost layers was calculated, respectively. Results show that the CLT will provide more stable bending properties, whilst be more prone to the rolling shear failure in its transverse laminas, when the number of the outermost longitudinal laminas enhances. The system effect exists for the CLT bending strength; for the Karamatsu larch CLT consisting of 3 longitudinal laminas, 5 longitudinal laminas, and 7 longitudinal laminas in its bottom layer, the corresponding system strength factor can be approximated as 0.570, 0.720, and 0.920, respectively. © 2021 Elsevier Ltd",
          "keywords": [
            "Bending test",
            "Cross-laminated timber",
            "Karamatsu larch",
            "Mechanical property",
            "System effect"
          ],
