          "exclusionCriterion": null,
          "extractionCriteria": null,
          "searchString": "(( \"cross laminated timber\" OR \"cross-laminated timber\" OR “x-lam” OR CLT) AND ( slab OR floor OR roof ))",
          "importDate": "2026-02-23T22:36:58.093Z",
          "lastModified": "2026-02-23T22:36:58.093Z"
        },
        {
          "id": "scopus_727",
          "title": "Evaluation of the seismic behavior of historical churches reinforced by cross-lam roof-structure",
          "authors": "Longarini, Nicola and Crespi, Pietro Giuseppe and Zucca, Marco",
          "affiliations": "Politecnico di Milano, Department of Architecture, Milan, MI, Italy",
          "countries": [
            "Italy"
          ],
          "journal": "COMPDYN Proceedings",
          "year": 2021,
          "score": 21,
          "abstract": "Many typical historical masonry churches, with one nave and wooden roofs, have shown high seismic vulnerability in the recent seismic events, especially under transverse earthquakes. In fact, the nave transverse response of this kind of constructions is influenced both by the materials features and the geometrical characteristics. In order to improve the seismic response, the box behavior strategy basing on dissipative roof-diaphragm, can be pursued by adopting cross laminated timber panels (CLT). In this paper, for five historical masonry churches, the effectiveness of different CLT panels solutions is investigated by performing comparative nonlinear dynamic analyses adopting equivalent finite element models. The CLT solutions differ themselves for the panels thickness (6mm and 10mm) and the number of the connections (8, 16 and 32 crews in one linear meter). The results are shown in terms of dimensionless transverse displacement (drift) and shear occurred at the base of the façade. Therefore, the influence of the most important geometrical features (as slenderness of the perimeter walls, the width and the length of the churches) in the seismic response is pointed out for each church equipped by different CLT roof-diaphragm solutions.",
          "keywords": [
            "Historical churches",
            "Roof-diaphragm",
            "Seismic vulnerability",
            "Time-history analysis"
          ],
          "studyType": "Conference paper",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": null,
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Scopus",
          "numString": 2,
          "isDuplicate": false,
          "duplicateOf": null,
          "dataProcessingStatus": "pending",
          "filter1Status": "pending",
          "filter2Status": "pending",
          "filter3Status": "pending",
          "exclusionReason": null,
          "inclusionCriterion": null,
          "exclusionCriterion": null,
          "extractionCriteria": null,
          "searchString": "(( \"cross laminated timber\" OR \"cross-laminated timber\" OR “x-lam” OR CLT) AND ( slab OR floor OR roof ))",
          "importDate": "2026-02-23T22:36:58.093Z",
          "lastModified": "2026-02-23T22:36:58.093Z"
        },
        {
          "id": "scopus_728",
          "title": "Sound insulation performance of mass timber wall and floor assemblies other than CLT",
          "authors": "Hu, Linjuan and Dagenais, Christian and Qian, Cheng and Gagnon, Sylvain",
          "affiliations": "FPInnovations, Canada, Pointe-Claire, QC, Canada",
          "countries": [
            "Canada"
          ],
          "journal": "World Conference on Timber Engineering 2021, WCTE 2021",
          "year": 2021,
          "score": 31,
          "abstract": "Lack of information on noise control of mass timber assemblies other than Cross-laminated timber (CLT), motivated this study. Laboratory tests were performed on bare CLT, Dowel-laminated timber (DLT) and Glue-laminated timber (GLT) floors with various finish and topping details for apparent sound transmission class (ASTC) and apparent impact insulation class (AIIC), and Nail-laminated timber (NLT) walls for ASTC according to ASTM standards. It was found that for similar thickness, the NLT wall with a plywood sheathing nailed to one side of the NLT had similar ASTC to CLT, for the DLT and GLT floor with a plywood sheathing nailed on their tops, the AIIC of the DLT floor was the highest, and the AIIC of the CLT floor was the lowest. The differences in the AIIC ratings were from 9-12 points. With the topping and finish, the differences in the AIIC ratings were reduced by 2-3 points. With the innovative wood raised floor on the top, developed by FPInnovations, the GLT floor achieved a 56 ASTC and AIIC. The results suggest that mass timbers can achieve high sound insulation performance through innovation. More research is underway at FPInnovations to develop solutions for sound insulation of mass timber other than CLT. © WCTE 2021. All rights reserved.",
          "keywords": [
            "Apparent impact insulation class (AIIC)",
            "Apparent sound transmission class (ASTC)",
            "Cross-laminated timber (CLT)",
            "Dowel-laminated timber (DLT)",
            "Floor",
            "Glue-laminated timber (GLT)",
            "Nail-laminated timber (GLT)",
            "Wall"
          ],
          "studyType": "Conference paper",
