          "extractionCriteria": null,
          "searchString": "(( \"cross laminated timber\" OR \"cross-laminated timber\" OR “x-lam” OR CLT) AND ( slab OR floor OR roof ))",
          "importDate": "2026-02-23T22:36:58.094Z",
          "lastModified": "2026-02-23T22:36:58.094Z"
        },
        {
          "id": "scopus_738",
          "title": "Finite element modelling of catenary action in a cross-laminated timber floor system",
          "authors": "Huber, Johannes A.J. and Mpidi Bita, Hercend and Ekevad, Mats and Tannert, Thomas",
          "affiliations": "Luleå University of Technology, Wood Science and Engineering, Lulea, Norrbotten, Sweden; Katerra, Product Structural Design Group, Vancouver, BC, Canada; University of Northern British Columbia, School of Engineering, Prince George, BC, Canada",
          "countries": [
            "Sweden",
            "Canada"
          ],
          "journal": "World Conference on Timber Engineering 2021, WCTE 2021",
          "year": 2021,
          "score": 19,
          "abstract": "Buildings taller than four storeys require the designer to mitigate consequences of unexpected events, e.g. terrorism or accidents, such that a disproportionate collapse can be avoided after initial damage. One approach to halt damage propagation is using structural robustness, i.e. by providing alternative load paths. For platform-type cross-laminated timber (CLT) buildings, catenary action as an alternative load path is not fully understood. The goal of the research presented in this paper is to numerically study catenary action as a resistance mechanism for floor panels after internal load-bearing wall removal, and to identify the governing parameters of this mechanism. For this purpose, a non-linear high-fidelity finite element model was constructed and calibrated against test results. All components and connections were individually modelled, to account for failure in the connectors and the timber components. A parameter study evaluated the impact of the floor span, the storey location, the connection type and the tie level on the development of catenary action. It was shown that in specific the connection, the storey location and the tie level had significant effects on catenary action. The results provide insight into how CLT floor systems can be detailed to trigger catenary action following internal wall removal. © WCTE 2021. All rights reserved.",
          "keywords": [
            "CLT",
            "Disproportionate collapse",
            "Finite element modelling",
            "Progressive collapse",
            "Structural robustness"
          ],
          "studyType": "Conference paper",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": null,
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Scopus",
          "numString": 2,
          "isDuplicate": false,
          "duplicateOf": null,
          "dataProcessingStatus": "pending",
          "filter1Status": "pending",
          "filter2Status": "pending",
          "filter3Status": "pending",
          "exclusionReason": null,
          "inclusionCriterion": null,
          "exclusionCriterion": null,
          "extractionCriteria": null,
          "searchString": "(( \"cross laminated timber\" OR \"cross-laminated timber\" OR “x-lam” OR CLT) AND ( slab OR floor OR roof ))",
          "importDate": "2026-02-23T22:36:58.094Z",
          "lastModified": "2026-02-23T22:36:58.094Z"
        },
        {
          "id": "scopus_739",
          "title": "Experimental analysis of a two-story three-dimensional panelized CLT module",
          "authors": "Benedetti, Franco and Rosales, Victor and Opazo-Vega, Alexander and Sepúlveda, Sebastián and Jara-Cisterna, Alan",
          "affiliations": "Universidad del Bio Bio, Department of Civil & Environmental Engineering, Concepcion, BI, Chile",
          "countries": [
            "Chile"
          ],
          "journal": "World Conference on Timber Engineering 2021, WCTE 2021",
          "year": 2021,
          "score": 14,
          "abstract": "In this study, the displacement ductility response and the degradation of the global stiffness, and the first mode frequency of a panelized 2-stories CLT structure is analyzed. This behavior is studied under lateral non-reversible cyclic loads. The structure is an I-shaped 3 dimensional module composed of 10 CLT panels formed with C16 graded radiata pine laminations. For the foundation connections and the floor-to-floor connections to different setups are considered. Results suggest that the stiffness degradation is very sensitive to the friction between CLT panels, with a rapid reduction at low displacement levels. Moreover, the first mode frequency depends on the ductility demand and the yielding of joints and connectors. With regard to the ductility demand, results suggest that for a given level of global ductility demand, the maximum local ductility is around 10% to 15% higher. © WCTE 2021. All rights reserved.",
          "keywords": [
            "Ductility response",
            "Frequency degradation",
            "Lateral load test",
            "Panelized CLT structures",
            "Stiffness"
          ],
          "studyType": "Conference paper",
