        {
          "id": "scopus_740",
          "title": "Development testing and application of mass timber composite rib floor panels for office buildings",
          "authors": "Blomgren, Hans Erik and Barbosa, Andre R. and Powers, Josh and Zimmerman, Reid B.",
          "affiliations": "Katerra, Seattle, WA, United States; Oregon State University, Corvallis, OR, United States; KPFF, Denver, CO, United States",
          "countries": [
            "United States"
          ],
          "journal": "World Conference on Timber Engineering 2021, WCTE 2021",
          "year": 2021,
          "score": 16,
          "abstract": "Developers' interest in mass timber mid-rise office building solutions in North American markets is rapidly expanding due to the benefits of construction efficiency, desirable exposed wood aesthetic, and carbon storage. Current practice of post and beam glulam frames overlaid with flat plate mass timber floor panels are limited to column grid spacings of approximately 6.1m (20ft). In 2017, to meet the request for larger column grids for a market rate open office space, Katerra engineers embarked on North America's first development, testing, and application of a lightweight and efficient factory-built composite glulam (rib) and CLT floor panel product for the five story 13,935m<sup>2</sup> (150,000ft<sup>2</sup>) Catalyst building located in Spokane, WA. These 3.1m (10ft) by 9.2m (30ft) rib floor panels are supported on glulam girder lines to achieve a 9.2m x9.2m (30ft x 30ft) building column grid spacing in the office bays. This paper describes the (i) product and process development, (ii) full-scale destructive load testing performed at Oregon State University, (iii) field installation construction method, and (iv) field floor vibration testing. © WCTE 2021. All rights reserved.",
          "keywords": [
            "CLT",
            "Glulam",
            "Office floor plates",
            "Rib floor panels",
            "Timber-timber composite"
          ],
