        {
          "id": "scopus_744",
          "title": "Drying of horizontal timber roof elements after water damage",
          "authors": "Ullah, Samee and Darvishi, Javad and Austigard, Mari Sand and Nore, Kristine",
          "affiliations": "Treteknisk, Oslo, Oslo, Norway; Mycoteam AS, Oslo, Oslo, Norway; Splitkon AS, Åmot, Norway",
          "countries": [
            "Norway"
          ],
          "journal": "World Conference on Timber Engineering 2021, WCTE 2021",
          "year": 2021,
          "score": 16,
          "abstract": "In recent years, the use of Cross Laminated Timber (CLT) has increased in Norway and the rest of Europe. Norway has long experience with wood constructions, but wood is only recently being used in large constructions. As CLT is a new material, there is not much knowledge available about the material's behaviour in various situations. There is a lot of building damages reported to insurance companies in Norway every year and an earlier study conducted by SINTEF- Byggforsk could show that 75% of the reported damages are caused by water. The aim of the study “TrebyggTørk” has been to investigate if compact roofs with laminated timber elements have an advantage when it comes to drying, in comparison with ordinary ventilated timber framed roofs. Based on the results the CLT element with vapour retarder shows the most constant and rapid decrease of moisture content and seems to be the best option among the tested elements. If the vapor retarder is completely intact, it protects the CLT from water leakages. © WCTE 2021. All rights reserved.",
          "keywords": [
            "Cross Laminated Timber (CLT)",
            "Drying process",
            "Moisture",
            "Screw Laminated Timber (SLT)",
            "Timber Framed roof system (TF)",
            "Vapor barrier",
            "Vapor retarder",
            "Weather barrier",
            "Wood"
          ],
