        {
          "id": "scopus_781",
          "title": "Effect of ceiling and dry-type double floor on heavy-weight floor impact sound in concrete building and CLT building",
          "authors": "Yamauchi, Takashi and Hiramitsu, Atsuo and Hirakawa, Susumu",
          "affiliations": "Toda Corporation, Research and Development Center, Tokyo, Japan; University of Tsukuba, Doctoral Program in Engineering, Tsukuba, Ibaraki, Japan; Japan National Institute for Land and Infrastructure Management, Building Dept., Tsukuba, Ibaraki, Japan; University of Tsukuba, Faculty of Art and Design, Tsukuba, Ibaraki, Japan; Building Research Institute, Department of Environmental Engineering, Tsukuba, Ibaraki, Japan",
          "countries": [
            "Japan"
          ],
          "journal": "Proceedings of INTER-NOISE 2021 - 2021 International Congress and Exposition of Noise Control Engineering",
          "year": 2021,
          "score": 37,
          "abstract": "The air layer between the interior finishes and the structure is used as piping and wiring space. In many cases, ceilings and dry-type double floors are commonly constructed in Japan. However, the effect of ceilings and dry-type double floors on the heavy-weight floor impact sound insulation performance has not yet quantitatively studied. Therefore, in this paper, the same ceiling and floor structures were constructed for concrete and CLT (Cross Laminated Timber) buildings, and the heavyweight floor impact sound was investigated. As results, it was confirmed that the reduction amount of the heavy-weight floor impact sound by the ceiling tended to be smaller in CLT building than in concrete building. However, the trends were similar. Due to the dry-type double floor structure, the heavy-weight floor impact sound level was increased in concrete building and decreased in CLT building at 63 Hz in the octave band center frequency band. Therefore, it can be said that the dry-type double floor structure can be used to improve the heavy-weight floor impact sound insulation performance in the CLT building. © INTER-NOISE 2021 .All right reserved.",
          "keywords": [
            "Concretes",
            "Floors",
            "Sound insulation",
            "% reductions",
            "Air layers",
            "Cross laminated",
            "Dry types",
            "Floor structures",
            "Heavy-weight floor impact sounds",
