        {
          "id": "scopus_794",
          "title": "MULTI-DIRECTIONAL CYCLIC TESTING OF CROSS-LAMINATED TIMBER ROCKING WALL-FLOOR DIAPHRAGM SUB-ASSEMBLIES",
          "authors": "Sause, Richard S. and Amer, Alia and Ricles, James M. and Marullo, Thomas M.",
          "affiliations": "Lehigh University, ATLSS Engineering Research Center, Bethlehem, PA, United States; Lehigh University, Lehigh Experimental Facility, Bethlehem, PA, United States",
          "countries": [
            "United States"
          ],
          "journal": "World Conference on Earthquake Engineering proceedings",
          "year": 2021,
          "score": 32,
          "abstract": "Conventional cross-laminated timber (CLT) construction can deliver collapse prevention performance but has the potential for significant damage from large earthquake ground motions. A seismically resilient structural system for tall wood buildings can be achieved using post-tensioned self-centering (SC) rocking CLT structural walls (SC-CLT walls). Previous experimental research focused on unidirectional testing of SC-CLT walls in isolation without including other structural components, such as the floor diaphragm and gravity load system. These other structural components need to be assessed for capability to accommodate the controlled rocking motion of the SC-CLT walls. A series of 0.625-scale tests was performed at the NHERI Lehigh Large-Scale Multi-directional Hybrid Simulation Experimental Facility to investigate the behavior of SC-CLT walls and their interaction with the CLT floor diaphragm, collector beams, and gravity load system under bidirectional loading. The test matrix, setup, and multi-directional displacement test control implementation are presented. Preliminary test results for the SC-CLT wall damage states at given story-drift levels and the lateral-load drift hysteretic response are then presented. Finally, these results are compared to results from unidirectional loading tests to assess the effects of bidirectional loading on the hysteretic response and damage states of the SC-CLT walls. © The 17th World Conference on Earthquake Engineering",
          "keywords": [
            "Cross-laminated timber",
            "Floor diaphragm",
            "Gravity load system",
            "Resilience",
            "Self-centering"
          ],
