        {
          "id": "scopus_795",
          "title": "Cyclic loading tests of 3-story CLT structures",
          "authors": "Zhang, Xiaolan and Isoda, Hiroshi and Sumida, Kotaro and Araki, Yasuhiro and Nakashima, Shouichi and Nakagawa, Takafumi and Akiyama, Nobuhiko",
          "affiliations": "Kyoto University, Kyoto, Kyoto, Japan; Japan National Institute for Land and Infrastructure Management, Tsukuba, Ibaraki, Japan; Building Research Institute, Tokyo, Japan",
          "countries": [
            "Japan"
          ],
          "journal": "World Conference on Earthquake Engineering proceedings",
          "year": 2021,
          "score": 32,
          "abstract": "For the sake of promoting cross laminated timber (CLT) structure, Japanese government notifications (GN) on the structural design of CLT panel buildings and definition of standard strength of CLT were issued on 2016. Following the issue of the GN, the guidebook on the regulations of the GN and the manual on design and construction of CLT panel buildings were published on Jun. and Oct. 2016 respectively. In the GN and manual, three kinds of platform framing CLT structures were classified. The seismic behavior of these structures was evaluated based on numerical study and full-scaled shaking table test. This paper introduced the research on balloon framing CLT structure, which is not covered by GN. Comparing with platform framing structure balloon framing structure has superiority in shortening the construction period and reducing the amount of CLT panels and metal connectors. Aiming to provide more options for GN, the seismic property of balloon framing CLT structure was studied. In this research, static cyclic loading tests were carried on four kinds of 3-storey CLT structures: ① platform framing with small size shear walls; ② balloon framing with continuous shear walls; ③ platform framing with broad panels; ④ balloon framing with continuous shear walls coupled with glulam beams. The photos of 4 specimens are shown in Fig 1. CLT structures were fixed to basement (steel I-beam) through steel anchor bolts (Φ14). All specimens had the same global dimension (height × length × depth = 8.7×6.0×2.2 m) and same story height (2.9 m). CLT panels used in this research were made by Japanese cedar. 3-ply CLT panels (t=90 mm) were used as shear walls and hang walls, 7-ply CLT panels (t=210 mm) were used as floors. Steel braces were used in crosswise. Fig. 2 shows the hysteretic curves of 4 specimens. According to the results, No. ① ② ③ showed similar failure mode and seismic performance. Lateral force resistance of No.4 was better than other 3 specimens. © 2024, International Association for Earthquake Engineering. All rights reserved.",
          "keywords": [
            "balloon framing",
            "cross laminated timber",
            "cyclic loading test",
            "platform framing",
            "seismic property"
          ],
