        {
          "id": "scopus_797",
          "title": "FRAGILITY ASSESSMENT OF MID-RISE CLT PLATFORM BUILDING",
          "authors": "Pan, Yuxin and Shahnewaz, Md and Alam, Morshed Morshed and Tannert, Thomas",
          "affiliations": "University of Northern British Columbia, Prince George, BC, Canada; University of Northern British Columbia, Prince George, BC, Canada; University of British Columbia Okanagan, Kelowna, BC, Canada; University of Northern British Columbia, Wood Engineering, Prince George, BC, Canada",
          "countries": [
            "Canada"
          ],
          "journal": "World Conference on Earthquake Engineering proceedings",
          "year": 2021,
          "score": 18,
          "abstract": "Cross-laminated timber (CLT) has been gaining popularity as a structural material, also in seismic regions, because of its low carbon foot-print and potential cost-competitiveness when compared to concrete and steel. Recent effort has focused on developing design provisions for CLT platform-type buildings for inclusion into engineering design standards. In the research presented in this contribution, a numerical model of a 6-storey CLT platform-type building located in Vancouver, BC, was developed in the platform OpenSees. The CLT shear walls were considered as elastic shell elements and the structures’ main connections (wall to foundation, wall to floor and wall to wall) as non-linear spring elements. The hysteresis behaviour of these connections was modelled using the ‘pinching4’ element after calibrating its parameters against experimental results. The resulting load-deformation responses of the shear walls were successfully validated against full-scale test results. Incremental Dynamic Analyses (IDA) were performed and the case-study building’s seismic performance was evaluated in terms of inter-storey drift until collapse using fragility curves constructed from the IDA. The probability of collapse was less than 0.1% at the maximum considered earthquake. The resulting collapse margin ratio of 3.1 demonstrated that the case-study 6-storey CLT platform-type building could meet the seismic demands in a high seismic zone if appropriately designed. © The 17th World Conference on Earthquake Engineering.",
          "keywords": [
            "Cross-laminated Timber",
            "Finite Element Analyses",
            "Incremental Dynamic Analysis",
            "Mass-timber construction",
            "Seismic Design"
          ],
