        {
          "id": "scopus_807",
          "title": "Whistler Gateway Loop - Wood-steel composite adhesive system in aesthetic roof structure; Whistler Gateway Loop: Holz-Stahl-Klebeverbindungssystem in ästhetischem Dachtragwerk",
          "authors": "Diehl, Friedemann and Bletz-Mühldorfer, Oliver and Bathon, Leander Anton",
          "affiliations": "Hochschule RheinMain, Labor für Holzbau, Wiesbaden, Germany",
          "countries": [
            "Germany"
          ],
          "journal": "Bautechnik",
          "year": 2020,
          "score": 11,
          "abstract": "Whistler Gateway Loop – wood-steel composite adhesive system in aesthetic roof structure. In the Coast Mountains of British Columbia, which are worldwide known among skiers, mountain bikers and hikers, the Resort Municipality of Whistler had a striking canopy built for a bus station. It acts as main transport hub in Whistler Village. For the roof structure, the architects of Public Architecture and Communication chose cross-laminated timber, which is supported by glulam beams. A wood-steel composite adhesive system (HSK system) was used to aesthetically connect the roof structure, which is stressed by high snow loads, with the supporting pillars made of steel. With this system, which was developed in Germany, the project was realized without braces as well as visible lateral supports. The connections are covered by the glulam and the beauty of the wooden supporting structure is fully preserved. In addition to the described construction project, the article focuses on the wood-steel composite adhesive system. © 2020, Ernst und Sohn. All rights reserved.",
          "keywords": [
            "adhesive",
            "Building materials",
            "Buildings",
            "glued in perforated steel plates",
            "HSK-System",
