        {
          "id": "scopus_878",
          "title": "Time-dependent deflection measurement for steel-timber composite (STC) flooring system",
          "authors": "Chiniforush, Alireza A. and Akbarnezhad, Ali and Malek, Sardar",
          "affiliations": "The University of Sydney, Sydney, NSW, Australia; University of Technology Sydney, Sydney, NSW, Australia",
          "countries": [
            "Australia"
          ],
          "journal": "International Conference on Advances in Experimental Structural Engineering",
          "year": 2020,
          "score": 2,
          "abstract": "Estimating the amount of creep is an important aspect in designing multi-storey timber structures. In most cases, long-term deflections govern the design of members and the cross-section of flooring systems. Therefore, the deflection values should be considered carefully in the design process of such systems. This article provides instructions on designing large-scale creep experimental setups for measuring the time-dependent deflections in a composite system with particular focus on steel-timber composite (STC) flooring. Three experimental setups are designed and described for measuring the time-dependent slips and deflections of shear connectors, beams, and beam-to-column joints. Measurements are made over the course of 600 days. The challenges involved in designing, instrumentation, conducting the tests, and extracting results are discussed in details. Recommendations for future improvements are also provided. © 2020 EUCENTRE. All rights reserved.",
          "keywords": [
            "Composite floors",
            "Creep",
            "Cross-laminated timber (CLT)",
            "Large-scale testing",
            "Long-term behaviour"
          ],
