        {
          "id": "scopus_903",
          "title": "An assessment of greenhouse gas emissions from CLT and glulam in two residential nearly zero energy buildings",
          "authors": "Lolli, Nicola and Fufa, Selamawit Mamo and Wiik, Marianne Kjendseth",
          "affiliations": "SINTEF Foundation for Scientific and Industrial Research, Trondheim, Sor-Trondelag, Norway; SINTEF Foundation for Scientific and Industrial Research, Trondheim, Sor-Trondelag, Norway",
          "countries": [
            "Norway"
          ],
          "journal": "Wood Material Science and Engineering",
          "year": 2019,
          "score": 22,
          "abstract": "This paper analyses and compares the embodied and operational greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions of two nearly Zero Energy Buildings, a student housing building in Trondheim, Norway, and a residential building in Växjö, Sweden. The student housing in Trondheim, Moholt Allmenning Tower B, is a 9-storey tower composed of cross laminated timber (CLT) panels and concrete floor slabs with a total heated floor area of 3801 m<sup>2</sup>. The apartment building in Växjö, House A, consists of 8 floors composed of glued laminated timber (glulam) beams with a wood-cassette floor, and concrete slabs with a total heated floor area of 3483 m<sup>2</sup>. The results show that the use of CLT has the largest reduction of total GHG emissions due to substituting concrete elements in the structural frame. The comparative assessment of the external wall and the internal floor constructions shows that the use of CLT adds marginal GHG emissions per unit of area of component. The calculation of operational GHG emissions shows that the use of the Nordic electricity mix disfavours the full-electric energy system of Moholt Allmenning Tower B, in contrast to the district heating used in Norra Vallen Building A. © 2019, © 2019 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.",
          "keywords": [
            "Cross laminated timber",
            "environmental product declaration",
            "glue laminated timber",
            "greenhouse gas emission",
            "life cycle assessment",
            "nearly zero energy building"
          ],
