        },
        {
          "id": "scopus_910",
          "title": "Experimental study on charring rate of cross-laminated timber floor considering the effect of delamination; 考虑层板脱落影响的正交胶合木楼板炭化速度试验研究",
          "authors": "Zhang, Jin and Wu, Lei and Liao, Jianan and Wang, Weichang and Li, Weibin",
          "affiliations": "Key Laboratory of Concrete and Prestressed Concrete Structures, Ministry of Education, Nanjing, Jiangsu, China; Organizing Committee of Nanning New & Hi-tech Industry Development Zone, Nanning, Guangxi, China",
          "countries": [
            "China"
          ],
          "journal": "Dongnan Daxue Xuebao (Ziran Kexue Ban)/Journal of Southeast University (Natural Science Edition)",
          "year": 2019,
          "score": 30,
          "abstract": "To study the delamination of cross-laminated timber (CLT) floor under fire and its influence on the charring rate, fire tests were carried out on six specimens at different given times. The effects of fire duration, the thickness of CLT floor, and falling off of charred layers on the charring rate of CLT floor were studied. The charring depth calculation method considering the influence of falling off of charred layers was established. The results show that when the CLT floor is exposed to fire within a short time and the char line does not develop to the glue line, the charred layer will not fall off. The charring rate will remain basically the same as that of the solid wood, glue-laminated timber and other engineered timber products. CLT bonded with one-component polyurethane (PUR) adhesive will fall off when the char line develops to the glue line after longer fire exposure. In addition, the longer the fire exposure time, the more serious the falling off and the faster the average charring rate. Compared the results of CLT with different layers, the falling off of the charred layers is found to be more serious and the average charring rate is higher when the number of layers is larger and the lamella is thinner. The proposed charring depth calculation model effectively takes into account the influence of accelerated charring rate caused by falling off of the charred layers under fire. The calculational results are in good agreement with the test ones and can provide a reference for engineers. © 2019, Editorial Department of Journal of Southeast University. All right reserved.",
          "keywords": [
            "Charring depth model",
            "Charring rate",
            "Cross-laminated timber (CLT)",
            "Delamination",
            "Fire design"
          ],
          "studyType": "Article",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.3969/j.issn.1001-0505.2019.04.013",
          "language": "Chinese",
          "source": "Scopus",
          "numString": 2,
          "isDuplicate": false,
          "duplicateOf": null,
