        },
        {
          "id": "scopus_1006",
          "title": "Ring-core method in determining the amount of non-uniform residual stress in laminated composites: experimental, finite element and theoretical evaluation",
          "authors": "Ghaedamini, Roohollah and Ghassemi, Aazam and Atrian, Amir",
          "affiliations": "Islamic Azad University, Najafabad Branch, Department of Mechanical Engineering, Najafabad, Isfahan, Iran; Islamic Azad University, Najafabad Branch, Najafabad Branch, Najafabad, Isfahan, Iran",
          "countries": [
            "Iran"
          ],
          "journal": "Archive of Applied Mechanics",
          "year": 2018,
          "score": 1,
          "abstract": "Residual stresses in composite constructions are created because of non-uniform contraction of layers during cooling from curing to ambient temperature. The aim of this study is to investigate the feasibility of incremental ring-core method to estimate non-uniform residual stresses in various laminates of polymer matrix composites. To carry out this test, glass/epoxy specimens were selected in two different types of symmetric cross-ply laminate ([04∘/904∘]S) and symmetric quasi-isotropic laminate ([02∘/±45∘/902∘]S). The samples were created using hand layup and vacuum bagging. By experimental tests, released strains measured during milling process and coefficient factors for every annular groove machining operations were obtained by finite element modeling. As well, residual stresses were calculated by classical lamination plate theory. For symmetric cross-ply, the maximum difference between experimental and theoretical results was about 16%, which was observed in the first and eighth layers. Symmetric quasi-isotropic specimen showed also good agreement between the results and the maximum measurement error of 8% was observed. For the mentioned two cases, summation of components of the residual stress in all directions is near to zero. The results of this study show that in the ring-core method, because of the nature and higher volume of material removal than other methods, values of residual stress released are higher and the results have a good consistency compared with the theoretical values. © 2017, Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature.",
          "keywords": [
            "Classical laminate theory (CLT)",
            "Composite",
            "Finite element modeling (FEM)",
            "Residual stresses",
            "Ring core"
          ],
          "studyType": "Article",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.1007/s00419-017-1340-z",
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Scopus",
          "numString": 2,
          "isDuplicate": false,
          "duplicateOf": null,
          "dataProcessingStatus": "pending",
          "filter1Status": "pending",
          "filter2Status": "pending",
          "filter3Status": "pending",
          "exclusionReason": null,
          "inclusionCriterion": null,
          "exclusionCriterion": null,
          "extractionCriteria": null,
          "searchString": "(( \"cross laminated timber\" OR \"cross-laminated timber\" OR “x-lam” OR CLT) AND ( slab OR floor OR roof ))",
          "importDate": "2026-02-23T22:36:58.138Z",
          "lastModified": "2026-02-23T22:36:58.138Z"
        },
        {
          "id": "scopus_1007",
          "title": "In-plain shear test of joint of CLT floor adapting CSTS system",
          "authors": "Fujita, Masanori and Otaki, Mayo and Okoshi, Yuki and Kikuchi, Takamasa and Koyano, Kazuhisa and Iwata, Mamoru",
          "affiliations": "Yamaguchi University, Graduate School of Sciences and Technology for Innovation, Yamaguchi, Yamaguchi, Japan; Nice Corporation, Japan; Kanagawa University, Faculty of Architecture and Building Engineering, Yokohama, Kanagawa, Japan",
          "countries": [
            "Japan"
          ],
          "journal": "AIJ Journal of Technology and Design",
          "year": 2018,
          "score": 18,
          "abstract": "In light of the global environment, the authors proposed a building system comprising steel and timber (Hereafter referred to as CSTS), which consist of rolled section steels and timbers. A design method of the CSTS uses the concept of a damage controlled structure and its mechanical model is established based on the previous tests. In this study, we propose floor structure adapting for the CSTS using cross laminated timber. In-plane shear test of the joint which are consisted of unheaded studs or bolts for joint of the CSTS beam and floor structure is conducted. Structural performances of joint of the CSTS beam and floor structure are evaluated. The findings obtained from tests indicate that the joint of the CSTS beam and the floor structure has a high stiffness and strength. © 2018 Architectural Institute of Japan. All rights reserved.",
          "keywords": [
            "Bolt",
            "Composite steel-timber structure",
            "Cross laminated timber floor",
            "In-plane shear test",
            "Stud",
            "Yield shear strength"
          ],
          "studyType": "Article",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.3130/aijt.24.189",
          "language": "Japanese",
          "source": "Scopus",
          "numString": 2,
          "isDuplicate": false,
          "duplicateOf": null,
