        {
          "id": "scopus_1017",
          "title": "The transmission loss of nail-laminated timber elements",
          "authors": "Mahn, Jeffrey P. and Müller-Trapet, Markus",
          "affiliations": "National Research Council Canada, Ottawa, ON, Canada",
          "countries": [
            "Canada"
          ],
          "journal": "25th International Congress on Sound and Vibration 2018, ICSV 2018: Hiroshima Calling",
          "year": 2018,
          "score": 4,
          "abstract": "Nail-laminated timber (NLT or Nail-lam) elements are often used as the floors and walls of mass timber building constructions in Canada. While NLT elements have been used in buildings for over a century, little is known about the acoustic properties of the elements. The National Research Council of Canada undertook a study of the acoustic properties of the NLT elements including the transmission loss and the radiation efficiency of the elements with and without linings. The results are presented and compared versus the acoustic properties of cross-laminated timber (CLT) and doweled-laminated timber (DLT) elements which were also measured as part of the project. © 25th International Congress on Sound and Vibration 2018, ICSV 2018: Hiroshima Calling. All rights reserved.",
