        {
          "id": "scopus_1018",
          "title": "Mass timber system in Japan: Environmental and economic impact of a mid-storey residential building",
          "authors": "Passarelli, R. N. and Koshihara, Mikio",
          "affiliations": "The University of Tokyo, Tokyo, Japan",
          "countries": [
            "Japan"
          ],
          "journal": "WCTE 2018 - World Conference on Timber Engineering",
          "year": 2018,
          "score": 13,
          "abstract": "The study investigates the environmental and economic impacts of CLT load-bearing systems, for mid-storey residential construction in Japan. The work aims to indicate how different solutions correlate with different impacts, and to what extent a local approach can be beneficial, thus supporting the further development of the domestic industry. Using the LCA method, we calculated the Global Warming Potential (GWP) and material cost for three types of wall and slab elements in a hypothetical 3-storeys building, located in Tokyo. We considered both domestic and imported supply options for timber products. After, we assessed each building element individually and the possible combinations between them. The results showed that mass timber systems employing domestic CLT present higher material cost than other systems, due to their greater use of timber per unit area of construction, in addition to the high price of Japanese CLT. On the other hand, domestic CLT mass timber system shows smaller GWP than other assessed options. The findings suggest a local approach is beneficial from the environmental point of view. Hence, the use of evaluation systems based on net carbon emissions could favour the use of domestic CLT and increase its market competitiveness against imported options. © WCTE 2018 Committee.",
          "keywords": [
            "CLT",
            "Life cycle assessment",
            "Multistorey timber construction",
            "Tokyo",
            "Wooden construction"
          ],
          "studyType": "Conference paper",
          "quality": null,
