        {
          "id": "scopus_1020",
          "title": "Punching behaviour of continous two-way CLT flat slabs at interior connections to columns",
          "authors": "Muster, Marcel and Frangi, Andrea",
          "affiliations": "ETH Zürich, Institute of Structural Engineering, Zurich, ZH, Switzerland",
          "countries": [
            "Switzerland"
          ],
          "journal": "WCTE 2018 - World Conference on Timber Engineering",
          "year": 2018,
          "score": 33,
          "abstract": "A newly developed bonding technology offers the possibility to connect multiple cross laminated timber (CLT) slabs rigidly together. Therefore, it is now possible to build biaxial CLT slabs which can carry loads over more than one span and which are only point supported. In such a structure high loads have to be transferred from the slab into the column. In this paper punching tests on different CLT plates are presented. The results of these tests allow a better understanding of the important parameters of a point supported CLT slab and should help creating the basic design criteria. The tested plates feature a hole above the column. Vertical forces from higher storeys are transferred through this opening without subjecting the CLT slab to stresses perpendicular to the grain. Even though the opening leads to a stress concentration in the CLT plate, the rolling shear failure was the governing failure mode during the tests. After the punching tests every plate was cut open and the visible failures were recorded. In addition, some smaller specimens were taken from the plates and tested to determine the rolling shear strength of single boards. It was observed that the achieved rolling shear strength in the punching tests was about 1.4 times higher than the rolling shear strength determined in shear tests on single boards. This finding is in line with previous independent experiments. The results of the punching tests showed the potential of CLT slabs to be used as biaxial, continuous, point supported floor slabs. Nevertheless, further investigations on the rolling shear strength and on the stress concentration are necessary to describe the punching behaviour of a CLT slab. © WCTE 2018 Committee.",
          "keywords": [
            "Cross-laminated timber (CLT)",
            "Orthotropic plates",
            "Punching",
            "Rolling shear",
            "Stress concentration"
          ],
          "studyType": "Conference paper",
