        {
          "id": "scopus_1045",
          "title": "Moisture intrusion in cross laminated timber and the potential for fungal attack",
          "authors": "Morrell, Jeffrey J. and Morrell, Ian N. and Trebelhorn, Daniel B. and Sinha, Arijit",
          "affiliations": "Oregon State University, Corvallis, OR, United States",
          "countries": [
            "United States"
          ],
          "journal": "WCTE 2018 - World Conference on Timber Engineering",
          "year": 2018,
          "score": 15,
          "abstract": "Cross laminated timber (CLT) has tremendous potential as a building material in tall timber structures. However, one potential weakness of the material is the risk of wetting during construction and in service. Moisture intrusion can have important effects on composite panel properties and, if left wet long enough, can foster fungal attack. Measurable precipitation occurred in 35 of the 71 days it took to complete the recent construction of the Brock Commons Dormitory at the University of British Columbia. Water is subject to gravity and tends to find pathways downward through building materials. Water was observed moving through multiple floors in this building. The potential effects of this moisture intrusion on panel properties and fungal attack have received little attention but could have tremendous impacts on the potential for these materials in North America. The rates of moisture intrusion into three-ply Douglas-fir cross laminated timber was studied by exposing panel sections to rainfall for one month to simulate a CLT floor. Moisture uptake was assessed by mass changes. In addition, the panels were examined using CAT-Scans at the start of the test, after the one month exposure, and approximately one year after the end of exposure. Panels were exposed to over 144 mm of rainfall over 29 days and moisture content increased from 10 to 25 % moisture content. CAT-Scans showed that moisture distribution was uneven, with large increases at the non-edge glued joints. The Scans also showed substantial increases in volume that were concentrated at these same joints. Examination of samples 3 months after the wetting period revealed the presence of multiple internal cracks. The effects of these cracks on panel integrity merit further attention. The results will be discussed in terms of the possible effects on CLT properties as well as the potential for fungal attack. © WCTE 2018 Committee.",
          "keywords": [
            "CT-Scans",
            "Dimensional changes",
            "Durability",
            "Moisture content"
          ],
          "studyType": "Conference paper",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": null,
          "language": "English",
