        {
          "id": "scopus_1077",
          "title": "Airborne sound transmission of cross laminated timber panel walls",
          "authors": "Tanaka, Manabu and Kasai, Yusuke and Murakami, Tsuyoshi and Kawatani, Shoji and Kawai, Makoto",
          "affiliations": "General Building Research Corporation of Japan, Suita, Osaka, Japan; Japan Cross Laminated Timber Association, Japan",
          "countries": [
            "Japan"
          ],
          "journal": "INTER-NOISE 2017 - 46th International Congress and Exposition on Noise Control Engineering: Taming Noise and Moving Quiet",
          "year": 2017,
          "score": 22,
          "abstract": "Recently, Cross Laminated Timber (=CLT) panels have come to be used as building elements, such as partition walls between rooms, or party walls and floor slab structures between dwelling houses in apartments. In addition, sometimes CLT panel partition walls have narrow and through slits between panels. In such a case, effect of sound transmission through the slits might be negligible. However, their sound insulation performance has not been researched enough. The purpose of this study is to estimate the airborne sound insulation performance of partition walls made with CLT panels. To grasp their sound insulation performance, laboratory measurements were conducted for fourteen wall specimens which were with and without slits. From the results of sound insulation experiments in the laboratory, following facts were revealed. In case of a partition wall without slit between CLT panels, its sound transmission loss increases monotonically to frequency from 100 Hz up to 3150 Hz band. In this frequency range, measured sound transmission loss of the CLT panel wall is lower than prediction using the mass law equation, but regression line formula is applicable with a high correlation coefficient. In case of partition walls with narrow slits between CLT panels, effect of sound transmission through the slits is not negligible. Especially in frequency range over 500Hz band, decrease of sound transmission loss appears remarkably compared to the wall specimen of no slit condition. However, this decrease is predictable by regression line equations with the total length of the slits. © 2017 Institute of Noise Control Engineering. All rights reserved.",
          "keywords": [
            "Airborne sound insulation",
            "Cross laminated timber",
            "Slit"
          ],
          "studyType": "Conference paper",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": null,
          "language": "English",
