        {
          "id": "scopus_1087",
          "title": "Lateral-Load Resistance of Cross-Laminated Timber Shear Walls",
          "authors": "Reynolds, Thomas P.S. and Foster, Robert M. and Bregulla, Julie and Chang, Wen-Shao and Harris, Richard and Ramage, Michael H.",
          "affiliations": "The University of Edinburgh, School of Engineering, Edinburgh, Scotland, United Kingdom; The University of Queensland, School of Civil Engineering, Brisbane, QLD, Australia; Building Research Establishment, Building Technology Group, Watford, Hertfordshire, United Kingdom; University of Bath, Faculty of Engineering & Design, Bath, Somerset, United Kingdom; University of Cambridge, Department of Architecture, Cambridge, Cambridgeshire, United Kingdom",
          "countries": [
            "United Kingdom",
            "Australia"
          ],
          "journal": "Journal of Structural Engineering (United States)",
          "year": 2017,
          "score": 13,
          "abstract": "Cross-laminated timber shear wall systems are used as a lateral load resisting system in multistory timber buildings. Walls at each level typically bear directly on the floor panels below and are connected by nailed steel brackets. Design guidance for the lateral-load resistance of such systems is not well established and design approaches vary among practitioners. Two cross-laminated two-story timber shear wall systems are tested under vertical and lateral load, along with pullout tests on individual steel connectors. Comprehensive kinematic behavior is obtained from a combination of discrete transducers and continuous field displacements along the base of the walls, obtained by digital image correlation, giving a measure of the length of wall in contact with the floor below. Existing design approaches are evaluated. A new offset-yield criterion based on acceptable permanent deformations is proposed. A lower bound plastic distribution of stresses, reflecting yielding of all connectors in tension and cross-grain crushing of the floor panel, is found to most accurately reflect the observed behavior. © 2017 American Society of Civil Engineers.",
          "keywords": [
            "Cross-laminated timber (CLT)",
            "Digital image correlation",
            "Engineered wood",
            "Kinematics",
            "Wood structures"
          ],
          "studyType": "Article",
          "quality": null,
