        {
          "id": "scopus_1240",
          "title": "Dynamic characteristics of shallow floors with cross-laminated-timber spines",
          "authors": "Weckendorf, Jan and Smith, Ian",
          "affiliations": "University of New Brunswick, Hybrid Construction Research Group, Fredericton, NB, Canada",
          "countries": [
            "Canada"
          ],
          "journal": "World Conference on Timber Engineering 2012, WCTE 2012",
          "year": 2012,
          "score": 12,
          "abstract": "Ensuring vibration serviceability is a key aspect of design and construction of shallow floors of buildings. This paper presents an experimental investigation of low amplitude dynamic responses of slabs that employ cross-laminated-timber (CLT) as their structural spines. Such slabs are intended to be used in demanding applications like floor substructures of mid- and high-rise buildings with various categories of occupancy. Information on vibration properties of CLT plate systems is very limited, and almost non-existent for a new generation of CLT products entering the North American marketplace. Experiments were performed on rectangular plan slab systems having different areas, aspect ratios and support conditions. Advanced modal analysis tools were used to determine the dynamic characteristics of each system. Data extraction focussed on determination of fundamental natural frequencies, mode shapes, separations between modal frequencies, and modal damping ratios; which are parameters used to assess the vibration serviceability of floor systems in buildings.",
          "keywords": [
            "Cross-laminated-timber",
            "Damping",
            "Floors",
            "Hybrid structures",
            "Modal analysis",
            "Serviceability"
          ],
