          "title": "Vibrational performance of cross laminated timber floors",
          "authors": "Maldonado, Saúl A.Hernández and Chui, Yinghei",
          "affiliations": "University of New Brunswick, Faculty of Forestry and Environmental Management, Fredericton, NB, Canada",
          "countries": [
            "Canada"
          ],
          "journal": "World Conference on Timber Engineering 2012, WCTE 2012",
          "year": 2012,
          "score": 29,
          "abstract": "This study investigated the vibration behaviour of a cross-laminated timber (CLT) floor plate under various support conditions. CLT floors consisting of up to a total of four CLT panels were tested with two and four sides simply supported. The floor specimens tested included a single CLT panel simply supported at both ends and then with a second, third, and fourth panel progressively added. The same was done with the floor supported on all four sides. Natural frequencies of each floor configuration are presented. The study also included investigating the influence of fastening the CLT panels at the perimeters of the floor with screws when supported on all four sides. The natural frequencies are predicted using a model proposed by Leissa [4]. The predicted frequencies are somewhat different from the measured values. The modulus of elasticity, E<inf>x</inf> and E<inf>y</inf>, and the modulus of rigidity, G<inf>xy</inf> of an individual panel were measured using a vibration method proposed by Sobue and Katoh [5]. This study also presents the results of vibration tests and predicted natural frequencies of selected floors in a three-storey CLT building.",
          "keywords": [
            "Cross laminated timber",
            "Floor vibrations",
            "Natural frequencies",
            "Support condition"
          ],
