          "journal": "Structures and Architecture - Proceedings of the 1st International Conference on Structures and Architecture, ICSA 2010",
          "year": 2010,
          "score": 6,
          "abstract": "Until recently, modern medium- and high-rise construction has been limited to steel and concrete. However, the introduction of new performance and objective-based building codes and the development of innovative structural wood products and systems around the world have contributed to the construction of several tall timber-based buildings. The series of five 8-storey residential timber-based buildings in the city of Växjö, Sweden, and the most recent 9-storey residential building made of cross-laminated timber (CLT) panels in London, UK, are good examples of constructions that have pushed the envelope and reached new heights. Such projects were seen as not possible using wood products, especially under previous building codes. In Canada, the new 2005 edition of the National Building Code (NBCC) of construction recognizes both",
