        {
          "id": "scopus_1283",
          "title": "Designed for Nietzsche - A Roof- and Facade-Structure Cut Out from Cross-Laminated Timber-Plates; Für Nietzsche konstruiert - ein Dach- und Fassadentragwerk ausgeschnitten aus Brettsperrholz",
          "authors": "Trautz, Martin",
          "affiliations": "Rheinisch-Westfälische Technische Hochschule Aachen, Aachen, Nordrhein-Westfalen, Germany",
          "countries": [
            "Germany"
          ],
          "journal": "Bautechnik",
          "year": 2010,
          "score": 8,
          "abstract": "The structure of the roof and the facade of the Nietzsche-Documentation- Centre in Naumburg/Saale, a small town in the heart of Germany where the philosopher Friedrich Nietzsche spent a major part of his life, is an unconventional application of cross-laminated-timber-plates. It consists of frame elements cut out from the wooden plate-material similar to puzzle-parts sawed out from plywood with a jigsaw. All parts were produced that way and put together with a roofing plate also made from cross-laminated-timber, which works as an elastic longitudinal hinge-element. The structure is a rather unconventional one and refers to the critical and non conformal ideas of the well-known philosopher Friedrich Nietzsche. Copyright © 2010 Ernst & Sohn Verlag für Architektur und technische Wissenschaften GmbH & Co. KG, Berlin.",
          "keywords": [],
