        {
          "id": "scopus_1285",
          "title": "Sound transmission loss of Cross Laminated Timber 'CLT'floors, measurements and modelling using SEA",
          "authors": "Kouyoumji, Jean Luc and Boulet, Sylvain and Gagnon, Sylvain",
          "affiliations": "FCBA, Timber Construction Group, Bordeaux, France; FPInnovations, Canada, Pointe-Claire, QC, Canada",
          "countries": [
            "France",
            "Canada"
          ],
          "journal": "38th International Congress and Exposition on Noise Control Engineering 2009, INTER-NOISE 2009",
          "year": 2009,
          "score": 10,
          "abstract": "Timber framed construction can achieve high acoustic and thermal insulation levels. Wood is a sustainable material and thus a smart material for the 'green building' applications. This article presents acoustic characterization of lightweight floors, as well as acoustic modeling using Statistical Energy Analysis (SEA). We also present a methodology to extend measurements towards non tested structures. First we predict sound transmission loss, second we fit the calculation close to the measured one, third we calculate the sound transmission of a different structure, and finally we go back to the measured shape. The framework of the methodology is SEA. The theory involves cutting the structure into subsystems and decomposing the spectrum into third-octaves or octaves. In this way, the exchange of energy flow in the substructures can be analyzed. The parameters that govern vibrational transmission between subsystems are thus damping and coupling loss factors and can be identified experimentally by reversing the problem (Reverse SEA). This particularity makes SEA a most effective design tool for flanking transmission calculation. Insulation results are shown for medium-rise and office buildings using CLT for floor configuration. Part of the results is measured and the other part is calculated with a software dedicated to timber lightweight constructions: SEA-Wood.",
          "keywords": [
            "Acoustic characterization",
            "Acoustic modeling",
            "Coupling loss factors",
            "Design tool",
