        },
        {
          "id": "web_of_science_1368",
          "title": "Experimental and Analytical Lateral Performance of Posttensioned CLT Shear Walls and Conventional CLT Shear Walls",
          "authors": "Sun, Xiaofeng and He, Minjuan and Li, Zheng",
          "affiliations": "Tongji University",
          "countries": [],
          "journal": "JOURNAL OF STRUCTURAL ENGINEERING",
          "year": 2020,
          "score": 56,
          "abstract": "Cross-laminated timber (CLT) is suitable for shear walls owing to its high in-plane stiffness. However, according to a series of shaking table tests on multistory CLT structures, premature connection damage always occurs in CLT shear walls under the applied loading scenarios representing moderate and major earthquakes. To mitigate the premature connection damage and improve the seismic resilience of CLT shear walls, a specific kind of posttensioned (PT) CLT shear wall is proposed herein, which is constructed by posttensioning CLT wall panels to the foundation with PT steel strands or bars. Previous studies on PT CLT shear walls with vertically continuous wall panels already exist. In this study, cyclic loading tests were conducted on three PT CLT shear wall specimens with different initial posttensioning forces. Floor diaphragms were included in these wall specimens and were structurally designed to mitigate the creep deformation of the CLT floor diaphragm subjected to perpendicular-to-grain compression. In addition, two conventional CLT shear walls with wall panels connected to the foundation using metal connections were also tested. The hysteretic behavior of the PT CLT shear walls was analyzed, and their lateral load-resisting performance was compared with that of the conventional CLT shear walls. Finally, fiber-based nonlinear finite-element models were developed within the Open System for Earthquake Engineering Simulation (OpenSees) framework to duplicate the lateral performance of PT CLT shear walls and conventional CLT shear walls. Results indicate that PT CLT shear walls exhibit excellent lateral performance with self-centering capability. After the entire loading process, almost no damage occurred in the PT CLT shear walls compared with the premature damage occurring in the metal connections of the conventional CLT shear walls. A friction coefficient of 0.32 is recommended for the contact interface between the CLT and steel foundation. Furthermore, for the PT CLT shear walls or conventional CLT shear walls, their analytical responses from numerical models agree well with the experimental responses, indicating the potential values of these fiber-based nonlinear models for engineering design. (C) 2020 American Society of Civil Engineers.",
          "keywords": [
            "Cross-laminated timber",
            "Posttensioned cross-laminated timber (CLT) shear wall",
            "Conventional cross-laminated timber shear wall",
            "Lateral performance",
            "Failure mode",
            "Fiber-based nonlinear model"
          ],
          "studyType": "Article",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.1061/(ASCE)ST.1943-541X.0002638",
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Web of Science",
          "numString": 3,
          "isDuplicate": false,
          "duplicateOf": null,
          "dataProcessingStatus": "pending",
          "filter1Status": "pending",
          "filter2Status": "pending",
          "filter3Status": "pending",
          "exclusionReason": null,
          "inclusionCriterion": null,
          "exclusionCriterion": null,
          "extractionCriteria": null,
          "searchString": "(( \"cross laminated timber\" OR \"cross-laminated timber\" OR “x-lam” OR CLT) AND ( slab OR floor OR roof ))",
          "importDate": "2026-02-23T22:37:03.813Z",
          "lastModified": "2026-02-23T22:37:03.813Z"
        },
        {
          "id": "web_of_science_1369",
          "title": "Elevated Temperature Effects on Performance of a Cross-Laminated Timber Floor-to-Wall Bracket Connections",
          "authors": "Mahr, Kolton and Sinha, Arijit and Barbosa, Andre R.",
          "affiliations": "Oregon State University; Oregon State University",
          "countries": [],
          "journal": "JOURNAL OF STRUCTURAL ENGINEERING",
          "year": 2020,
          "score": 26,
          "abstract": "Connections in mass timber structural systems transfer lateral forces from mass timber elements such as shear walls to floor diaphragms. Cross-laminated timber (CLT) is a prominent engineered mass timber material used to manufacture walls and floor assemblies. Fire performance research of CLT walls and floors has been abundant in recent years in an effort to develop an understanding of the performance of structural members under fire. Some fire-protected structural elements, including connections, may not be directly exposed to flames in a fire event but will experience elevated temperatures. There is limited research on elevated temperature performance of CLT connections, and consequently a lack of understanding of their fire performance in CLT structures. To aid in bridging this knowledge gap, in this study a series of cyclic shear tests were conducted on a CLT wall-to-floor bracket connection assembly to characterize thermal degradation according to a matrix of 28 exposure duration-temperature combinations. Two different methods were employed to develop force-displacement backbone models from the experimental hysteresis data. Models from both methods indicate an overall degradation of the connection performance in terms of load carrying capacity, elastic stiffness, ductility, and energy dissipation with increasing exposure to elevated temperatures. (C) 2020 American Society of Civil Engineers.",
          "keywords": [
            "Cross-laminated timber (CLT)",
            "Thermal degradation",
            "Connection",
            "Cyclic",
            "Shear"
          ],
          "studyType": "Article",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.1061/(ASCE)ST.1943-541X.0002737",
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Web of Science",
          "numString": 3,
          "isDuplicate": false,
          "duplicateOf": null,
          "dataProcessingStatus": "pending",
          "filter1Status": "pending",
          "filter2Status": "pending",
          "filter3Status": "pending",
          "exclusionReason": null,
          "inclusionCriterion": null,
          "exclusionCriterion": null,
          "extractionCriteria": null,
          "searchString": "(( \"cross laminated timber\" OR \"cross-laminated timber\" OR “x-lam” OR CLT) AND ( slab OR floor OR roof ))",
          "importDate": "2026-02-23T22:37:03.814Z",
          "lastModified": "2026-02-23T22:37:03.814Z"
        },
        {
          "id": "web_of_science_1370",
          "title": "Scaling study on the fire resistance of cross-laminated timber floors",
          "authors": "Bai, Yu and Zhang, Jin and Zhang, Xilin",
          "affiliations": "Southeast University - China; Southeast University - China",
          "countries": [
            "China"
          ],
          "journal": "JOURNAL OF BUILDING ENGINEERING",
          "year": 2024,
          "score": 27,
          "abstract": "Compared to full-scale prototype experiments in timber structures, the use of reduced scale models in fire tests significantly reduces research costs and operational difficulties, thus enhancing research efficiency. However, there has been limited research on the fire performance similarity of cross-laminated timber (CLT) components. In this study, the scaling relationships of fire resistances of CLT floors, including the similarity of out-of-plane bending performance and the similarity of fire resistance, were investigated. To assess compliance with static similarity criteria, out-of-plane bending tests were conducted on CLT floors with varying scales. Based on the similarity analysis of static loading test results, the influence of size effect on out-of-plane load-carrying capacity was analysed. Afterwards, the basic requirements for the similarity of fire resistance were studied, and the thermomechanical similarity criteria with size effect of CLT floors were derived. Fire tests designed by the proposed thermomechanical similarity criteria were performed on CLT floors with different scales. A numerical approach, including mechanical improved models with size effect and similar thermomechanical models, was developed to determine the fire response of CLT scale components and verify the thermomechanical similarity criteria. The test and numerical results demonstrated that the fire tests of CLT scale components designed using the proposed thermomechanical similarity criteria yielded similar fire resistances. Moreover, the presented results could be used as reference for the scaling fire design of CLT structures.",
          "keywords": [
            "Cross-laminated timber",
            "Fire resistance",
            "Floor",
            "Scale model",
            "Similarity"
          ],
          "studyType": "Article",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.1016/j.jobe.2024.108679",
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Web of Science",
          "numString": 3,
          "isDuplicate": false,
          "duplicateOf": null,
          "dataProcessingStatus": "pending",
          "filter1Status": "pending",
          "filter2Status": "pending",
          "filter3Status": "pending",
          "exclusionReason": null,
          "inclusionCriterion": null,
          "exclusionCriterion": null,
          "extractionCriteria": null,
          "searchString": "(( \"cross laminated timber\" OR \"cross-laminated timber\" OR “x-lam” OR CLT) AND ( slab OR floor OR roof ))",
          "importDate": "2026-02-23T22:37:03.814Z",
          "lastModified": "2026-02-23T22:37:03.814Z"
        },
        {
          "id": "web_of_science_1371",
          "title": "Bending performance of domestic larch cross-laminated timber reinforced with fiber-reinforced polymer",
          "authors": "Baek, Seungyoup and Song, Yojin and Hong, Soonil",
          "affiliations": "Kangwon National University",
          "countries": [],
          "journal": "WOOD MATERIAL SCIENCE \\& ENGINEERING",
          "year": 2025,
          "score": 78,
          "abstract": "The added fiber-reinforced polymer (FRP) to cross-laminated timber (CLT) panels is expected to enable cost down and light-weight of the floor slab and improve bending performance. In this study, FRP was reinforced in Larix kaempferi 5-layer CLT, which was two types FRP and three types of reinforcement locations. The FRPs used Glass Fiber Cloth (GFC) and Carbon Fiber Reinforced Plastic sheet (CFRP). The reinforcement locations were the outermost tensile part, between the outermost tensile layer and the Minor strength direction layer, and between all layers. The bending performance of FRP-reinforced CLTs was evaluated and compared with unreinforced CLTs (control). The specimens reinforced with FRP between the outermost tensile layer and the minor strength direction layer had delamination, decreasing the modulus of rupture (MOR). The specimen with reinforced GFC between all layers improved bending performance but had delamination. The specimen reinforced with GFC in the outermost tensile component of the CLT improved bending performance but had tensile failure. However, CFRP has suppressed the tensile failure of CLT and improved bending performance by introducing consistent rolling shear failure. The reinforcement of CFRP on the outermost tensile section of CLT resulted in a 57\\% improvement in MOR compared to the control.",
          "keywords": [
            "Larix kaempferi carr",
            "cross-laminated timber (CLT)",
