        {
          "id": "web_of_science_1484",
          "title": "Integrating thermal bridge analysis with life-cycle assessment: energy and environmental performance of Ply-Lam CLT envelopes in ZEB-Level apartments",
          "authors": "Park, Ji Hun and Yuk, Hyeonseong and Kang, Seong Taek and Kim, Sumin",
          "affiliations": "Yonsei University",
          "countries": [],
          "journal": "BUILDING AND ENVIRONMENT",
          "year": 2026,
          "score": 28,
          "abstract": "Building envelopes play a decisive role in both the operational energy use and life-cycle carbon emissions, particularly when junction thermal bridges are considered. In Korean apartment housing, reinforced concrete (RC) construction with interior insulation remains dominant; however, it is structurally vulnerable to heat losses at wall-slab connections. To address this drawback, ply-lam cross-laminated timber (CLT) was evaluated as an alternative envelope system using a coupled framework integrating ISO 10,211-based thermal bridge modeling, whole-building simulations in DesignBuilder, and life-cycle assessment (LCA), following EN 15,804+A2. Substituting RC walls with ply-lam CLT reduced annual primary energy consumption by 5\\%-9\\%. Junction thermal bridges in RC resulted in additional losses of 125.1 W/K, whereas the CLT alternative achieved only 13.0 W/K, representing an 89.6\\% reduction. Roof and corner junctions in CLT satisfied the stringent Psi <= 0.05 W/m \\& sdot;K benchmark, whereas floor junctions approached this target. From a carbon perspective, CLT envelopes achieved an approximately 10\\% reduction in 30-year operational global warming potential (GWP) and further reductions in embodied GWP through lower manufacturing emissions and biogenic carbon storage, leading to a net decrease of approximately 59 kgCO2eq/m2 relative to RC. The results highlight the structural vulnerability of RC systems and the resilience of CLT alternatives when configured with continuous insulation and optimized details. By integrating junction-scale thermal bridge modeling with whole-building energy and life-cycle carbon analyses, this study provides comprehensive evidence for CLT as a competitive pathway to support low-carbon housing and national carbon neutrality goals.",
          "keywords": [
            "Thermal bridge",
            "Cross-laminated timber",
            "Zero-energy building",
            "Apartment",
            "Life-cycle assessment"
          ],
          "studyType": "Article",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.1016/j.buildenv.2025.114169",
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Web of Science",
          "numString": 3,
          "isDuplicate": false,
