        },
        {
          "id": "web_of_science_1510",
          "title": "Methods for practice-oriented linear analysis in seismic design of Cross Laminated Timber buildings",
          "authors": "Christovasilis, I. P. and Riparbelli, L. and Rinaldin, G. and Tamagnone, G.",
          "affiliations": "University of Trieste",
          "countries": [],
          "journal": "SOIL DYNAMICS AND EARTHQUAKE ENGINEERING",
          "year": 2020,
          "score": 13,
          "abstract": "In the context of innovative seismic-resistant structures, Cross Laminated Timber (CLT) construction holds an important position at a global level, supported by important full-scale tests and studies of the non-linear behaviour of these structures for severe events, demonstrating excellent performance. The present study aims to transfer current knowledge in the form of modelling approaches applicable to design engineers presenting tools and methodologies pertinent to the daily procedures of professional practice, thus relating to linear static and dynamic analyses. Two different approaches of structural modelling are presented, validated and compared; one that is based on a distributed representation of the connections between the structural panels and a second that is based on a discrete representation. Both models are validated by comparing the time-history responses with the full-scale shake-table test results of a 3-storey CLT structure under three earthquakes, showing in both cases a reliable estimate of the structural response. Finally, a parametric study for the damping coefficient of the structural response is presented and the typical value of 5\\% is evaluated. It is shown that values greater than 5\\% can be more applicable in order to compensate for the influence of the friction developed primarily between wall and floor panels on the global energy dissipation capacity of the structure.",
          "keywords": [
            "Cross laminated timber",
            "CLT buildings",
            "Seismic design",
            "Linear dynamic analysis",
            "Viscous damping"
          ],
          "studyType": "Article",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.1016/j.soildyn.2019.105869",
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Web of Science",
          "numString": 3,
          "isDuplicate": false,
          "duplicateOf": null,
          "dataProcessingStatus": "pending",
          "filter1Status": "pending",
          "filter2Status": "pending",
          "filter3Status": "pending",
          "exclusionReason": null,
          "inclusionCriterion": null,
          "exclusionCriterion": null,
          "extractionCriteria": null,
          "searchString": "(( \"cross laminated timber\" OR \"cross-laminated timber\" OR “x-lam” OR CLT) AND ( slab OR floor OR roof ))",
          "importDate": "2026-02-23T22:37:03.833Z",
          "lastModified": "2026-02-23T22:37:03.833Z"
        },
        {
          "id": "web_of_science_1511",
          "title": "Shear performance of timber-bamboo composite joints in hybrid cross-laminated timber (HCLT) ribbed floor system",
          "authors": "Yin, Tianxiao and Wang, Zhiqiang and Reynolds, Thomas P. S.",
          "affiliations": "Nanjing Forestry University; University of Edinburgh",
          "countries": [],
          "journal": "JOURNAL OF BUILDING ENGINEERING",
          "year": 2026,
          "score": 17,
          "abstract": "Conventional cross-laminated timber (CLT) may encounter difficulties when achieving spans of more than 6 m, resulting in a significant increase in cost. The development of new types of hybrid CLT (HCLT) and timber-bamboo composite can provide reliable potential solutions for this situation. In this paper, connections for a new timber-bamboo composite floor system, made of HCLT and bamboo scrimber were assessed. Different connection configurations were compared, including use of adhesive on interface, the insertion methods and parameters of fully threaded self-tapping screws (STS). Load-carrying capacity, slip modulus and ductility ratio of joints were evaluated through experimental push-out tests. The prediction models in Eurocode 5 and Canadian standard CSA-O86 were employed to predict the load-carrying capacity of joints for comparison with experimental values. The results showed that the load-carrying capacity and slip modulus of joints using one-component polyurethane (1C-PUR) was significantly higher than those using screws, but the ductility ratio was relatively lower. Joints with inclined screws showed higher load-carrying capacity and slip modulus than those with horizontal screws, while the ductility ratio of joints with horizontally inserted screws was higher. The current models showed underestimation of load-carrying capacity of joints connected by screws. The results of this study provide a reference for the subsequent modification of theoretical models and the future development of HCLT-bamboo composite floor systems.",
          "keywords": [
            "Hybrid cross-laminated timber",
            "Bamboo scrimber",
            "Self-tapping screws",
            "Mechanical properties",
            "Theoretical model"
          ],
          "studyType": "Article",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.1016/j.jobe.2025.114834",
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Web of Science",
          "numString": 3,
          "isDuplicate": false,
