        {
          "id": "web_of_science_1614",
          "title": "Development and experimental validation of deformable wall-to-floor connection for seismic resilient steel-timber hybrid structures",
          "authors": "Wang, Zhen and Li, Zheng and He, Minjuan",
          "affiliations": "Tongji University; Tongji University",
          "countries": [],
          "journal": "ENGINEERING STRUCTURES",
          "year": 2026,
          "score": 26,
          "abstract": "This study investigates a deformable wall-to-floor connection for seismic resilient steel-timber hybrid structures. Introducing deformable connections between the lateral load resisting system and the gravity load resisting system could mitigate structural seismic responses and associated damage. A novel wall-to-floor connection is proposed based on the functional requirements of such hybrid systems. Theoretical analyses are conducted on steel plate shear wall and timber frame hybrid structures incorporating deformable wall-to-floor connections, leading to the design of two 2/3 scale structure specimens. Each specimen comprises a steel plate shear wall, a cross-laminated timber floor slab, and a deformable wall-to-floor connection. The deformable connection integrated two distinct dampers: a slip-friction damper and a self-centering slip-friction damper. Prior to testing the structure specimens, cyclic experiments were performed on the dampers to validate their hysteretic behavior. Quasi-static test results of the structure specimens revealed that plastic deformation in both specimens concentrated in the infill steel plates and angle steels within the steel plate shear walls, while other components, including the dampers, remained undamaged. Minimal slippage was observed between the shear connector and cross-laminated timber slab, confirming the reliability of self-tapping screw and dowel pin connections. The postyield stiffness of the specimens was significantly influenced by the post-activation stiffness of the dampers, while the yield load depended on the damper activation load. The deformable wall-to-floor connections equipped the structure with multi-stage strengthening characteristics, enabling sequential lateral resistance and energy dissipation mechanisms to enhance capacity reserves against beyond-design-basis earthquakes. Furthermore, the deformable connections improved structural ductility. These findings validate the effectiveness of the proposed deformable wall-to-floor connection in balancing deformability, energy dissipation, and damage control for seismic resilient steel-timber hybrid structures.",
          "keywords": [
            "Hybrid structure",
            "Steel-timber",
            "Steel plate shear wall",
            "Cross-laminated timber",
            "Connection",
            "Experiment"
          ],
          "studyType": "Article",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.1016/j.engstruct.2025.121898",
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Web of Science",
