        {
          "id": "web_of_science_1624",
          "title": "Prestressed CLT-concrete composite panels with adhesive shear connection",
          "authors": "Bajzecerova, Viktoria and Kanocz, Jan and Rovnak, Marian and Kovac, Maros",
          "affiliations": "Technical University Kosice; Technical University Kosice",
          "countries": [],
          "journal": "JOURNAL OF BUILDING ENGINEERING",
          "year": 2022,
          "score": 18,
          "abstract": "The wet-on-wet gluing method seems to be a highly effective way to ensure a rigid shear connection between timber and concrete. However, in this case, the concrete shrinkage induces a relatively high deflection of timber-concrete composite elements. A new type of slender composite floor panel with a high load-bearing capacity was designed: prestressed cross-laminated timber (CLT)-concrete composite panels with an adhesive shear connection, made using the wet-on-wet method. The prestressing of composite panels was induced by releasing both ends of CLT slabs cambered by 3-point bending with a camber of about 1/100 of the panel length. Doing it this way eliminates the negative effect of the wet-on-wet gluing method, as was experimentally proven. Even one year after concreting, the middle-span deflection of the CLT-concrete composite panels was negative, with a residual camber of 5.5 mm. The theoretical model reflecting the phases of the manufacturing process, rheological phenomena and cracking of the concrete proved to be suitable for estimating the behaviour of prestressed CLT-concrete composite panels with adhesive shear connections. The contributions of influencing factors to the deformations and stresses of panels were quantified. The unfavourable effect of shrinkage has been confirmed regardless of the different anchoring times of panels, and the deflection only due to shrinkage exceeded 1/300 of the panel spans. By prestressing the panels, it was possible to achieve about a 1.2 times higher load-bearing capacity compared to the load capacities of non-prestressed panels.",
          "keywords": [
            "Timber-concrete composite element",
            "Prestressing",
            "Adhesive shear connection",
            "Cross-laminated timber panel",
            "Time-depended behaviour",
            "Creep",
            "Shrinkage"
          ],
          "studyType": "Article",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.1016/j.jobe.2022.104785",
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Web of Science",
