        {
          "id": "web_of_science_1670",
          "title": "A Review of Factors Influencing Rolling Shear in CLT and Test Methodology",
          "authors": "Kumar, Chandan and Li, Xin and Subhani, Mahbube and Shanks, Jon and Dakin, Tony and McGavin, Robert L. and Ashraf, Mahmud",
          "affiliations": "Queensland Department of Agriculture \\& Fisheries; Deakin University; University of Queensland",
          "countries": [],
          "journal": "JOURNAL OF TESTING AND EVALUATION",
          "year": 2022,
          "score": 21,
          "abstract": "Rolling shear (RS) is one of the key design parameters that influence the out-of-plane bending stiffness of cross-laminated timber (CLT) panels and thus affects the design and performance of CLT floor and wall systems. Various experimental methods are currently used to determine the RS capacity of CLT panels and feedstock. Some methods have been adapted to measure RS properties of clear wood such as Iosipescu, Arcan, and single cube apparatus, but there is no specific standard test method to accurately determine the RS stiffness or strength of CLT feedstock. This article presents a comprehensive review of potential RS test methods and highlights the importance of considering RS in CLT design. Test methods were thoroughly reviewed, and critically evaluated for their relative advantages and shortcomings. It was shown that sample preparation, testing procedure, and post-processing of results can significantly affect experimental outcomes, especially the RS modulus. It is recommended that in addition to current recommendations in CEN - EN 408, Timber Structures ??? Structural Timber and Glued Laminated Timber ??? Determination of Some Physical and Mechanical Properties, further specifications should be outlined in regards to plate material and type, glue type, and, more importantly, the relative location of reference points used for deformation measurement to ensure consistent and reliable comparison of RS characteristics reported by researchers. An equation was derived, and is presented, that ensures the vertical load passes through the centroid of the inclined specimen to avoid eccentricity in the specimen and moment transfer at the base of the plates. The effect of various factors on RS properties have been reviewed.",
          "keywords": [
            "rolling shear cross-laminated timber (CLT) factors failure shear analogy model test method"
          ],
          "studyType": "Review",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.1520/JTE20210160",
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Web of Science",
          "numString": 3,
          "isDuplicate": false,
          "duplicateOf": null,
          "dataProcessingStatus": "pending",
          "filter1Status": "pending",
          "filter2Status": "pending",
          "filter3Status": "pending",
          "exclusionReason": null,
          "inclusionCriterion": null,
          "exclusionCriterion": null,
          "extractionCriteria": null,
          "searchString": "(( \"cross laminated timber\" OR \"cross-laminated timber\" OR “x-lam” OR CLT) AND ( slab OR floor OR roof ))",
          "importDate": "2026-02-23T22:37:03.855Z",
          "lastModified": "2026-02-23T22:37:03.855Z"
        },
        {
          "id": "web_of_science_1671",
          "title": "Comparative study of connection performance for cross-laminated timber-glulam composite floor systems",
          "authors": "Zhang, Xiaoyue and Zhang, Sigong and Niu, Binjie and Yang, Huifeng",
          "affiliations": "Chongqing University; Newcastle University - UK; Nanjing Tech University",
          "countries": [],
          "journal": "STRUCTURES",
          "year": 2025,
          "score": 15,
          "abstract": "Long-span timber floor systems can be effectively achieved by integrating mass timber panels with linear timber elements to form composite action. Their flexural performance is largely governed by the mechanical behaviour of the connectors. This study investigated the structural performance of various connection systems used in crosslaminated timber (CLT)-glulam composite floors. A series of push-out tests were conducted on seven groups of specimens, each featuring different shear connector types, including inclined and vertical self-tapping screws, steel tubes, and perforated steel plates. Key performance parameters-shear strength, initial stiffness, and ductility-were experimentally evaluated and compared across all configurations. In addition, the test results were benchmarked against analytical predictions to assess the accuracy and applicability of simplified design models. Among the systems tested, the combination of steel tubes and screws significantly improved both stiffness and load-carrying capacity, while also demonstrating favourable post-peak behaviour (i.e., enhanced ductility). This hybrid connector system offers a cost-effective solution for composite floor systems. Furthermore, it was found that the ductility factors defined in EN 12512 do not adequately capture the ductile behaviour of different connection systems. In contrast, the use of absolute ductility-calculated as the difference between the ultimate slip and the slip at yield-provided a more accurate representation of system behaviour.",
          "keywords": [
            "Cross-laminated timber",
            "Glulam",
            "Self-tapping screws",
            "Push-out tests",
            "Ductility factors"
          ],
          "studyType": "Article",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.1016/j.istruc.2025.110083",
          "language": "English",
