        {
          "id": "web_of_science_1864",
          "title": "Experimental and theoretical study on mechanical behavior of high strength bolt connections for prefabricated steel-CLBT composite floors",
          "authors": "Gong, Shuhong and Hu, Xiamin and Yu, Runjun and Li, Jiang and Zhang, Jing and Li, Tao and Hong, Wan and Nie, Jianguo",
          "affiliations": "Nanjing Tech University; Nanjing University of Aeronautics \\& Astronautics; Sanjiang University; Sanjiang University",
          "countries": [],
          "journal": "STRUCTURES",
          "year": 2025,
          "score": 4,
          "abstract": "Bamboo scrimber is utilized to develop cross-laminated bamboo and timber (CLBT), providing higher strength than traditional cross-laminated timber (CLT) and enabling rapid, large-scale production due to bamboo's fast growth. In this study, CLBT and magnesium phosphate cement (MPC) mortar are incorporated into prefabricated steel-CLBT composite (SBTC) floors. MPC mortar exhibits high early strength, rapid setting, and self-compaction, making it an ideal connecting material. High strength bolts were employed to form connection for SBTC floors. A comprehensive push-out test program involving 27 specimens across 9 groups was conducted to study the influences of variates including bolt diameter and strength, grouting diameter, mortar strength, CLBT panel thickness, and fiber orientation on failure modes, bearing capacity, shear stiffness, ductility, and load-slip curves. Three dominated failure modes were identified: bolt fracture, single hinge, and mortar compression failure. Results show that increasing bolt diameter and strength significantly enhances bearing capacity. Bolts with diameters of 18 mm and 20 mm provide approximately 24 \\% and 46 \\% higher bearing capacities compared to 16 mm bolts. The bearing capacity increases by approximately 40 \\% when using grade 12.9 bolts compared to grade 8.8 bolts. Higher mortar strength also improves performance. Increasing compressive strength from 80 MPa to 100 MPa results in about a 10 \\% increase in capacity and a 13 \\% improvement in initial stiffness. A smaller grouting diameter slightly increases capacity but reduces stiffness. Reducing the grouting diameter from 60 mm to 40 mm increases the bearing capacity by approximately 16 \\%. Thicker CLBT panels and perpendicular fiber orientation enhance both capacity and ductility. Moreover, comprehensive models are developed to calculate bearing capacity and load-slip behavior, demonstrating greater accuracy than existing models and providing a basis for future research.",
          "keywords": [
            "Composite floors",
            "Bolt connections",
            "Bearing capacity",
            "Load-slip curves",
            "Load-strain curves",
            "Cross laminated bamboo and timber (CLBT)",
            "Magnesium phosphate cement (MPC) mortar"
          ],
          "studyType": "Article",
          "quality": null,
          "doi": "10.1016/j.istruc.2025.110477",
          "language": "English",
          "source": "Web of Science",
