        {
          "id": "scopus_2025",
          "title": "SEISMIC PERFORMANCE OF A TIMBER HOUSE USING CLT HORIZONTAL DIAPHRAGM",
          "authors": "Furuta, Tomoki and Nakao, Masato",
          "affiliations": "Nishinippon Institute of Technology, Miyako, Fukuoka, Japan; Yokohama National University, Yokohama, Kanagawa, Japan",
          "countries": [
            "Japan"
          ],
          "journal": "World Conference on Earthquake Engineering proceedings",
          "year": 2024,
          "score": 25,
          "abstract": "Authors have developed a new post-and-beam construction system with a CLT horizontal diaphragm. In the developed construction system, CLTs are used as a horizontal diaphragm, whereas beams are omitted. It may realize a short construction period, high structural performance, and high sound insulation. This paper presents the seismic performance evaluation of the newly developed construction system through the static incremental analysis of a prototype timber house. Two storied prototype house with a new construction system has CLT as a floor on the second story. It was tolerated that the external wall of the second story was not arranged just above the one on the first story. The CLT grade was Mx60-5-5, and the species was Japanese cedar. Braces were used as the shear wall in the prototype house, and the ends of the braces were connected by newly developed ductile brace connectors. A half-lapped joint was adopted as the joint between CLTs of the floor on the second story. Screws, 7 mm in diameter and 200 mm long, were driven at an angle of 45 degrees on the joint for reinforcement. The weight of the first story of the prototype house was estimated as 191.1 kN, while the one of the second story was 120.7 kN. To evaluate the seismic performance of the prototype house, a 3D structural model for static incremental analysis was built. The CLT in the model was composed of lamina elements to evaluate two-way bending characteristics. Therefore, the 3D structural model of the prototype house contained 8944 nodes and 16747 members. Every connection between the members, such as columns, CLTs, and braces, was represented by a non-linear spring. The force-displacement relationships of the springs were determined by element experiments. From the non-linear incremental analysis result of the 3D structural model, it was found that story drift angle was approximately 0.01 under C0=0.3, where C0 is the value of the story shear force divided by the weight the story carries. The maximum bending stress of all CLT laminas was 77% of the lower limit strength. For the maximum tensile stress, it was 21% of the lower limit strength. Therefore, it was confirmed that the prototype house by the new construction system has sufficient seismic performance, and no damage to the CLT was expected even under relatively large earthquakes. © 2024, International Association for Earthquake Engineering. All rights reserved.",
          "keywords": [
            "Architectural acoustics",
            "Concretes",
            "Dynamic response",
            "Earthquake effects",
            "Lapping",
            "Mortar",
            "Seismic design",
            "Seismic response",
